6,32,68,101,118,32,109,111,100,101,10,54,54,57,58,35,35,32,49,53,46,32,84,111,117,99,104,45,111,110,108,121,32,100,101,118,105,99,101,115,10,54,55,55,58,35,35,32,49,54,46,32,65,114,99,104,105,116,101,99,116,117,114,101,32,97,110,100,32,98,117,105,108,100,10,55,51,55,58,35,35,32,49,55,46,32,84,101,115,116,105,110,103,10,56,49,52,58,35,35,32,49,56,46,32,66,117,105,108,100,32,111,114,100,101,114,32,97,110,100,32,99,117,116,32,111,114,100,101,114,10,56,51,51,58,35,35,32,49,57,46,32,68,111,110,101,32,97,110,100,32,114,101,112,111,114,116,105,110,103,10,56,55,57,58,35,35,32,83,111,117,114,99,101,32,73,110,102,111,114,109,97,116,105,111,110,10,56,56,49,58,35,35,32,84,101,115,116,105,110,103,32,70,101,101,100,98,97,99,107,10,56,56,51,58,35,35,32,69,110,103,105,110,101,32,70,101,101,100,98,97,99,107,10,56,56,53,58,35,35,32,80,114,111,109,112,116,32,70,101,101,100,98,97,99,107,10,57,52,53,58,35,35,32,50,48,46,32,87,104,97,116,32,119,97,115,32,97,110,100,32,119,97,115,110,39,116,32,118,101,114,105,102,105,101,100,10,57,54,49,58,35,35,32,50,49,46,32,84,65,83,75,46,109,100,32,99,111,118,101,114,97,103,101,32,97,110,100,32,112,114,111,111,102,10,49,48,50,54,58,35,35,32,50,50,46,32,80,108,97,110,110,105,110,103,45,115,116,97,103,101,32,114,101,112,111,114,116,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":26,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":11,\"content\":\"## 1. The game\"},{\"line_number\":50,\"content\":\"## 2. Originality rules\"},{\"line_number\":61,\"content\":\"## 3. Controls\"},{\"line_number\":98,\"content\":\"## 4. The balance model\"},{\"line_number\":186,\"content\":\"## 5. Programme and progress\"},{\"line_number\":228,\"content\":\"## 6. Events\"},{\"line_number\":361,\"content\":\"## 7. Screens\"},{\"line_number\":389,\"content\":\"## 8. Cast and writing\"},{\"line_number\":425,\"content\":\"## 9. Art bible\"},{\"line_number\":523,\"content\":\"## 10. Animation\"},{\"line_number\":538,\"content\":\"## 11. Audio\"},{\"line_number\":584,\"content\":\"## 12. Multiplayer\"},{\"line_number\":644,\"content\":\"## 13. Attract mode\"},{\"line_number\":652,\"content\":\"## 14. Dev mode\"},{\"line_number\":669,\"content\":\"## 15. Touch-only devices\"},{\"line_number\":677,\"content\":\"## 16. Architecture and build\"},{\"line_number\":737,\"content\":\"## 17. Testing\"},{\"line_number\":814,\"content\":\"## 18. Build order and cut order\"},{\"line_number\":833,\"content\":\"## 19. Done and reporting\"},{\"line_number\":879,\"content\":\"## Source Information\"},{\"line_number\":881,\"content\":\"## Testing Feedback\"},{\"line_number\":883,\"content\":\"## Engine Feedback\"},{\"line_number\":885,\"content\":\"## Prompt Feedback\"},{\"line_number\":945,\"content\":\"## 20. What was and wasn't verified\"},{\"line_number\":961,\"content\":\"## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\"},{\"line_number\":1026,\"content\":\"## 22. Planning-stage report\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e59109ff-b2d0-4e5e-aa24-f35c9a027a50-8","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"400→- \\\"You fell very consistently. Consistency is important.\\\"\\n- \\\"It's been three days. The island tilted a bit. I'm not saying those things are connected. They are connected.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your Wobble Age is 61. That's down from 74! If this continues, you will eventually be born.\\\"\\n- \\\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\\\"\\n- *Personal questions, always Yes/No, always a reply either way.* \\\"Do you trust your knees?\\\" Yes: \\\"They speak highly of you too.\\\" No: \\\"They know.\\\" · \\\"Do you ever stand on one leg without knowing why?\\\" Yes: \\\"I thought so.\\\" No: \\\"That's what someone on one leg would say.\\\" · \\\"Have you been leaning on things? Be honest. I can tell.\\\" · \\\"When you walk into a room, which way do you lean first?\\\" Left / Right: \\\"Interesting. I'm writing that down.\\\"\\n\\n**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\\n\\n- \\\"Breathe in. Breathe out. Not like that, but thank you for trying.\\\"\\n- \\\"This pose is called the Apologetic Pine. Feel your weight. Feel it apologise.\\\"\\n410→- \\\"If you are shaking, that is only your body leaving.\\\"\\n- \\\"A heron can stand like this for hours. A heron has also never been asked why.\\\"\\n- \\\"You got off the mat. I saw. It's all right. I see everything. It's all right.\\\"\\n\\n**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\\n\\n- \\\"YOU'RE DOING IT. I don't know what it is, but you're doing it.\\\"\\n- \\\"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one. I've lied to you and I'd do it again.\\\"\\n- \\\"I believe in you as much as I believe in lunch.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your form is a form! That's what form is!\\\"\\n420→\\n**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \\\"He's part of it now.\\\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Art bible\\n\\nThe look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\\n\\n**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\\n430→\\n**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\\n\\n**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\\n\\n| Time | skyTop | skyMid | skyLow | sea | seaHi | sun, intensity | hemi sky, ground, intensity |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| dawn ✔ | 8FB4E0 | F6CFD8 | FFE3B5 | 3E8FB5 | A9E3E4 | FFE6D8, 2.6 | FCE8F0, 7C8BB5, 1.35 |\\n| noon ✔ | 6FC3E8 | BFE9F0 | FFF2D0 | 16A8B0 | 8CF0DD | FFFBE8, 3.2 | FFFFFF, A09080, 1.35 |\\n| golden ✔ | 7FB7C9 | F7D9A6 | F59E6C | 1F9EA3 | 71DCCB | FFF0D0, 3.1 | FFF4E0, 8A7FA0, 1.25 |\\n440→| dusk ✔ | 2B2F6B | A8558F | F58A5C | 24406F | C26A8C | FFB98A, 2.4 | B48AD0, 2B2F5F, 1.1 |\\n\\nLerp palette uniforms and lights over 3 s between steps.\\n\\n**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\\n\\n**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\\n\\n**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\\n\\n450→```js\\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { mrt, output, normalView, toonOutlinePass, color, float, vec2, vec4, uv, screenSize, Fn, abs, max,\\n  smoothstep, mix, uniform, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, time, length, hash, floor, oneMinus } from 'three/tsl';\\n\\nexport function makeInk(renderer, scene, camera) {\\n  const INK = 0x1a1420;\\n  const u = { hull: uniform(0.0028), width: uniform(1.4), boil: uniform(0.5), fadeNear: uniform(30), fadeFar: uniform(90),\\n              grain: uniform(0.02), near: uniform(camera.near), far: uniform(camera.far), ink: uniform(new THREE.Color(INK)) };\\n  const scenePass = toonOutlinePass(scene, camera, color(INK), u.hull, float(1));\\n460→  scenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, normal: normalView }));\\n  const colorTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), normalTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('normal'),\\n        depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\\n  const post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\\n  post.outputNode = Fn(() => {\\n    const cell = floor(uv().mul(vec2(40, 23)));\\n    const wob = hash(floor(time.mul(8)).add(cell.x).add(cell.y.mul(17))).sub(0.5).mul(u.boil);   // hand-drawn line boil\\n    const px = u.width.add(wob).div(screenSize);\\n    const viewZ = (o) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(uv().add(o)).r, u.near, u.far);\\n    const n = (o) => normalTex.sample(uv().add(o)).xyz;\\n470→    const dx = vec2(px.x, 0), dy = vec2(0, px.y);\\n    const z0 = viewZ(vec2(0, 0)).abs();\\n    const dz = max(abs(viewZ(dx).sub(viewZ(dx.negate()))), abs(viewZ(dy).sub(viewZ(dy.negate())))).div(z0.max(0.5));\\n    const dn = max(length(n(dx).sub(n(dx.negate()))), length(n(dy).sub(n(dy.negate()))));\\n    const e = max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.12, dz), smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, dn)).mul(oneMinus(smoothstep(u.fadeNear, u.fadeFar, z0)));\\n    const c = mix(colorTex.rgb, u.ink, e);\\n    const p = floor(uv().mul(screenSize));\\n    const g = hash(p.x.add(p.y.mul(1571))).sub(0.5).mul(u.grain);\\n    return vec4(c.add(g).mul(oneMinus(length(uv().sub(0.5)).mul(0.62).pow(2.2))), 1);\\n  })();\\n480→  return { post, u };\\n}\\n// Render with post.render(). Remember: camera far changes need u.far updated.\\n```\\nGotchas ✔:\\n- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical streaks over the whole image.\\n- `fadeNear/fadeFar` removes depth-line noise at the horizon, which otherwise draws a thick black band. Make sure the sea disc is big (radius 590) and blends to `skyLow` with distance.\\n- Do not merge vertices on hard-edged props: smooth normals smear the toon shading. Plain `BoxGeometry` looks right, and the depth lines cover the hull's corners.\\n- `InstancedMesh` with `setColorAt` works with toon and the hull ✔. Use it for marbles, splash blobs, fruit crowds.\\n- Glass ✔: **do not use a toon material on glass.** The hull pass draws solid ink back faces behind it and the glass turns smoky grey. Use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ color: 0xe8fbff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })`, and draw its outline with thin toon torus rings at the base, shoulder, neck and lip, plus a couple of narrow white highlight strips. It looks like a clean inked glass jar.\\n490→- No TSL compute anywhere. It breaks the WebGL2 fallback. All particles are CPU-driven instances.\\n\\n**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\\n\\n**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\\n\\n**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\\n\\n- **Peg doll, 1.5 m.** Feet: white flattened spheres r .1 scaled (1, .55, 1.5), at x ±.13. Legs: skin capsules r .08, length .3. Hips at y .46. Shorts: capsule r .2 (scaled 1.05, 1, .85). Shirt: capsule r .21, length .24 (z scale .82). Head: sphere r .23, centre .82 above hips. Headband: torus r .228. Eyes: ink spheres r .024 at (±.08, .85, .205) from hips. Cheeks: pink flattened spheres. Arms: visible darker wooden ball joints r .075 at shoulders (±.26, .5) and r .06 at elbows, skin capsule segments, white mitten spheres r .07. Skin `#F2D3A8`, joints `#D9A86C`, shorts `#2B4A6F`. Vary with paint, hats (visor, beret, bucket hat, perm of spheres, wizard hat, tiny crown), and small height and width changes for guests.\\n- **Trudy.** Rounded box (1.1, .42, .26), radius .1, `#F5B700`, on two stubby brown pegs. Dark window (.56, .2, .05) on the face holding a lime `#B9F26B` capsule vial r .075, length .36, lying on its side. Bubble: white flattened sphere with an ink pupil; it slides in the vial. Two ink marks on the vial like a level's centre lines. Round lime end vials at x ±.42 as cheeks. Ink torus arc for a mouth. She hops with squash and stretch. This design is very readable: keep it.\\n500→- **Ms. Stillwater.** One long orange leg (cylinder r .025, height 1.05), the other tucked. Body sphere r .34 scaled (.8, .92, 1.35), `#A9C2D4`. Wings: flattened ellipsoids `#7E9CB4`. S-curve neck: `TubeGeometry` of a Catmull-Rom curve, r .062, `#D6E3EC`, rising to y 2.2. Head sphere r .115. Beak: yellow cone. Ink crest cone. Half-closed eyes as ink torus arcs. A small `#C24D6A` scarf at the base of the neck. For animation, build the neck from 5 joint segments.\\n- **Coach Bolster.** Capsule r .72, length .5, scaled (1.06, 1, .9), `#B9806A`. Red sweatband torus at the top. Two pale muzzle spheres, cream cone tusks, tiny ink eyes, flattened flippers `#8D5F4F`, a white lanyard torus and a yellow whistle.\\n- **Gary the gull.** White ellipsoid body, grey flattened wings, yellow cone beak, orange peg legs, ink dot eyes and one permanently angry brow.\\n- **The jar ✔.** `LatheGeometry` through (.01,0) (1,.02) (1.08,.12) (1.08,1.9) (.86,2.2) (.86,2.45) (.95,2.5) with the glass recipe from the gotchas above; rings at (r 1.08, y .12), (1.08, 1.9), (.86, 2.2), (.95, 2.5). Marbles: `InstancedMesh` of toon spheres r .14 with `setColorAt`. Physics: a base cylinder and a ring of 16 cuboid walls.\\n- **Island.** Sand disc r 22 over a grass disc r 15 (flush with the sand, or dolls sink into it: all walking surfaces share y = 0), big inverted rock cone below on its one pivot rock. Toy trees: peg trunk plus ball top. Lighthouse: stacked red and cream rings with a lamp and a blue cap. Pavilions: toy-block posts and rounded slab roofs in teal and orange. Sun Deck, Bandstand, Circus Green, Yard, Pier, Jar, Mirror pavilion, Notice Board, Gazebo, Bed Hut at fixed spots around the rim and centre. Every set in section 6 appears here as a landmark, and the class itself runs in its own world (section 16).\\n- **Island map** (island-local x, z; you arrive at 0, 10; the ring road for Island Loop runs at r 19):\\n\\n| Landmark | x, z | What's there | Landmark | x, z | What's there |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Lighthouse | 2, −9 | The Grand Hold | Bandstand | 15, −3 | Step Class |\\n510→| Jar | −3, −4 | Marble pours | Boardwalk gate | 19, 3 | Room Service |\\n| Circus Green | 0, 2 | Plate Spinners | Pier | 15, 12, running out to sea | Fruit Hat, Plank & Perch, Springboard tower at its end |\\n| Sun Deck | −16, −3 | Poses | Beach | 5, 17 | Tug of Peace |\\n| Lily Pond | −13, 7 | Still Water | Notice Board | 1, 9 | Today's Plan, Journal |\\n| Quarry Terrace | −9, −13 | The Cairn | Bed Hut | 8, 8 | End of the day |\\n| The Yard | −7, 14 | Log Reps | Mirror pavilion | 9, −14 | Paint and hats |\\n| Gazebo | 14, −11 | Group Class, Tippy Raft's dock | | | |\\n\\n  Guests wander between these spots on simple paths in island-local coordinates. They need no physics.\\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words: CLACK!, SPLOSH!, WOBBLE. Canvas-texture sprites that pop and fade. Everyone squashes on landing. A small hit-stop on catches and big events. Gentle camera shake, off with reduce-shake.\\n520→\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Animation\\n\\nUse Three's `AnimationMixer` and `AnimationClip` as the base (TASK.md requires the engine's system). Rig each character as a hierarchy of named `THREE.Group` joints with the rigid parts parented to them: `root, hips, spine, head, shoulderL/R, elbowL/R, hipL/R, kneeL/R, ankleL/R` for dolls. No skinned meshes are needed for jointed toys. `poseClip(name, duration, keyframes)` in `src/anim/clips.js` compiles `{ t, pose: { shoulderL: [x, y, z in degrees], ... } }` into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s on `'shoulderL.quaternion'`. `AnimationMixer` plays them with `crossFadeTo`.\\n\\n- **Doll clips:** idle, breathe, walk, jog, hop, land, cheer, clap, shrug, bow, flail, sleep, one per pose (8), squat (scrubbed: `action.paused = true`, `action.time = depth × duration`), plank, sit cross-legged, lean-back tug, hip-circle (scrubbed by hoop phase), step-L, step-R, carry-tray, hold-slab, tuck, pike, dive-ready.\\n- **Trudy:** hop, idle wobble, gasp, sulk, faint, read clipboard; plus procedural squash and bubble.\\n- **Heron:** stand, slow demo versions of each pose, neck bow, head turn, pat. **Walrus:** bounce, clap, huge inhale, salute, catch log. **Gull:** fly, land, peck, squawk.\\n530→- **Layering order each frame:** `mixer.update(dt)`, then apply procedural lean from the balance core at `root`, head bobble, breath on spine. Then, while ragdolled, parts follow bodies instead.\\n\\n**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\\n\\nGhost lanes do not ragdoll: they play a flop clip.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Audio\\n\\n540→Everything is synthesised with Web Audio. No files. All instruments take a `ctx` argument, so the same code runs on an `AudioContext` and on an `OfflineAudioContext` for tests.\\n\\n**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\\n\\n**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\\n\\n**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\\n\\n**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\\n\\n550→| Track | Where | Feel | Chords per bar |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Level Best | Title, menus, ending | 92 BPM light swing; e-piano, vibes lead, soft bass, brushes | D♭maj9 B♭m9 E♭m9 A♭13, twice; G♭maj9 Fm7 E♭m9 A♭13sus |\\n| Island | Hub | 84 BPM bossa; marimba, upright-ish bass, shaker | Fmaj7 Am7 B♭maj7 C9, Fmaj7 Dm9 Gm9 C13 |\\n| Stillness | Poses, Cairn, Still Water | 60 BPM, free; pad, bells on the breath cycle, flute | Dmaj9 Emaj9/D, slowly, Lydian |\\n| Strength | Log Reps, Plank, Tug | 100 BPM funk; slap bass, clav-like e-piano, brass stabs, gated snare | Em9 A13 |\\n| Cardio | Step Class, Plates, Loop | Bpm from the chart; four-on-the-floor, octave slap bass, e-piano, brass, gated snare | D♭maj7 Cm7 B♭m7 E♭sus |\\n| Balance | Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Raft | 118 BPM bouncy; marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, tuba-like bass | C6 Am7 Dm7 G7 |\\n| Level | The Grand Hold | Starts as bare e-piano of the title theme, then every instrument, slower, in tune | As Level Best |\\n| Stings | Results, reveal, unlock | Three seconds or less, one per medal, drum roll and reveal, little fanfare | |\\n560→\\n**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\\n```\\nBar 1 D♭maj9: [0,77,3] [3,75,1] [4,73,2] [6,68,4] [12,70,2] [14,72,2]\\nBar 2 B♭m9:   [0,73,3] [3,72,1] [4,70,2] [6,65,6] [14,68,2]\\nBar 3 E♭m9:   [0,70,3] [3,68,1] [4,66,2] [6,63,4] [12,65,2] [14,66,2]\\nBar 4 A♭13:   [0,68,3] [3,70,1] [4,72,2] [6,75,6]\\n```\\n**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\\n\\n570→**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\\n\\n| Voice | Base pitch | Rate | Character |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Trudy | 420 Hz | 16 syll/s | squeaky, nervous, high formants |\\n| Ms. Stillwater | 170 Hz | 7 syll/s | breathy, adds noise, long gaps |\\n| Coach Bolster | 95 Hz | 11 syll/s | big, saw, loud, bursts |\\n| Guests | 200-320 Hz | 12 syll/s | varied per guest |\\n| Gary | | | FM squawk |\\n\\n580→**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Multiplayer\\n\\n**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\\n\\n### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\\n\\n590→From the main menu or the Gazebo. **Lobby:** four mats. Each pad's Cross joins it, Enter or a click joins keyboard and mouse as one player, Circle or Esc leaves. Seats use the hub's colours. The first to join drives menus. **Playlist:** Quick Class (three random) or pick three. All events with `couch: true`: Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, Room Service. **Points:** 4, 3, 2, 1 per event. **Final podium:** a toy-block podium, winners on top, and it tips over.\\n\\nAll lanes use one camera that frames everyone; each event's camera has a wide setting for this. Room Service uses four parallel boardwalks with the camera following the group, and the leader's finish starts a 15 s clock for the rest. Each lane reads its own pad by gamepad index, and prompts on that player's disc follow that device.\\n\\n### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\\n\\nThe contract is `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Read it. I ran a minimal WebGPU ghost game built this way through the real harness and it passed all 16 checks at 1, 2 and 4 players, on the bad network, and with the host leaving ✔.\\n\\n**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\\n\\n600→**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\\n```html\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>…vendor/session.js, verbatim, nothing before it…</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay balanced through the class: highest score wins\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\\\">\\n610→<meta name=\\\"hub:type\\\" content=\\\"Game\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:source\\\" content=\\\"Wii Fit\\\">\\n```\\nDo not set `hub:webgpu required`: the WebGL2 fallback works ✔, so the hub can deal this game to everyone. Fetch the client from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` into `vendor/session.js`. **The hub checks it by SHA-256** from its banner to its last line, with LF line endings: 1.4.0 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` ✔. Make the build fail if it doesn't match. If the hub has released a newer client, take its hash from `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` in that repo.\\n\\n**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\\n```js\\nasync function main() {\\n  if (AIHub.active) {\\n620→    const pConnect = AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });   // hello must go out within 10 s\\n    await bootEngine();                       // renderer.init(), RAPIER.init(), build the world\\n    const info = await pConnect;\\n    if (info) runSession(info); else runStandalone();     // null: no hub after all\\n  } else { await bootEngine(); runStandalone(); }\\n}\\nmain().catch(showBootFailure);                // shows the message, calls AIHub.fail(message)\\n```\\n\\n**In `runSession(info)`:**\\n630→1. No title, menus or attract. Build the class stage, line up the dolls, then `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press and without waiting for every shader. Register `AIHub.on` handlers before `ready()`.\\n2. **Your own card** (`card: true`). The class lined up on the deck, Trudy with a clipboard, the music. Overlay: the game's name, each player's name in their colour with a tick when ready (from `card.ready`), how to win, this player's controls with glyphs, time left from `card.left` (show \\\"Starting...\\\" when `card.starting`, \\\"Paused\\\" when `card.paused`). Enter or Cross or a tap: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle or M (name the key on the card) or a tap: `AIHub.answer('more')`. The harness presses Enter by dispatching a keydown on the page, so listen on `window`. Nothing on the card changes the round, and it never touches `Math.random`.\\n3. **The round runs on `AIHub.time()` alone,** so everyone is in the same class at the same moment. Count down from 3 while the time is negative. Three slots picked with `AIHub.rng('deal')` from the session set (Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard), no repeats: each is 6 s intro, 50 s play (`sessionConfig` makes each event fit exactly), 6 s score. Total 186 s, within the 270 s limit that 3 minutes gives ✔. If time jumps (hidden tab), go to the slot the clock says.\\n4. **Ghosts.** From the moment `AIHub.time()` reaches 0, including during intros, about 10 times a second, `AIHub.send({ t:'g', a:slotIndex, x, z, lx, lz, c:clipIndex, f:fallen?1:0, s:score }, { reliable:false })`. Keep it well under 1,200 bytes. Show a ghost only when its `a` equals your slot. Interpolate over about 150 ms. Ghosts are visual only: name tag (set with `textContent`, never HTML), seat colour on the shirt, a flop clip for `f`. They never touch your physics. Remove on `leave`.\\n5. **Scoring.** Each slot gives `round(form × 10)`. Call `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'Fruit Hat 420' })` every second **from the moment time reaches 0, with score 0 during the first intro.** The harness checks for ghost traffic and progress about 4 seconds into play, well before the first class starts, and fails the game if there is none. `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once at the end, for example stats `{ 'Floor Exercises': 2, 'Best medal': 'Pleasant', 'Wobble Age': 41 }`, and an award from a small list (\\\"Suspiciously Still\\\"). Then a calm \\\"waiting for the others\\\" with Trudy.\\n6. `time-up`: send `result` at once if not already sent (grace is 5 s ✔). `end`: stop input, sending and all sound, and keep the last frame; the hub draws its results over the game.\\n7. Options opens a small overlay with volumes only, and it doesn't pause. No restart, no quit, no Dev Mode.\\n\\n**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\\n\\n640→**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"400→- \\\"You fell very consistently. Consistency is important.\\\"\\n- \\\"It's been three days. The island tilted a bit. I'm not saying those things are connected. They are connected.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your Wobble Age is 61. That's down from 74! If this continues, you will eventually be born.\\\"\\n- \\\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\\\"\\n- *Personal questions, always Yes/No, always a reply either way.* \\\"Do you trust your knees?\\\" Yes: \\\"They speak highly of you too.\\\" No: \\\"They know.\\\" · \\\"Do you ever stand on one leg without knowing why?\\\" Yes: \\\"I thought so.\\\" No: \\\"That's what someone on one leg would say.\\\" · \\\"Have you been leaning on things? Be honest. I can tell.\\\" · \\\"When you walk into a room, which way do you lean first?\\\" Left / Right: \\\"Interesting. I'm writing that down.\\\"\\n\\n**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\\n\\n- \\\"Breathe in. Breathe out. Not like that, but thank you for trying.\\\"\\n- \\\"This pose is called the Apologetic Pine. Feel your weight. Feel it apologise.\\\"\\n410→- \\\"If you are shaking, that is only your body leaving.\\\"\\n- \\\"A heron can stand like this for hours. A heron has also never been asked why.\\\"\\n- \\\"You got off the mat. I saw. It's all right. I see everything. It's all right.\\\"\\n\\n**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\\n\\n- \\\"YOU'RE DOING IT. I don't know what it is, but you're doing it.\\\"\\n- \\\"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one. I've lied to you and I'd do it again.\\\"\\n- \\\"I believe in you as much as I believe in lunch.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your form is a form! That's what form is!\\\"\\n420→\\n**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \\\"He's part of it now.\\\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Art bible\\n\\nThe look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\\n\\n**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\\n430→\\n**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\\n\\n**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\\n\\n| Time | skyTop | skyMid | skyLow | sea | seaHi | sun, intensity | hemi sky, ground, intensity |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| dawn ✔ | 8FB4E0 | F6CFD8 | FFE3B5 | 3E8FB5 | A9E3E4 | FFE6D8, 2.6 | FCE8F0, 7C8BB5, 1.35 |\\n| noon ✔ | 6FC3E8 | BFE9F0 | FFF2D0 | 16A8B0 | 8CF0DD | FFFBE8, 3.2 | FFFFFF, A09080, 1.35 |\\n| golden ✔ | 7FB7C9 | F7D9A6 | F59E6C | 1F9EA3 | 71DCCB | FFF0D0, 3.1 | FFF4E0, 8A7FA0, 1.25 |\\n440→| dusk ✔ | 2B2F6B | A8558F | F58A5C | 24406F | C26A8C | FFB98A, 2.4 | B48AD0, 2B2F5F, 1.1 |\\n\\nLerp palette uniforms and lights over 3 s between steps.\\n\\n**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\\n\\n**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\\n\\n**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\\n\\n450→```js\\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { mrt, output, normalView, toonOutlinePass, color, float, vec2, vec4, uv, screenSize, Fn, abs, max,\\n  smoothstep, mix, uniform, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, time, length, hash, floor, oneMinus } from 'three/tsl';\\n\\nexport function makeInk(renderer, scene, camera) {\\n  const INK = 0x1a1420;\\n  const u = { hull: uniform(0.0028), width: uniform(1.4), boil: uniform(0.5), fadeNear: uniform(30), fadeFar: uniform(90),\\n              grain: uniform(0.02), near: uniform(camera.near), far: uniform(camera.far), ink: uniform(new THREE.Color(INK)) };\\n  const scenePass = toonOutlinePass(scene, camera, color(INK), u.hull, float(1));\\n460→  scenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, normal: normalView }));\\n  const colorTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), normalTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('normal'),\\n        depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\\n  const post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\\n  post.outputNode = Fn(() => {\\n    const cell = floor(uv().mul(vec2(40, 23)));\\n    const wob = hash(floor(time.mul(8)).add(cell.x).add(cell.y.mul(17))).sub(0.5).mul(u.boil);   // hand-drawn line boil\\n    const px = u.width.add(wob).div(screenSize);\\n    const viewZ = (o) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(uv().add(o)).r, u.near, u.far);\\n    const n = (o) => normalTex.sample(uv().add(o)).xyz;\\n470→    const dx = vec2(px.x, 0), dy = vec2(0, px.y);\\n    const z0 = viewZ(vec2(0, 0)).abs();\\n    const dz = max(abs(viewZ(dx).sub(viewZ(dx.negate()))), abs(viewZ(dy).sub(viewZ(dy.negate())))).div(z0.max(0.5));\\n    const dn = max(length(n(dx).sub(n(dx.negate()))), length(n(dy).sub(n(dy.negate()))));\\n    const e = max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.12, dz), smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, dn)).mul(oneMinus(smoothstep(u.fadeNear, u.fadeFar, z0)));\\n    const c = mix(colorTex.rgb, u.ink, e);\\n    const p = floor(uv().mul(screenSize));\\n    const g = hash(p.x.add(p.y.mul(1571))).sub(0.5).mul(u.grain);\\n    return vec4(c.add(g).mul(oneMinus(length(uv().sub(0.5)).mul(0.62).pow(2.2))), 1);\\n  })();\\n480→  return { post, u };\\n}\\n// Render with post.render(). Remember: camera far changes need u.far updated.\\n```\\nGotchas ✔:\\n- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical streaks over the whole image.\\n- `fadeNear/fadeFar` removes depth-line noise at the horizon, which otherwise draws a thick black band. Make sure the sea disc is big (radius 590) and blends to `skyLow` with distance.\\n- Do not merge vertices on hard-edged props: smooth normals smear the toon shading. Plain `BoxGeometry` looks right, and the depth lines cover the hull's corners.\\n- `InstancedMesh` with `setColorAt` works with toon and the hull ✔. Use it for marbles, splash blobs, fruit crowds.\\n- Glass ✔: **do not use a toon material on glass.** The hull pass draws solid ink back faces behind it and the glass turns smoky grey. Use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ color: 0xe8fbff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })`, and draw its outline with thin toon torus rings at the base, shoulder, neck and lip, plus a couple of narrow white highlight strips. It looks like a clean inked glass jar.\\n490→- No TSL compute anywhere. It breaks the WebGL2 fallback. All particles are CPU-driven instances.\\n\\n**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\\n\\n**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\\n\\n**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\\n\\n- **Peg doll, 1.5 m.** Feet: white flattened spheres r .1 scaled (1, .55, 1.5), at x ±.13. Legs: skin capsules r .08, length .3. Hips at y .46. Shorts: capsule r .2 (scaled 1.05, 1, .85). Shirt: capsule r .21, length .24 (z scale .82). Head: sphere r .23, centre .82 above hips. Headband: torus r .228. Eyes: ink spheres r .024 at (±.08, .85, .205) from hips. Cheeks: pink flattened spheres. Arms: visible darker wooden ball joints r .075 at shoulders (±.26, .5) and r .06 at elbows, skin capsule segments, white mitten spheres r .07. Skin `#F2D3A8`, joints `#D9A86C`, shorts `#2B4A6F`. Vary with paint, hats (visor, beret, bucket hat, perm of spheres, wizard hat, tiny crown), and small height and width changes for guests.\\n- **Trudy.** Rounded box (1.1, .42, .26), radius .1, `#F5B700`, on two stubby brown pegs. Dark window (.56, .2, .05) on the face holding a lime `#B9F26B` capsule vial r .075, length .36, lying on its side. Bubble: white flattened sphere with an ink pupil; it slides in the vial. Two ink marks on the vial like a level's centre lines. Round lime end vials at x ±.42 as cheeks. Ink torus arc for a mouth. She hops with squash and stretch. This design is very readable: keep it.\\n500→- **Ms. Stillwater.** One long orange leg (cylinder r .025, height 1.05), the other tucked. Body sphere r .34 scaled (.8, .92, 1.35), `#A9C2D4`. Wings: flattened ellipsoids `#7E9CB4`. S-curve neck: `TubeGeometry` of a Catmull-Rom curve, r .062, `#D6E3EC`, rising to y 2.2. Head sphere r .115. Beak: yellow cone. Ink crest cone. Half-closed eyes as ink torus arcs. A small `#C24D6A` scarf at the base of the neck. For animation, build the neck from 5 joint segments.\\n- **Coach Bolster.** Capsule r .72, length .5, scaled (1.06, 1, .9), `#B9806A`. Red sweatband torus at the top. Two pale muzzle spheres, cream cone tusks, tiny ink eyes, flattened flippers `#8D5F4F`, a white lanyard torus and a yellow whistle.\\n- **Gary the gull.** White ellipsoid body, grey flattened wings, yellow cone beak, orange peg legs, ink dot eyes and one permanently angry brow.\\n- **The jar ✔.** `LatheGeometry` through (.01,0) (1,.02) (1.08,.12) (1.08,1.9) (.86,2.2) (.86,2.45) (.95,2.5) with the glass recipe from the gotchas above; rings at (r 1.08, y .12), (1.08, 1.9), (.86, 2.2), (.95, 2.5). Marbles: `InstancedMesh` of toon spheres r .14 with `setColorAt`. Physics: a base cylinder and a ring of 16 cuboid walls.\\n- **Island.** Sand disc r 22 over a grass disc r 15 (flush with the sand, or dolls sink into it: all walking surfaces share y = 0), big inverted rock cone below on its one pivot rock. Toy trees: peg trunk plus ball top. Lighthouse: stacked red and cream rings with a lamp and a blue cap. Pavilions: toy-block posts and rounded slab roofs in teal and orange. Sun Deck, Bandstand, Circus Green, Yard, Pier, Jar, Mirror pavilion, Notice Board, Gazebo, Bed Hut at fixed spots around the rim and centre. Every set in section 6 appears here as a landmark, and the class itself runs in its own world (section 16).\\n- **Island map** (island-local x, z; you arrive at 0, 10; the ring road for Island Loop runs at r 19):\\n\\n| Landmark | x, z | What's there | Landmark | x, z | What's there |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Lighthouse | 2, −9 | The Grand Hold | Bandstand | 15, −3 | Step Class |\\n510→| Jar | −3, −4 | Marble pours | Boardwalk gate | 19, 3 | Room Service |\\n| Circus Green | 0, 2 | Plate Spinners | Pier | 15, 12, running out to sea | Fruit Hat, Plank & Perch, Springboard tower at its end |\\n| Sun Deck | −16, −3 | Poses | Beach | 5, 17 | Tug of Peace |\\n| Lily Pond | −13, 7 | Still Water | Notice Board | 1, 9 | Today's Plan, Journal |\\n| Quarry Terrace | −9, −13 | The Cairn | Bed Hut | 8, 8 | End of the day |\\n| The Yard | −7, 14 | Log Reps | Mirror pavilion | 9, −14 | Paint and hats |\\n| Gazebo | 14, −11 | Group Class, Tippy Raft's dock | | | |\\n\\n  Guests wander between these spots on simple paths in island-local coordinates. They need no physics.\\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words: CLACK!, SPLOSH!, WOBBLE. Canvas-texture sprites that pop and fade. Everyone squashes on landing. A small hit-stop on catches and big events. Gentle camera shake, off with reduce-shake.\\n520→\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Animation\\n\\nUse Three's `AnimationMixer` and `AnimationClip` as the base (TASK.md requires the engine's system). Rig each character as a hierarchy of named `THREE.Group` joints with the rigid parts parented to them: `root, hips, spine, head, shoulderL/R, elbowL/R, hipL/R, kneeL/R, ankleL/R` for dolls. No skinned meshes are needed for jointed toys. `poseClip(name, duration, keyframes)` in `src/anim/clips.js` compiles `{ t, pose: { shoulderL: [x, y, z in degrees], ... } }` into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s on `'shoulderL.quaternion'`. `AnimationMixer` plays them with `crossFadeTo`.\\n\\n- **Doll clips:** idle, breathe, walk, jog, hop, land, cheer, clap, shrug, bow, flail, sleep, one per pose (8), squat (scrubbed: `action.paused = true`, `action.time = depth × duration`), plank, sit cross-legged, lean-back tug, hip-circle (scrubbed by hoop phase), step-L, step-R, carry-tray, hold-slab, tuck, pike, dive-ready.\\n- **Trudy:** hop, idle wobble, gasp, sulk, faint, read clipboard; plus procedural squash and bubble.\\n- **Heron:** stand, slow demo versions of each pose, neck bow, head turn, pat. **Walrus:** bounce, clap, huge inhale, salute, catch log. **Gull:** fly, land, peck, squawk.\\n530→- **Layering order each frame:** `mixer.update(dt)`, then apply procedural lean from the balance core at `root`, head bobble, breath on spine. Then, while ragdolled, parts follow bodies instead.\\n\\n**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\\n\\nGhost lanes do not ragdoll: they play a flop clip.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Audio\\n\\n540→Everything is synthesised with Web Audio. No files. All instruments take a `ctx` argument, so the same code runs on an `AudioContext` and on an `OfflineAudioContext` for tests.\\n\\n**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\\n\\n**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\\n\\n**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\\n\\n**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\\n\\n550→| Track | Where | Feel | Chords per bar |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Level Best | Title, menus, ending | 92 BPM light swing; e-piano, vibes lead, soft bass, brushes | D♭maj9 B♭m9 E♭m9 A♭13, twice; G♭maj9 Fm7 E♭m9 A♭13sus |\\n| Island | Hub | 84 BPM bossa; marimba, upright-ish bass, shaker | Fmaj7 Am7 B♭maj7 C9, Fmaj7 Dm9 Gm9 C13 |\\n| Stillness | Poses, Cairn, Still Water | 60 BPM, free; pad, bells on the breath cycle, flute | Dmaj9 Emaj9/D, slowly, Lydian |\\n| Strength | Log Reps, Plank, Tug | 100 BPM funk; slap bass, clav-like e-piano, brass stabs, gated snare | Em9 A13 |\\n| Cardio | Step Class, Plates, Loop | Bpm from the chart; four-on-the-floor, octave slap bass, e-piano, brass, gated snare | D♭maj7 Cm7 B♭m7 E♭sus |\\n| Balance | Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Raft | 118 BPM bouncy; marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, tuba-like bass | C6 Am7 Dm7 G7 |\\n| Level | The Grand Hold | Starts as bare e-piano of the title theme, then every instrument, slower, in tune | As Level Best |\\n| Stings | Results, reveal, unlock | Three seconds or less, one per medal, drum roll and reveal, little fanfare | |\\n560→\\n**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\\n```\\nBar 1 D♭maj9: [0,77,3] [3,75,1] [4,73,2] [6,68,4] [12,70,2] [14,72,2]\\nBar 2 B♭m9:   [0,73,3] [3,72,1] [4,70,2] [6,65,6] [14,68,2]\\nBar 3 E♭m9:   [0,70,3] [3,68,1] [4,66,2] [6,63,4] [12,65,2] [14,66,2]\\nBar 4 A♭13:   [0,68,3] [3,70,1] [4,72,2] [6,75,6]\\n```\\n**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\\n\\n570→**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\\n\\n| Voice | Base pitch | Rate | Character |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Trudy | 420 Hz | 16 syll/s | squeaky, nervous, high formants |\\n| Ms. Stillwater | 170 Hz | 7 syll/s | breathy, adds noise, long gaps |\\n| Coach Bolster | 95 Hz | 11 syll/s | big, saw, loud, bursts |\\n| Guests | 200-320 Hz | 12 syll/s | varied per guest |\\n| Gary | | | FM squawk |\\n\\n580→**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Multiplayer\\n\\n**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\\n\\n### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\\n\\n590→From the main menu or the Gazebo. **Lobby:** four mats. Each pad's Cross joins it, Enter or a click joins keyboard and mouse as one player, Circle or Esc leaves. Seats use the hub's colours. The first to join drives menus. **Playlist:** Quick Class (three random) or pick three. All events with `couch: true`: Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, Room Service. **Points:** 4, 3, 2, 1 per event. **Final podium:** a toy-block podium, winners on top, and it tips over.\\n\\nAll lanes use one camera that frames everyone; each event's camera has a wide setting for this. Room Service uses four parallel boardwalks with the camera following the group, and the leader's finish starts a 15 s clock for the rest. Each lane reads its own pad by gamepad index, and prompts on that player's disc follow that device.\\n\\n### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\\n\\nThe contract is `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Read it. I ran a minimal WebGPU ghost game built this way through the real harness and it passed all 16 checks at 1, 2 and 4 players, on the bad network, and with the host leaving ✔.\\n\\n**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\\n\\n600→**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\\n```html\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>…vendor/session.js, verbatim, nothing before it…</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay balanced through the class: highest score wins\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\\\">\\n610→<meta name=\\\"hub:type\\\" content=\\\"Game\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:source\\\" content=\\\"Wii Fit\\\">\\n```\\nDo not set `hub:webgpu required`: the WebGL2 fallback works ✔, so the hub can deal this game to everyone. Fetch the client from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` into `vendor/session.js`. **The hub checks it by SHA-256** from its banner to its last line, with LF line endings: 1.4.0 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` ✔. Make the build fail if it doesn't match. If the hub has released a newer client, take its hash from `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` in that repo.\\n\\n**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\\n```js\\nasync function main() {\\n  if (AIHub.active) {\\n620→    const pConnect = AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });   // hello must go out within 10 s\\n    await bootEngine();                       // renderer.init(), RAPIER.init(), build the world\\n    const info = await pConnect;\\n    if (info) runSession(info); else runStandalone();     // null: no hub after all\\n  } else { await bootEngine(); runStandalone(); }\\n}\\nmain().catch(showBootFailure);                // shows the message, calls AIHub.fail(message)\\n```\\n\\n**In `runSession(info)`:**\\n630→1. No title, menus or attract. Build the class stage, line up the dolls, then `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press and without waiting for every shader. Register `AIHub.on` handlers before `ready()`.\\n2. **Your own card** (`card: true`). The class lined up on the deck, Trudy with a clipboard, the music. Overlay: the game's name, each player's name in their colour with a tick when ready (from `card.ready`), how to win, this player's controls with glyphs, time left from `card.left` (show \\\"Starting...\\\" when `card.starting`, \\\"Paused\\\" when `card.paused`). Enter or Cross or a tap: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle or M (name the key on the card) or a tap: `AIHub.answer('more')`. The harness presses Enter by dispatching a keydown on the page, so listen on `window`. Nothing on the card changes the round, and it never touches `Math.random`.\\n3. **The round runs on `AIHub.time()` alone,** so everyone is in the same class at the same moment. Count down from 3 while the time is negative. Three slots picked with `AIHub.rng('deal')` from the session set (Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard), no repeats: each is 6 s intro, 50 s play (`sessionConfig` makes each event fit exactly), 6 s score. Total 186 s, within the 270 s limit that 3 minutes gives ✔. If time jumps (hidden tab), go to the slot the clock says.\\n4. **Ghosts.** From the moment `AIHub.time()` reaches 0, including during intros, about 10 times a second, `AIHub.send({ t:'g', a:slotIndex, x, z, lx, lz, c:clipIndex, f:fallen?1:0, s:score }, { reliable:false })`. Keep it well under 1,200 bytes. Show a ghost only when its `a` equals your slot. Interpolate over about 150 ms. Ghosts are visual only: name tag (set with `textContent`, never HTML), seat colour on the shirt, a flop clip for `f`. They never touch your physics. Remove on `leave`.\\n5. **Scoring.** Each slot gives `round(form × 10)`. Call `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'Fruit Hat 420' })` every second **from the moment time reaches 0, with score 0 during the first intro.** The harness checks for ghost traffic and progress about 4 seconds into play, well before the first class starts, and fails the game if there is none. `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once at the end, for example stats `{ 'Floor Exercises': 2, 'Best medal': 'Pleasant', 'Wobble Age': 41 }`, and an award from a small list (\\\"Suspiciously Still\\\"). Then a calm \\\"waiting for the others\\\" with Trudy.\\n6. `time-up`: send `result` at once if not already sent (grace is 5 s ✔). `end`: stop input, sending and all sound, and keep the last frame; the hub draws its results over the game.\\n7. Options opens a small overlay with volumes only, and it doesn't pause. No restart, no quit, no Dev Mode.\\n\\n**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\\n\\n640→**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":400,\"limit\":250,\"raw_output\":\"- \\\"You fell very consistently. Consistency is important.\\\"\\n- \\\"It's been three days. The island tilted a bit. I'm not saying those things are connected. They are connected.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your Wobble Age is 61. That's down from 74! If this continues, you will eventually be born.\\\"\\n- \\\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\\\"\\n- *Personal questions, always Yes/No, always a reply either way.* \\\"Do you trust your knees?\\\" Yes: \\\"They speak highly of you too.\\\" No: \\\"They know.\\\" · \\\"Do you ever stand on one leg without knowing why?\\\" Yes: \\\"I thought so.\\\" No: \\\"That's what someone on one leg would say.\\\" · \\\"Have you been leaning on things? Be honest. I can tell.\\\" · \\\"When you walk into a room, which way do you lean first?\\\" Left / Right: \\\"Interesting. I'm writing that down.\\\"\\n\\n**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\\n\\n- \\\"Breathe in. Breathe out. Not like that, but thank you for trying.\\\"\\n- \\\"This pose is called the Apologetic Pine. Feel your weight. Feel it apologise.\\\"\\n- \\\"If you are shaking, that is only your body leaving.\\\"\\n- \\\"A heron can stand like this for hours. A heron has also never been asked why.\\\"\\n- \\\"You got off the mat. I saw. It's all right. I see everything. It's all right.\\\"\\n\\n**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\\n\\n- \\\"YOU'RE DOING IT. I don't know what it is, but you're doing it.\\\"\\n- \\\"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one. I've lied to you and I'd do it again.\\\"\\n- \\\"I believe in you as much as I believe in lunch.\\\"\\n- \\\"Your form is a form! That's what form is!\\\"\\n\\n**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \\\"He's part of it now.\\\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Art bible\\n\\nThe look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\\n\\n**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\\n\\n**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\\n\\n**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\\n\\n| Time | skyTop | skyMid | skyLow | sea | seaHi | sun, intensity | hemi sky, ground, intensity |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| dawn ✔ | 8FB4E0 | F6CFD8 | FFE3B5 | 3E8FB5 | A9E3E4 | FFE6D8, 2.6 | FCE8F0, 7C8BB5, 1.35 |\\n| noon ✔ | 6FC3E8 | BFE9F0 | FFF2D0 | 16A8B0 | 8CF0DD | FFFBE8, 3.2 | FFFFFF, A09080, 1.35 |\\n| golden ✔ | 7FB7C9 | F7D9A6 | F59E6C | 1F9EA3 | 71DCCB | FFF0D0, 3.1 | FFF4E0, 8A7FA0, 1.25 |\\n| dusk ✔ | 2B2F6B | A8558F | F58A5C | 24406F | C26A8C | FFB98A, 2.4 | B48AD0, 2B2F5F, 1.1 |\\n\\nLerp palette uniforms and lights over 3 s between steps.\\n\\n**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\\n\\n**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\\n\\n**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\\n\\n```js\\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { mrt, output, normalView, toonOutlinePass, color, float, vec2, vec4, uv, screenSize, Fn, abs, max,\\n  smoothstep, mix, uniform, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, time, length, hash, floor, oneMinus } from 'three/tsl';\\n\\nexport function makeInk(renderer, scene, camera) {\\n  const INK = 0x1a1420;\\n  const u = { hull: uniform(0.0028), width: uniform(1.4), boil: uniform(0.5), fadeNear: uniform(30), fadeFar: uniform(90),\\n              grain: uniform(0.02), near: uniform(camera.near), far: uniform(camera.far), ink: uniform(new THREE.Color(INK)) };\\n  const scenePass = toonOutlinePass(scene, camera, color(INK), u.hull, float(1));\\n  scenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, normal: normalView }));\\n  const colorTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), normalTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('normal'),\\n        depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\\n  const post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\\n  post.outputNode = Fn(() => {\\n    const cell = floor(uv().mul(vec2(40, 23)));\\n    const wob = hash(floor(time.mul(8)).add(cell.x).add(cell.y.mul(17))).sub(0.5).mul(u.boil);   // hand-drawn line boil\\n    const px = u.width.add(wob).div(screenSize);\\n    const viewZ = (o) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(uv().add(o)).r, u.near, u.far);\\n    const n = (o) => normalTex.sample(uv().add(o)).xyz;\\n    const dx = vec2(px.x, 0), dy = vec2(0, px.y);\\n    const z0 = viewZ(vec2(0, 0)).abs();\\n    const dz = max(abs(viewZ(dx).sub(viewZ(dx.negate()))), abs(viewZ(dy).sub(viewZ(dy.negate())))).div(z0.max(0.5));\\n    const dn = max(length(n(dx).sub(n(dx.negate()))), length(n(dy).sub(n(dy.negate()))));\\n    const e = max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.12, dz), smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, dn)).mul(oneMinus(smoothstep(u.fadeNear, u.fadeFar, z0)));\\n    const c = mix(colorTex.rgb, u.ink, e);\\n    const p = floor(uv().mul(screenSize));\\n    const g = hash(p.x.add(p.y.mul(1571))).sub(0.5).mul(u.grain);\\n    return vec4(c.add(g).mul(oneMinus(length(uv().sub(0.5)).mul(0.62).pow(2.2))), 1);\\n  })();\\n  return { post, u };\\n}\\n// Render with post.render(). Remember: camera far changes need u.far updated.\\n```\\nGotchas ✔:\\n- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical streaks over the whole image.\\n- `fadeNear/fadeFar` removes depth-line noise at the horizon, which otherwise draws a thick black band. Make sure the sea disc is big (radius 590) and blends to `skyLow` with distance.\\n- Do not merge vertices on hard-edged props: smooth normals smear the toon shading. Plain `BoxGeometry` looks right, and the depth lines cover the hull's corners.\\n- `InstancedMesh` with `setColorAt` works with toon and the hull ✔. Use it for marbles, splash blobs, fruit crowds.\\n- Glass ✔: **do not use a toon material on glass.** The hull pass draws solid ink back faces behind it and the glass turns smoky grey. Use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ color: 0xe8fbff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })`, and draw its outline with thin toon torus rings at the base, shoulder, neck and lip, plus a couple of narrow white highlight strips. It looks like a clean inked glass jar.\\n- No TSL compute anywhere. It breaks the WebGL2 fallback. All particles are CPU-driven instances.\\n\\n**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\\n\\n**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\\n\\n**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\\n\\n- **Peg doll, 1.5 m.** Feet: white flattened spheres r .1 scaled (1, .55, 1.5), at x ±.13. Legs: skin capsules r .08, length .3. Hips at y .46. Shorts: capsule r .2 (scaled 1.05, 1, .85). Shirt: capsule r .21, length .24 (z scale .82). Head: sphere r .23, centre .82 above hips. Headband: torus r .228. Eyes: ink spheres r .024 at (±.08, .85, .205) from hips. Cheeks: pink flattened spheres. Arms: visible darker wooden ball joints r .075 at shoulders (±.26, .5) and r .06 at elbows, skin capsule segments, white mitten spheres r .07. Skin `#F2D3A8`, joints `#D9A86C`, shorts `#2B4A6F`. Vary with paint, hats (visor, beret, bucket hat, perm of spheres, wizard hat, tiny crown), and small height and width changes for guests.\\n- **Trudy.** Rounded box (1.1, .42, .26), radius .1, `#F5B700`, on two stubby brown pegs. Dark window (.56, .2, .05) on the face holding a lime `#B9F26B` capsule vial r .075, length .36, lying on its side. Bubble: white flattened sphere with an ink pupil; it slides in the vial. Two ink marks on the vial like a level's centre lines. Round lime end vials at x ±.42 as cheeks. Ink torus arc for a mouth. She hops with squash and stretch. This design is very readable: keep it.\\n- **Ms. Stillwater.** One long orange leg (cylinder r .025, height 1.05), the other tucked. Body sphere r .34 scaled (.8, .92, 1.35), `#A9C2D4`. Wings: flattened ellipsoids `#7E9CB4`. S-curve neck: `TubeGeometry` of a Catmull-Rom curve, r .062, `#D6E3EC`, rising to y 2.2. Head sphere r .115. Beak: yellow cone. Ink crest cone. Half-closed eyes as ink torus arcs. A small `#C24D6A` scarf at the base of the neck. For animation, build the neck from 5 joint segments.\\n- **Coach Bolster.** Capsule r .72, length .5, scaled (1.06, 1, .9), `#B9806A`. Red sweatband torus at the top. Two pale muzzle spheres, cream cone tusks, tiny ink eyes, flattened flippers `#8D5F4F`, a white lanyard torus and a yellow whistle.\\n- **Gary the gull.** White ellipsoid body, grey flattened wings, yellow cone beak, orange peg legs, ink dot eyes and one permanently angry brow.\\n- **The jar ✔.** `LatheGeometry` through (.01,0) (1,.02) (1.08,.12) (1.08,1.9) (.86,2.2) (.86,2.45) (.95,2.5) with the glass recipe from the gotchas above; rings at (r 1.08, y .12), (1.08, 1.9), (.86, 2.2), (.95, 2.5). Marbles: `InstancedMesh` of toon spheres r .14 with `setColorAt`. Physics: a base cylinder and a ring of 16 cuboid walls.\\n- **Island.** Sand disc r 22 over a grass disc r 15 (flush with the sand, or dolls sink into it: all walking surfaces share y = 0), big inverted rock cone below on its one pivot rock. Toy trees: peg trunk plus ball top. Lighthouse: stacked red and cream rings with a lamp and a blue cap. Pavilions: toy-block posts and rounded slab roofs in teal and orange. Sun Deck, Bandstand, Circus Green, Yard, Pier, Jar, Mirror pavilion, Notice Board, Gazebo, Bed Hut at fixed spots around the rim and centre. Every set in section 6 appears here as a landmark, and the class itself runs in its own world (section 16).\\n- **Island map** (island-local x, z; you arrive at 0, 10; the ring road for Island Loop runs at r 19):\\n\\n| Landmark | x, z | What's there | Landmark | x, z | What's there |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Lighthouse | 2, −9 | The Grand Hold | Bandstand | 15, −3 | Step Class |\\n| Jar | −3, −4 | Marble pours | Boardwalk gate | 19, 3 | Room Service |\\n| Circus Green | 0, 2 | Plate Spinners | Pier | 15, 12, running out to sea | Fruit Hat, Plank & Perch, Springboard tower at its end |\\n| Sun Deck | −16, −3 | Poses | Beach | 5, 17 | Tug of Peace |\\n| Lily Pond | −13, 7 | Still Water | Notice Board | 1, 9 | Today's Plan, Journal |\\n| Quarry Terrace | −9, −13 | The Cairn | Bed Hut | 8, 8 | End of the day |\\n| The Yard | −7, 14 | Log Reps | Mirror pavilion | 9, −14 | Paint and hats |\\n| Gazebo | 14, −11 | Group Class, Tippy Raft's dock | | | |\\n\\n  Guests wander between these spots on simple paths in island-local coordinates. They need no physics.\\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words: CLACK!, SPLOSH!, WOBBLE. Canvas-texture sprites that pop and fade. Everyone squashes on landing. A small hit-stop on catches and big events. Gentle camera shake, off with reduce-shake.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Animation\\n\\nUse Three's `AnimationMixer` and `AnimationClip` as the base (TASK.md requires the engine's system). Rig each character as a hierarchy of named `THREE.Group` joints with the rigid parts parented to them: `root, hips, spine, head, shoulderL/R, elbowL/R, hipL/R, kneeL/R, ankleL/R` for dolls. No skinned meshes are needed for jointed toys. `poseClip(name, duration, keyframes)` in `src/anim/clips.js` compiles `{ t, pose: { shoulderL: [x, y, z in degrees], ... } }` into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s on `'shoulderL.quaternion'`. `AnimationMixer` plays them with `crossFadeTo`.\\n\\n- **Doll clips:** idle, breathe, walk, jog, hop, land, cheer, clap, shrug, bow, flail, sleep, one per pose (8), squat (scrubbed: `action.paused = true`, `action.time = depth × duration`), plank, sit cross-legged, lean-back tug, hip-circle (scrubbed by hoop phase), step-L, step-R, carry-tray, hold-slab, tuck, pike, dive-ready.\\n- **Trudy:** hop, idle wobble, gasp, sulk, faint, read clipboard; plus procedural squash and bubble.\\n- **Heron:** stand, slow demo versions of each pose, neck bow, head turn, pat. **Walrus:** bounce, clap, huge inhale, salute, catch log. **Gull:** fly, land, peck, squawk.\\n- **Layering order each frame:** `mixer.update(dt)`, then apply procedural lean from the balance core at `root`, head bobble, breath on spine. Then, while ragdolled, parts follow bodies instead.\\n\\n**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\\n\\nGhost lanes do not ragdoll: they play a flop clip.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Audio\\n\\nEverything is synthesised with Web Audio. No files. All instruments take a `ctx` argument, so the same code runs on an `AudioContext` and on an `OfflineAudioContext` for tests.\\n\\n**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\\n\\n**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\\n\\n**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\\n\\n**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\\n\\n| Track | Where | Feel | Chords per bar |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Level Best | Title, menus, ending | 92 BPM light swing; e-piano, vibes lead, soft bass, brushes | D♭maj9 B♭m9 E♭m9 A♭13, twice; G♭maj9 Fm7 E♭m9 A♭13sus |\\n| Island | Hub | 84 BPM bossa; marimba, upright-ish bass, shaker | Fmaj7 Am7 B♭maj7 C9, Fmaj7 Dm9 Gm9 C13 |\\n| Stillness | Poses, Cairn, Still Water | 60 BPM, free; pad, bells on the breath cycle, flute | Dmaj9 Emaj9/D, slowly, Lydian |\\n| Strength | Log Reps, Plank, Tug | 100 BPM funk; slap bass, clav-like e-piano, brass stabs, gated snare | Em9 A13 |\\n| Cardio | Step Class, Plates, Loop | Bpm from the chart; four-on-the-floor, octave slap bass, e-piano, brass, gated snare | D♭maj7 Cm7 B♭m7 E♭sus |\\n| Balance | Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Raft | 118 BPM bouncy; marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, tuba-like bass | C6 Am7 Dm7 G7 |\\n| Level | The Grand Hold | Starts as bare e-piano of the title theme, then every instrument, slower, in tune | As Level Best |\\n| Stings | Results, reveal, unlock | Three seconds or less, one per medal, drum roll and reveal, little fanfare | |\\n\\n**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\\n```\\nBar 1 D♭maj9: [0,77,3] [3,75,1] [4,73,2] [6,68,4] [12,70,2] [14,72,2]\\nBar 2 B♭m9:   [0,73,3] [3,72,1] [4,70,2] [6,65,6] [14,68,2]\\nBar 3 E♭m9:   [0,70,3] [3,68,1] [4,66,2] [6,63,4] [12,65,2] [14,66,2]\\nBar 4 A♭13:   [0,68,3] [3,70,1] [4,72,2] [6,75,6]\\n```\\n**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\\n\\n**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\\n\\n| Voice | Base pitch | Rate | Character |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Trudy | 420 Hz | 16 syll/s | squeaky, nervous, high formants |\\n| Ms. Stillwater | 170 Hz | 7 syll/s | breathy, adds noise, long gaps |\\n| Coach Bolster | 95 Hz | 11 syll/s | big, saw, loud, bursts |\\n| Guests | 200-320 Hz | 12 syll/s | varied per guest |\\n| Gary | | | FM squawk |\\n\\n**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Multiplayer\\n\\n**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\\n\\n### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\\n\\nFrom the main menu or the Gazebo. **Lobby:** four mats. Each pad's Cross joins it, Enter or a click joins keyboard and mouse as one player, Circle or Esc leaves. Seats use the hub's colours. The first to join drives menus. **Playlist:** Quick Class (three random) or pick three. All events with `couch: true`: Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, Room Service. **Points:** 4, 3, 2, 1 per event. **Final podium:** a toy-block podium, winners on top, and it tips over.\\n\\nAll lanes use one camera that frames everyone; each event's camera has a wide setting for this. Room Service uses four parallel boardwalks with the camera following the group, and the leader's finish starts a 15 s clock for the rest. Each lane reads its own pad by gamepad index, and prompts on that player's disc follow that device.\\n\\n### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\\n\\nThe contract is `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Read it. I ran a minimal WebGPU ghost game built this way through the real harness and it passed all 16 checks at 1, 2 and 4 players, on the bad network, and with the host leaving ✔.\\n\\n**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\\n\\n**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\\n```html\\n<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\n<script>…vendor/session.js, verbatim, nothing before it…</script>\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay balanced through the class: highest score wins\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:type\\\" content=\\\"Game\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:source\\\" content=\\\"Wii Fit\\\">\\n```\\nDo not set `hub:webgpu required`: the WebGL2 fallback works ✔, so the hub can deal this game to everyone. Fetch the client from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` into `vendor/session.js`. **The hub checks it by SHA-256** from its banner to its last line, with LF line endings: 1.4.0 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` ✔. Make the build fail if it doesn't match. If the hub has released a newer client, take its hash from `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` in that repo.\\n\\n**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\\n```js\\nasync function main() {\\n  if (AIHub.active) {\\n    const pConnect = AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });   // hello must go out within 10 s\\n    await bootEngine();                       // renderer.init(), RAPIER.init(), build the world\\n    const info = await pConnect;\\n    if (info) runSession(info); else runStandalone();     // null: no hub after all\\n  } else { await bootEngine(); runStandalone(); }\\n}\\nmain().catch(showBootFailure);                // shows the message, calls AIHub.fail(message)\\n```\\n\\n**In `runSession(info)`:**\\n1. No title, menus or attract. Build the class stage, line up the dolls, then `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press and without waiting for every shader. Register `AIHub.on` handlers before `ready()`.\\n2. **Your own card** (`card: true`). The class lined up on the deck, Trudy with a clipboard, the music. Overlay: the game's name, each player's name in their colour with a tick when ready (from `card.ready`), how to win, this player's controls with glyphs, time left from `card.left` (show \\\"Starting...\\\" when `card.starting`, \\\"Paused\\\" when `card.paused`). Enter or Cross or a tap: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle or M (name the key on the card) or a tap: `AIHub.answer('more')`. The harness presses Enter by dispatching a keydown on the page, so listen on `window`. Nothing on the card changes the round, and it never touches `Math.random`.\\n3. **The round runs on `AIHub.time()` alone,** so everyone is in the same class at the same moment. Count down from 3 while the time is negative. Three slots picked with `AIHub.rng('deal')` from the session set (Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard), no repeats: each is 6 s intro, 50 s play (`sessionConfig` makes each event fit exactly), 6 s score. Total 186 s, within the 270 s limit that 3 minutes gives ✔. If time jumps (hidden tab), go to the slot the clock says.\\n4. **Ghosts.** From the moment `AIHub.time()` reaches 0, including during intros, about 10 times a second, `AIHub.send({ t:'g', a:slotIndex, x, z, lx, lz, c:clipIndex, f:fallen?1:0, s:score }, { reliable:false })`. Keep it well under 1,200 bytes. Show a ghost only when its `a` equals your slot. Interpolate over about 150 ms. Ghosts are visual only: name tag (set with `textContent`, never HTML), seat colour on the shirt, a flop clip for `f`. They never touch your physics. Remove on `leave`.\\n5. **Scoring.** Each slot gives `round(form × 10)`. Call `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'Fruit Hat 420' })` every second **from the moment time reaches 0, with score 0 during the first intro.** The harness checks for ghost traffic and progress about 4 seconds into play, well before the first class starts, and fails the game if there is none. `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once at the end, for example stats `{ 'Floor Exercises': 2, 'Best medal': 'Pleasant', 'Wobble Age': 41 }`, and an award from a small list (\\\"Suspiciously Still\\\"). Then a calm \\\"waiting for the others\\\" with Trudy.\\n6. `time-up`: send `result` at once if not already sent (grace is 5 s ✔). `end`: stop input, sending and all sound, and keep the last frame; the hub draws its results over the game.\\n7. Options opens a small overlay with volumes only, and it doesn't pause. No restart, no quit, no Dev Mode.\\n\\n**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\\n\\n**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":1112}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"df78ff49-a681-4d47-b376-009fbb549575"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_4","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d149867b-72b3-4d76-b363-572e9de078c6-9","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","offset":644,"limit":400}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9930,"output_tokens":91,"cache_read_input_tokens":38016,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"ed711254-a734-4779-9936-6ef9dbeacb2d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d149867b-72b3-4d76-b363-572e9de078c6-9","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"644→## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\\n650→---\\n\\n## 14. Dev mode\\n\\nFrom the Pause menu. It follows the same 10-foot rules: pages of big controls by group, six to a page, usable by pad, keyboard and mouse. Dev Mode is off in a session.\\n\\n**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\\n\\n**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\\n- *Balance:* maxLean, margin, zeta, ankleMax, fall angle, reflex, flail boost, flail cooldown, sway scale, tremor scale, recover time.\\n660→- *World:* gravity, island weight (° at rim), island rest tilt, island spring, walk speed.\\n- *Events:* fruit mass, fruit interval, landing radius, stone mass, stone interval, ring size at end, gust force, plate decay, plate gain, rhythm window, rep tempo, board gain, air torque.\\n- *Rewards:* marbles per minute, medal scale.\\n- *Ink:* hull, line width, boil, grain.\\n\\n**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Touch-only devices\\n670→\\n**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\\n\\nShow the title with \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. There are no touch controls anywhere else. Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual, and a tap returns to this screen. If a pad connects or a key is pressed, drop this state and carry on normally. In a session the card's Ready and More time accept taps, per the hub's rules.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. Architecture and build\\n\\n**Where things go.** Keep all your dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names (your dev folder). Develop separate modules there, and bundle them into the single HTML file in the root, named as TASK.md says, when you're done. TASK.md allows external CDN dependencies; this plan inlines everything instead, so the file works offline and inside the hub.\\n680→\\n**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\\n\\n```\\npackage.json\\nvendor/session.js\\ntools/build.mjs  tools/test.mjs  tools/shots.mjs  tools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}\\nsrc/main.js\\nsrc/core/      loop.js  rng.js  save.js  script.js  tuning.js\\nsrc/input/     input.js  glyphs.js  bot.js\\n690→src/render/    renderer.js  ink.js  palette.js  sky.js  sea.js  cameraRig.js  particles.js  comicText.js\\nsrc/art/       materials.js  doll.js  trudy.js  heron.js  walrus.js  gull.js  island.js  props.js\\nsrc/anim/      clips.js  rig.js\\nsrc/physics/   world.js  balancer.js  ragdoll.js  island.js\\nsrc/audio/     engine.js  instruments.js  tracks.js  sequencer.js  voice.js  sfx.js\\nsrc/ui/        ui.css  focus.js  hud.js  disc.js  speech.js  screens/*.js\\nsrc/game/      director.js  programme.js  checkin.js  jar.js  hub.js  profiles.js  results.js\\n               attract.js  devmode.js  groupclass.js  session.js  ghost.js  ending.js\\nsrc/events/    base.js  poses.js  cairn.js  stillWater.js  logReps.js  plank.js  tug.js  stepClass.js\\n               plates.js  islandLoop.js  fruitHat.js  roomService.js  springboard.js  raft.js  grandHold.js\\n700→src/data/      config.js  events.js  programme.js  poses.js  charts.js  dialogue.js  tuning.js\\n               palettes.js  unlocks.js  names.js  about.js\\ntest/*.mjs\\n```\\n\\n**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\\n\\n**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\\n\\n**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\\n710→\\n**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\\n\\n**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\\n\\n```js\\n// tools/build.mjs (core; OUT is the file name and location TASK.md gives you)\\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js', 'utf8').replace(/\\\\r\\\\n/g, '\\\\n');\\nconst END = \\\"})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\\\";\\n720→const hash = createHash('sha256').update(session.slice(session.indexOf('/*! aihub-session '), session.indexOf(END) + END.length)).digest('hex');\\nif (hash !== '529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a') throw new Error('not the released hub client');\\nconst r = await build({ entryPoints: ['src/main.js'], bundle: true, format: 'iife', target: 'es2022', minify: true,\\n  write: false, legalComments: 'none', loader: { '.css': 'text' } });\\nconst js = r.outputFiles[0].text.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi, '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\nconst font = (w) => readFileSync(`node_modules/@fontsource/fredoka/files/fredoka-latin-${w}-normal.woff2`).toString('base64');\\n// Assemble with an array join, not String.replace: the bundle contains $ patterns that replace would corrupt.\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, ['<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\"><script>', session, '</script>',\\n  METAS, `<title>${TITLE}</title><style>@font-face{…data:font/woff2;base64,${font(500)}…}…</style></head><body>`,\\n  '<script>', js, '</script></body></html>'].join(''));\\n730→```\\nThe spike's bundle of Three and Rapier came to 3.2 MB ✔. Each Fredoka weight adds about 16 KB. Expect about 4-6 MB at the end. `format: 'iife'` means no dynamic imports. It opens from `file://` ✔.\\n\\n**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Testing\\n\\nFollow TASK.md's rules: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun it only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n740→\\n**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\\n- Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder. Don't read the folders beside yours.\\n- Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done. \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\\n- Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`: the browsers are already here. `npm i playwright@1.63.0` finds them ✔. `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` is already set; keep it if a script spawns a browser with a clean environment. If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`; this plan's tools are all Node.\\n- Prefer `file://`: every test except `hub` opens the built file directly. WebGPU only works on a secure page, `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A server is only for the hub's harness, bound to 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page is your game before trusting a result.\\n- Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n- There is no display. A headed browser needs `xvfb-run`, and you shouldn't need one.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\\n750→\\n| Missing | Stand-in |\\n|---|---|\\n| Physical controller | Stub `navigator.getGamepads` (below) |\\n| Touch device | Playwright phone and tablet profiles (Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad), tapping with `page.touchscreen` |\\n| Someone to listen | `OfflineAudioContext` renders and `AnalyserNode` levels on the live mix |\\n| Second player | TASK.md suggests two browser pages. The hub's harness is that test, built for this: it plays 2-4 copies at once, in frames of one page, on a simulated network. Add four stubbed pads for Group Class |\\n| Real iPhone or iPad | WebKit with an iPhone profile; it isn't iOS Safari, so never require more than it shows |\\n| TV | Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080 |\\n\\n760→**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\\n\\n**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\\n```js\\nconst RENDER = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];\\n```\\n- **Do not use TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone. With them, Three's device is lost 0.2 s after startup ✔** (\\\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\\\"). `backend === 'webgpu'` stays true and the frame counter climbs at about 60 fps, but nothing is being drawn, and that is why the screenshots are black. It fooled me: I first reported that speed as real. With the two extra flags, which the hub's own `hub-check` docs also list, the device stays alive, frames are real at about 6 fps, and `page.screenshot` shows the true image ✔.\\n- **Every test that renders must assert `render.lost` is null.** \\\"Frames rising\\\" proves nothing by itself. Also read the screenshot back as pixels and check it has ink and a range of colours ✔.\\n- Logic tests don't need any of this: `?test=1&render=0` runs with no renderer at all, on plain Chromium.\\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\\n770→- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\\n- Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning is harmless. **Treat any \\\"Device Lost\\\" message as a failure.** There are none with the flags above.\\n\\n**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\\n- `state()`: JSON with `screen, attract, day, step, profile, marbles, tilt, wobbleAge, event: { id, tier, phase, t, score, lanes: [{ kind, leanX, leanZ, cob, fallen, form, props }] }, input: { lastDevice, glyphSet }, audio: { state, music, sfx, voice }, render: { backend, frames }, errors`.\\n- `advance(seconds)`: runs the fixed tick and the frame logic without rendering or waiting. All tests use it, except the ones checking real time and real frames.\\n- `bot(skill | null)`: `sharp`, `average`, `clumsy` play through the real input path: they write to the stubbed gamepad, not into the game's state.\\n- `goto(screen, args)`, `tuning`, `settings`.\\nWith `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run. With `?manual=1`, the game's own loop doesn't run at all, so only `advance()` moves time and tests never race the frame loop ✔. Add `render: { lost }` to `state()`.\\n\\n780→**Input stubs.** Replace `navigator.getGamepads` with `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`, returning `{ connected: true, mapping: 'standard', axes: [...], buttons: [{ pressed, value }, ...], vibrationActuator: { playEffect() {} } }` from `window.__pads`. Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\\n\\n**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\\n\\n| Skill | delay | gain | gain under load (stacks, log, tray, Lean Match) | noise | reaction lag | speed error (rad/s) | timing error sd |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| sharp | .16 s | 1.4 | .7 | 0 | .12 s | .3 | 28 ms |\\n| average | .26 s | 1.0 | .55 | .03 | .28 s | .8 | 55 ms |\\n| clumsy | .38 s | .8 | .45 | .07 | .5 s | 1.4 | 95 ms |\\n\\n790→**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\\n\\n**Tests to write.**\\n\\n*unit* (Node, no browser): Wobble Age mapping and bounds; marble payouts; tier unlock rules; programme table integrity (every id exists, days still work with a Tier B event removed); charts valid; tuning defaults inside min and max; dialogue ids all exist; save corrupt or old version is handled; rng repeatable; the session client hash; **the exit-interview file**, once it exists: parses, has exactly TASK.md's keys, 4, 6 and 4 feedback answers, no empty value except `series`, `usage` reading `added on import`, 3 to 6 lowercase tags, `genre` from TASK.md's list, `name` equal to the title constant; the built HTML has the metas in `name`, `content` order, the client directly after `<meta charset>`, and no `</script` inside a script. **Source greps:** no `Math.random` outside `src/core/rng.js`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`; no `alert(`, `confirm(`, `prompt(`, `window.open`, `WebSocket`, `RTCPeerConnection`; no audio file extensions or `decodeAudioData`; none of the source's names (`/wii|mii\\\\b|balance board/i`) outside the title constant and `src/data/about.js`; no compute nodes.\\n\\n*flow:* **the scripted whole-game playthrough.** From a fresh save on `?test=1&render=0`: Title → profile → tutorial → all days through the real menus with a stubbed pad and the bot in every class → ending → credits → Maintenance day → Free Play has everything. Assert days, stamps, marbles rising, tilt falling to exactly 0, music detune reaching 0. Reload mid-way and assert the save resumes. Also attract: `advance(10)` on Title starts it, it visits at least four events, a key exits it and that key isn't acted on, and one wall-clock run of 10 real seconds. Also pause and resume, restart, Dev Mode opens only from Pause.\\n\\n*events:* for each event and tier, with the sharp bot: it completes, the result is valid, no NaN in any body, bodies return to the baseline count after `dispose()`, a forced fall ragdolls and recovers. One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `RENDER` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, `render.lost` null, frames rising, no console errors. Also soak the removal paths with the doll swaying, because Rapier's panic only shows once something moves (section 4). One WebKit boot through a few events: WebGL2 backend, no errors.\\n\\n800→*balance:* run bots over 3 seeds per event at Tier 1 and assert: sharp reaches Exemplary (≥85) on average, average reaches Pleasant or Adequate, clumsy reaches at least Adequate (≥40), and a bot that parks the stick exactly on the goal and never corrects stays below Exemplary in Poses (measured ✔ 75-81 on Tier 1). Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human. **Also guard against the failures I hit,** each of which looks fine until you measure it: Plate Spinners Tier 1 must separate the bots (sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share, stick and keys alike); Tug of Peace Tier 1 must be winnable; every Tippy Raft round ends within 90 s; Lean Match scores above .3 for the sharp bot; Log Reps doesn't give the clumsy bot above 70; Room Service Tier 1 with a brace delivers all five; a breath-synced bot beats the same bot unsynced on The Cairn Tier 2.\\n\\n*input:* every verb from the pad stub, from keys, from the mouse, same game effect. Prompts switch the same frame (read `data-glyph`). Xbox glyph option. Menus fully navigable by pad alone, by keyboard alone, by mouse alone. Hover moves the focus. Clicks activate. Options ignored while Create is held. Four pads join Group Class, each controls its own lane. Tippy Raft has a winner.\\n\\n*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. On the live mix, read an `AnalyserNode` on each bus. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero. No audio files are ever fetched.\\n\\n*hub:* fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` into `tools/hub/` from the same raw folder as the client. Serve the built game beside them from an in-process `http.createServer` on `listen(0, '127.0.0.1')`, so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping. (If you ever do start a server process, stop only that one, by its PID, as TASK.md says.) First confirm the page is your game. Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `RENDER` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`, wait for `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, read `window.__hubCheck.pass` and print failing checks. Runs: `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the same with `&scenario=host-leave`. Also on its own: open the page, press Enter, Space, Enter, Space, assert no errors, `AIHub.active` false and something drawn. The harness calls time about 5 seconds into play, so `time-up` must work from any point, and a `result` with score 0 is valid.\\n\\n*ui:* at 1920×1080, walk every screen and assert: no text under 30 px in computed style, nothing outside the viewport or the 5% margin except backgrounds, at most six focusable items, a visible focus. Touch-only: Pixel 7, iPhone and iPad profiles show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings, `page.touchscreen` tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state. About shows every Source Information field and the three feedback lists, and matches the exit-interview file.\\n\\n810→**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080: title, island on Day 1 and Day 5, each event mid-play, results, jar, Age Reveal, Group Class with four dolls, Dev Mode, About. Look for lost legibility at 10 feet, sunk characters, messy ink.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Build order and cut order\\n\\nEach milestone ends with something playable. Commit to the order: Fruit Hat before menus, because if the core isn't fun nothing else matters. Keep everything working as you go.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n820→| 0 | Skeleton | Build script, single HTML, renderer with ink, sky and sea, Rapier, the loop, hooks, `test.mjs`, session client in place |\\n| 1 | The soul | Balance core, Balance Disc, doll with clips and ragdoll, input with all three devices, **Fruit Hat complete with results.** Tune until it makes you want one more go |\\n| 2 | The shell | Title, menu, Who's Here, island with tilt physics, Trudy with voice, dialogue, music engine, save, pause, settings, glyphs |\\n| 3 | The programme | Check-In, Wobble Age, Jar, Today's Plan, days. **Tier A events:** Poses, Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn, Room Service, Plate Spinners, Springboard |\\n| 4 | The features | Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, Tippy Raft, hub session, touch-only, About. Passing the hub harness |\\n| 5 | The ending | The Grand Hold, ending, credits, Maintenance days |\\n| 6 | Tier B events, in this order | Still Water, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Island Loop |\\n| 7 | Polish | Juice, more lines, art pass, full tests, exit interview, final build |\\n\\n**Cut order if you run short. Cut whole things, never stub them, and leave no trace (TASK.md: no stubs, no placeholders).** First Island Loop, then Tug of Peace, then Plank & Perch, then Still Water, then Tier 3 of each event (leaving a two-tier ladder), then Tier 2. Do not cut: Fruit Hat, Poses, Check-In, Trudy, the tilting island, the jar, the finale, Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, the hub session, glyphs, audio. The day table builds from the registry, so cutting an event needs no other change.\\n830→\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Done and reporting\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\\n\\n- [ ] Single HTML where TASK.md says, opens from `file://`, no external requests, no audio files\\n- [ ] WebGPU backend by default, WebGL2 fallback works, cel-shaded with the ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Three R180, Web Audio, Gamepad API\\n- [ ] Original cast, events, art and music; title is your own random pick; nothing from section 2's lists\\n840→- [ ] All thirteen events, or the ones left after cuts, each with tiers, an intro, results and bots\\n- [ ] Programme through the ending, Wobble Age graph, jar, unlocks, Free Play\\n- [ ] Controller-first; keyboard and mouse native on the same screens; prompts follow last device; Xbox glyph option; no dense screens\\n- [ ] Attract at 10 s, any input exits; Dev Mode from Pause with tools, live sliders and reset; touch-only message and Settings\\n- [ ] Group Class 1-4 on one screen; hub harness passes at 1, 2, 4 players, on the bad network, with the host leaving; game on its own passes Enter and Space\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default; all sound in code; gibberish voices with every word on screen\\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs full` passes; a scripted whole-game playthrough is part of it\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in `src/`\\n- [ ] About/Credits, the exit-interview file and your chat reply are all filled in as below\\n\\n850→**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build. Nothing in this plan answers them for you, and section 22 is only an example. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n\\n**The last thing you do, in this order:**\\n1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\\n2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\\n3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\\n4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\\n5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\\n\\nDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \\\"unknown\\\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\\n860→\\n**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\\n\\n| Page | Shows |\\n|---|---|\\n| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\\n| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\\n| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\\n| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\\n| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n870→| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\\n| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\\n\\nUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\\n\\n```\\n## Source Information\\n880→- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\\n## Testing Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n## Engine Feedback\\n1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\\n## Prompt Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n```\\n\\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\\n890→\\n| Field | What to write |\\n|---|---|\\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\\n| Source | Wii Fit |\\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\\n900→| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\\n| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\\n\\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\\n910→2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n920→6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\\n2. What was confusing?\\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\\n\\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\\n\\n930→| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\\n|---|---|\\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\\n| `type` | Game |\\n| `genre` | Sports |\\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\\n| `players` | 1–4 |\\n940→| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\\n\\n**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\\n\\n**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\\n950→\\n**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\\n\\n**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \\\"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\\\", and these are as far as it goes:**\\n- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\\n- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\\n- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\\n- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\\n\\n---\\n960→\\n## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\\n\\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in | Proven at build time by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep; section 2, what we don't copy | `unit`: source's names absent outside the title and About. Your art pass |\\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 | Your report, section 19 |\\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 | Art pass screenshots |\\n970→| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 | You run the generator; one constant in `src/data/config.js` |\\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row | Your report |\\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build | `unit`: inline scripts only. `flow`: opens from `file://` |\\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack | `flow`: `THREE.REVISION` is 180 ✔ |\\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer | `events`: `backend` is webgpu and `render.lost` is null ✔ |\\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9 | `events`: ink pixels in a read-back screenshot ✔. Art pass |\\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 | `flow`: `RAPIER.version()` ✔ |\\n| Web Audio | Section 11 | `audio`: live bus and offline renders ✔ |\\n| Gamepad API | Section 3 | `input`: every verb through the stubbed pad ✔ |\\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka | Your report |\\n980→| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes | Art pass |\\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 | `events`: the mixer's time advances and clips change ✔ |\\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11 | `unit`: greps. `audio`: every track, sting, effect and voice renders non-silent |\\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices | `audio`: blips per line, peaks balanced |\\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11 | `audio`: setting 10, bus gain .10 ✔ |\\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 | `hub` ✔ |\\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build | The build writes where TASK.md says; `unit` opens it |\\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined | `unit`: no external scripts |\\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer; `hub` test | `hub`: in-process server on port 0, page confirmed first ✔ |\\n990→| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer | Nothing to prove: no FPS test exists on purpose |\\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test | `flow` reaches the ending and beyond |\\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command | Its own output ✔ |\\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass | Your practice |\\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph | Your practice |\\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours | Your report |\\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags | `events`: `render.lost` null ✔ |\\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags | `events`: WebKit boot ✔ |\\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \\\"never required\\\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\\n1000→| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 | `unit` greps; your art pass and judgement |\\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 | `flow`: every day and event, in order |\\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 | `balance` for mechanics; your judgement for tone |\\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 | `unit`: table integrity; a removed event still leaves a working programme |\\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 | `flow`: only reachable from Pause ✔ |\\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 | `flow`: a slider changes the running event, reset restores it ✔ |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 | `flow`: starts near 10 wall-clock seconds, loops, each device exits it and the press is swallowed ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session | `hub`: harness at 1, 2, 4 players, bad network, host leaving ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own | `input`: four pads, four lanes ✔. `hub`: Enter and Space on its own ✔ |\\n1010→| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 | `unit`: lines exist, no repeats. Your judgement |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 | `ui` at 1080p ✔. `input`: all menus by pad alone ✔ |\\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 | `input`: same effects from keys and mouse ✔. `balance` on keys |\\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 | `input`: pointer weight, hover, click ✔; a parked mouse doesn't steal focus ✔ |\\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3 | `input`: `data-glyph` changes in the same tick ✔ |\\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules | `ui`: 30 px minimum, six choices, safe area, on every screen ✔ |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 | `ui`: Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad ✔ |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19 | `unit`: greps ✔ |\\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\\n1020→| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\\n\\n✔ here means the check was run on the integration prototype of section 20.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 22. Planning-stage report\\n\\nTASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\\n\\n1030→**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\\n\\n**Testing Feedback**\\n1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\\n2. *How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?* 13: ground contact capping lean; a parked stick scoring 100%; Plate Spinners unlosable; Plate Spinners unreachable on keys; Tug of Peace unwinnable; Tippy Raft never ending; Lean Match impossible; the fall loop after a ragdoll; silent controller play; both mouse buttons never registering together; Rapier's page-killing panic; tests \\\"passing\\\" on a lost WebGPU device; a boot snippet that wouldn't build.\\n3. *What was the most helpful test you ran?* Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes. It found the lost device, the parked mouse, the mouse-button chord, silent pad play and the fall loop, none of which any separate check had shown.\\n4. *What was the least helpful test you ran?* The Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\\n\\n**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\\n1. *What did you like most about the engine?* `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n1040→2. *Did you get stuck or guess wrong?* Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps. `hash()` given a vec2 streaked the image, toon glass turned smoky, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\\n3. *What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?* The balance core: a torque-limited controller with gravity feed-forward on a ball joint.\\n4. *What was the hardest part about using the engine?* Knowing what was really happening. A lost WebGPU device gives no error and a loop that seems to run. A Rapier crash surfaces half a second after its cause, only once something moves.\\n5. *What did you change in the engine, and why?* None.\",\"content_concise\":\"644→## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\\n650→---\\n\\n## 14. Dev mode\\n\\nFrom the Pause menu. It follows the same 10-foot rules: pages of big controls by group, six to a page, usable by pad, keyboard and mouse. Dev Mode is off in a session.\\n\\n**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\\n\\n**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\\n- *Balance:* maxLean, margin, zeta, ankleMax, fall angle, reflex, flail boost, flail cooldown, sway scale, tremor scale, recover time.\\n660→- *World:* gravity, island weight (° at rim), island rest tilt, island spring, walk speed.\\n- *Events:* fruit mass, fruit interval, landing radius, stone mass, stone interval, ring size at end, gust force, plate decay, plate gain, rhythm window, rep tempo, board gain, air torque.\\n- *Rewards:* marbles per minute, medal scale.\\n- *Ink:* hull, line width, boil, grain.\\n\\n**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Touch-only devices\\n670→\\n**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\\n\\nShow the title with \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. There are no touch controls anywhere else. Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual, and a tap returns to this screen. If a pad connects or a key is pressed, drop this state and carry on normally. In a session the card's Ready and More time accept taps, per the hub's rules.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. Architecture and build\\n\\n**Where things go.** Keep all your dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names (your dev folder). Develop separate modules there, and bundle them into the single HTML file in the root, named as TASK.md says, when you're done. TASK.md allows external CDN dependencies; this plan inlines everything instead, so the file works offline and inside the hub.\\n680→\\n**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\\n\\n```\\npackage.json\\nvendor/session.js\\ntools/build.mjs  tools/test.mjs  tools/shots.mjs  tools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}\\nsrc/main.js\\nsrc/core/      loop.js  rng.js  save.js  script.js  tuning.js\\nsrc/input/     input.js  glyphs.js  bot.js\\n690→src/render/    renderer.js  ink.js  palette.js  sky.js  sea.js  cameraRig.js  particles.js  comicText.js\\nsrc/art/       materials.js  doll.js  trudy.js  heron.js  walrus.js  gull.js  island.js  props.js\\nsrc/anim/      clips.js  rig.js\\nsrc/physics/   world.js  balancer.js  ragdoll.js  island.js\\nsrc/audio/     engine.js  instruments.js  tracks.js  sequencer.js  voice.js  sfx.js\\nsrc/ui/        ui.css  focus.js  hud.js  disc.js  speech.js  screens/*.js\\nsrc/game/      director.js  programme.js  checkin.js  jar.js  hub.js  profiles.js  results.js\\n               attract.js  devmode.js  groupclass.js  session.js  ghost.js  ending.js\\nsrc/events/    base.js  poses.js  cairn.js  stillWater.js  logReps.js  plank.js  tug.js  stepClass.js\\n               plates.js  islandLoop.js  fruitHat.js  roomService.js  springboard.js  raft.js  grandHold.js\\n700→src/data/      config.js  events.js  programme.js  poses.js  charts.js  dialogue.js  tuning.js\\n               palettes.js  unlocks.js  names.js  about.js\\ntest/*.mjs\\n```\\n\\n**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\\n\\n**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\\n\\n**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\\n710→\\n**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\\n\\n**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\\n\\n```js\\n// tools/build.mjs (core; OUT is the file name and location TASK.md gives you)\\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js', 'utf8').replace(/\\\\r\\\\n/g, '\\\\n');\\nconst END = \\\"})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\\\";\\n720→const hash = createHash('sha256').update(session.slice(session.indexOf('/*! aihub-session '), session.indexOf(END) + END.length)).digest('hex');\\nif (hash !== '529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a') throw new Error('not the released hub client');\\nconst r = await build({ entryPoints: ['src/main.js'], bundle: true, format: 'iife', target: 'es2022', minify: true,\\n  write: false, legalComments: 'none', loader: { '.css': 'text' } });\\nconst js = r.outputFiles[0].text.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi, '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\nconst font = (w) => readFileSync(`node_modules/@fontsource/fredoka/files/fredoka-latin-${w}-normal.woff2`).toString('base64');\\n// Assemble with an array join, not String.replace: the bundle contains $ patterns that replace would corrupt.\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, ['<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\"><script>', session, '</script>',\\n  METAS, `<title>${TITLE}</title><style>@font-face{…data:font/woff2;base64,${font(500)}…}…</style></head><body>`,\\n  '<script>', js, '</script></body></html>'].join(''));\\n730→```\\nThe spike's bundle of Three and Rapier came to 3.2 MB ✔. Each Fredoka weight adds about 16 KB. Expect about 4-6 MB at the end. `format: 'iife'` means no dynamic imports. It opens from `file://` ✔.\\n\\n**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Testing\\n\\nFollow TASK.md's rules: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun it only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n740→\\n**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\\n- Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder. Don't read the folders beside yours.\\n- Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done. \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\\n- Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`: the browsers are already here. `npm i playwright@1.63.0` finds them ✔. `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` is already set; keep it if a script spawns a browser with a clean environment. If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`; this plan's tools are all Node.\\n- Prefer `file://`: every test except `hub` opens the built file directly. WebGPU only works on a secure page, `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A server is only for the hub's harness, bound to 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page is your game before trusting a result.\\n- Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n- There is no display. A headed browser needs `xvfb-run`, and you shouldn't need one.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\\n750→\\n| Missing | Stand-in |\\n|---|---|\\n| Physical controller | Stub `navigator.getGamepads` (below) |\\n| Touch device | Playwright phone and tablet profiles (Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad), tapping with `page.touchscreen` |\\n| Someone to listen | `OfflineAudioContext` renders and `AnalyserNode` levels on the live mix |\\n| Second player | TASK.md suggests two browser pages. The hub's harness is that test, built for this: it plays 2-4 copies at once, in frames of one page, on a simulated network. Add four stubbed pads for Group Class |\\n| Real iPhone or iPad | WebKit with an iPhone profile; it isn't iOS Safari, so never require more than it shows |\\n| TV | Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080 |\\n\\n760→**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\\n\\n**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\\n```js\\nconst RENDER = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];\\n```\\n- **Do not use TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone. With them, Three's device is lost 0.2 s after startup ✔** (\\\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\\\"). `backend === 'webgpu'` stays true and the frame counter climbs at about 60 fps, but nothing is being drawn, and that is why the screenshots are black. It fooled me: I first reported that speed as real. With the two extra flags, which the hub's own `hub-check` docs also list, the device stays alive, frames are real at about 6 fps, and `page.screenshot` shows the true image ✔.\\n- **Every test that renders must assert `render.lost` is null.** \\\"Frames rising\\\" proves nothing by itself. Also read the screenshot back as pixels and check it has ink and a range of colours ✔.\\n- Logic tests don't need any of this: `?test=1&render=0` runs with no renderer at all, on plain Chromium.\\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\\n770→- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\\n- Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning is harmless. **Treat any \\\"Device Lost\\\" message as a failure.** There are none with the flags above.\\n\\n**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\\n- `state()`: JSON with `screen, attract, day, step, profile, marbles, tilt, wobbleAge, event: { id, tier, phase, t, score, lanes: [{ kind, leanX, leanZ, cob, fallen, form, props }] }, input: { lastDevice, glyphSet }, audio: { state, music, sfx, voice }, render: { backend, frames }, errors`.\\n- `advance(seconds)`: runs the fixed tick and the frame logic without rendering or waiting. All tests use it, except the ones checking real time and real frames.\\n- `bot(skill | null)`: `sharp`, `average`, `clumsy` play through the real input path: they write to the stubbed gamepad, not into the game's state.\\n- `goto(screen, args)`, `tuning`, `settings`.\\nWith `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run. With `?manual=1`, the game's own loop doesn't run at all, so only `advance()` moves time and tests never race the frame loop ✔. Add `render: { lost }` to `state()`.\\n\\n780→**Input stubs.** Replace `navigator.getGamepads` with `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`, returning `{ connected: true, mapping: 'standard', axes: [...], buttons: [{ pressed, value }, ...], vibrationActuator: { playEffect() {} } }` from `window.__pads`. Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\\n\\n**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\\n\\n| Skill | delay | gain | gain under load (stacks, log, tray, Lean Match) | noise | reaction lag | speed error (rad/s) | timing error sd |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| sharp | .16 s | 1.4 | .7 | 0 | .12 s | .3 | 28 ms |\\n| average | .26 s | 1.0 | .55 | .03 | .28 s | .8 | 55 ms |\\n| clumsy | .38 s | .8 | .45 | .07 | .5 s | 1.4 | 95 ms |\\n\\n790→**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\\n\\n**Tests to write.**\\n\\n*unit* (Node, no browser): Wobble Age mapping and bounds; marble payouts; tier unlock rules; programme table integrity (every id exists, days still work with a Tier B event removed); charts valid; tuning defaults inside min and max; dialogue ids all exist; save corrupt or old version is handled; rng repeatable; the session client hash; **the exit-interview file**, once it exists: parses, has exactly TASK.md's keys, 4, 6 and 4 feedback answers, no empty value except `series`, `usage` reading `added on import`, 3 to 6 lowercase tags, `genre` from TASK.md's list, `name` equal to the title constant; the built HTML has the metas in `name`, `content` order, the client directly after `<meta charset>`, and no `</script` inside a script. **Source greps:** no `Math.random` outside `src/core/rng.js`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`; no `alert(`, `confirm(`, `prompt(`, `window.open`, `WebSocket`, `RTCPeerConnection`; no audio file extensions or `decodeAudioData`; none of the source's names (`/wii|mii\\\\b|balance board/i`) outside the title constant and `src/data/about.js`; no compute nodes.\\n\\n*flow:* **the scripted whole-game playthrough.** From a fresh save on `?test=1&render=0`: Title → profile → tutorial → all days through the real menus with a stubbed pad and the bot in every class → ending → credits → Maintenance day → Free Play has everything. Assert days, stamps, marbles rising, tilt falling to exactly 0, music detune reaching 0. Reload mid-way and assert the save resumes. Also attract: `advance(10)` on Title starts it, it visits at least four events, a key exits it and that key isn't acted on, and one wall-clock run of 10 real seconds. Also pause and resume, restart, Dev Mode opens only from Pause.\\n\\n*events:* for each event and tier, with the sharp bot: it completes, the result is valid, no NaN in any body, bodies return to the baseline count after `dispose()`, a forced fall ragdolls and recovers. One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `RENDER` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, `render.lost` null, frames rising, no console errors. Also soak the removal paths with the doll swaying, because Rapier's panic only shows once something moves (section 4). One WebKit boot through a few events: WebGL2 backend, no errors.\\n\\n800→*balance:* run bots over 3 seeds per event at Tier 1 and assert: sharp reaches Exemplary (≥85) on average, average reaches Pleasant or Adequate, clumsy reaches at least Adequate (≥40), and a bot that parks the stick exactly on the goal and never corrects stays below Exemplary in Poses (measured ✔ 75-81 on Tier 1). Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human. **Also guard against the failures I hit,** each of which looks fine until you measure it: Plate Spinners Tier 1 must separate the bots (sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share, stick and keys alike); Tug of Peace Tier 1 must be winnable; every Tippy Raft round ends within 90 s; Lean Match scores above .3 for the sharp bot; Log Reps doesn't give the clumsy bot above 70; Room Service Tier 1 with a brace delivers all five; a breath-synced bot beats the same bot unsynced on The Cairn Tier 2.\\n\\n*input:* every verb from the pad stub, from keys, from the mouse, same game effect. Prompts switch the same frame (read `data-glyph`). Xbox glyph option. Menus fully navigable by pad alone, by keyboard alone, by mouse alone. Hover moves the focus. Clicks activate. Options ignored while Create is held. Four pads join Group Class, each controls its own lane. Tippy Raft has a winner.\\n\\n*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. On the live mix, read an `AnalyserNode` on each bus. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero. No audio files are ever fetched.\\n\\n*hub:* fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` into `tools/hub/` from the same raw folder as the client. Serve the built game beside them from an in-process `http.createServer` on `listen(0, '127.0.0.1')`, so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping. (If you ever do start a server process, stop only that one, by its PID, as TASK.md says.) First confirm the page is your game. Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `RENDER` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`, wait for `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, read `window.__hubCheck.pass` and print failing checks. Runs: `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the same with `&scenario=host-leave`. Also on its own: open the page, press Enter, Space, Enter, Space, assert no errors, `AIHub.active` false and something drawn. The harness calls time about 5 seconds into play, so `time-up` must work from any point, and a `result` with score 0 is valid.\\n\\n*ui:* at 1920×1080, walk every screen and assert: no text under 30 px in computed style, nothing outside the viewport or the 5% margin except backgrounds, at most six focusable items, a visible focus. Touch-only: Pixel 7, iPhone and iPad profiles show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings, `page.touchscreen` tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state. About shows every Source Information field and the three feedback lists, and matches the exit-interview file.\\n\\n810→**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080: title, island on Day 1 and Day 5, each event mid-play, results, jar, Age Reveal, Group Class with four dolls, Dev Mode, About. Look for lost legibility at 10 feet, sunk characters, messy ink.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Build order and cut order\\n\\nEach milestone ends with something playable. Commit to the order: Fruit Hat before menus, because if the core isn't fun nothing else matters. Keep everything working as you go.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n820→| 0 | Skeleton | Build script, single HTML, renderer with ink, sky and sea, Rapier, the loop, hooks, `test.mjs`, session client in place |\\n| 1 | The soul | Balance core, Balance Disc, doll with clips and ragdoll, input with all three devices, **Fruit Hat complete with results.** Tune until it makes you want one more go |\\n| 2 | The shell | Title, menu, Who's Here, island with tilt physics, Trudy with voice, dialogue, music engine, save, pause, settings, glyphs |\\n| 3 | The programme | Check-In, Wobble Age, Jar, Today's Plan, days. **Tier A events:** Poses, Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn, Room Service, Plate Spinners, Springboard |\\n| 4 | The features | Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, Tippy Raft, hub session, touch-only, About. Passing the hub harness |\\n| 5 | The ending | The Grand Hold, ending, credits, Maintenance days |\\n| 6 | Tier B events, in this order | Still Water, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Island Loop |\\n| 7 | Polish | Juice, more lines, art pass, full tests, exit interview, final build |\\n\\n**Cut order if you run short. Cut whole things, never stub them, and leave no trace (TASK.md: no stubs, no placeholders).** First Island Loop, then Tug of Peace, then Plank & Perch, then Still Water, then Tier 3 of each event (leaving a two-tier ladder), then Tier 2. Do not cut: Fruit Hat, Poses, Check-In, Trudy, the tilting island, the jar, the finale, Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, the hub session, glyphs, audio. The day table builds from the registry, so cutting an event needs no other change.\\n830→\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Done and reporting\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\\n\\n- [ ] Single HTML where TASK.md says, opens from `file://`, no external requests, no audio files\\n- [ ] WebGPU backend by default, WebGL2 fallback works, cel-shaded with the ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Three R180, Web Audio, Gamepad API\\n- [ ] Original cast, events, art and music; title is your own random pick; nothing from section 2's lists\\n840→- [ ] All thirteen events, or the ones left after cuts, each with tiers, an intro, results and bots\\n- [ ] Programme through the ending, Wobble Age graph, jar, unlocks, Free Play\\n- [ ] Controller-first; keyboard and mouse native on the same screens; prompts follow last device; Xbox glyph option; no dense screens\\n- [ ] Attract at 10 s, any input exits; Dev Mode from Pause with tools, live sliders and reset; touch-only message and Settings\\n- [ ] Group Class 1-4 on one screen; hub harness passes at 1, 2, 4 players, on the bad network, with the host leaving; game on its own passes Enter and Space\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default; all sound in code; gibberish voices with every word on screen\\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs full` passes; a scripted whole-game playthrough is part of it\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in `src/`\\n- [ ] About/Credits, the exit-interview file and your chat reply are all filled in as below\\n\\n850→**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build. Nothing in this plan answers them for you, and section 22 is only an example. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n\\n**The last thing you do, in this order:**\\n1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\\n2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\\n3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\\n4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\\n5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\\n\\nDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \\\"unknown\\\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\\n860→\\n**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\\n\\n| Page | Shows |\\n|---|---|\\n| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\\n| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\\n| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\\n| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\\n| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n870→| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\\n| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\\n\\nUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\\n\\n```\\n## Source Information\\n880→- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\\n## Testing Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n## Engine Feedback\\n1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\\n## Prompt Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n```\\n\\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\\n890→\\n| Field | What to write |\\n|---|---|\\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\\n| Source | Wii Fit |\\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\\n900→| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\\n| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\\n\\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\\n910→2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n920→6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\\n2. What was confusing?\\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\\n\\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\\n\\n930→| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\\n|---|---|\\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\\n| `type` | Game |\\n| `genre` | Sports |\\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\\n| `players` | 1–4 |\\n940→| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\\n\\n**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\\n\\n**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\\n950→\\n**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\\n\\n**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \\\"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\\\", and these are as far as it goes:**\\n- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\\n- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\\n- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\\n- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\\n\\n---\\n960→\\n## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\\n\\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in | Proven at build time by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep; section 2, what we don't copy | `unit`: source's names absent outside the title and About. Your art pass |\\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 | Your report, section 19 |\\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 | Art pass screenshots |\\n970→| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 | You run the generator; one constant in `src/data/config.js` |\\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row | Your report |\\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build | `unit`: inline scripts only. `flow`: opens from `file://` |\\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack | `flow`: `THREE.REVISION` is 180 ✔ |\\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer | `events`: `backend` is webgpu and `render.lost` is null ✔ |\\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9 | `events`: ink pixels in a read-back screenshot ✔. Art pass |\\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 | `flow`: `RAPIER.version()` ✔ |\\n| Web Audio | Section 11 | `audio`: live bus and offline renders ✔ |\\n| Gamepad API | Section 3 | `input`: every verb through the stubbed pad ✔ |\\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka | Your report |\\n980→| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes | Art pass |\\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 | `events`: the mixer's time advances and clips change ✔ |\\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11 | `unit`: greps. `audio`: every track, sting, effect and voice renders non-silent |\\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices | `audio`: blips per line, peaks balanced |\\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11 | `audio`: setting 10, bus gain .10 ✔ |\\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 | `hub` ✔ |\\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build | The build writes where TASK.md says; `unit` opens it |\\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined | `unit`: no external scripts |\\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer; `hub` test | `hub`: in-process server on port 0, page confirmed first ✔ |\\n990→| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer | Nothing to prove: no FPS test exists on purpose |\\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test | `flow` reaches the ending and beyond |\\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command | Its own output ✔ |\\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass | Your practice |\\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph | Your practice |\\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours | Your report |\\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags | `events`: `render.lost` null ✔ |\\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags | `events`: WebKit boot ✔ |\\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \\\"never required\\\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\\n1000→| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 | `unit` greps; your art pass and judgement |\\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 | `flow`: every day and event, in order |\\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 | `balance` for mechanics; your judgement for tone |\\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 | `unit`: table integrity; a removed event still leaves a working programme |\\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 | `flow`: only reachable from Pause ✔ |\\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 | `flow`: a slider changes the running event, reset restores it ✔ |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 | `flow`: starts near 10 wall-clock seconds, loops, each device exits it and the press is swallowed ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session | `hub`: harness at 1, 2, 4 players, bad network, host leaving ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own | `input`: four pads, four lanes ✔. `hub`: Enter and Space on its own ✔ |\\n1010→| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 | `unit`: lines exist, no repeats. Your judgement |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 | `ui` at 1080p ✔. `input`: all menus by pad alone ✔ |\\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 | `input`: same effects from keys and mouse ✔. `balance` on keys |\\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 | `input`: pointer weight, hover, click ✔; a parked mouse doesn't steal focus ✔ |\\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3 | `input`: `data-glyph` changes in the same tick ✔ |\\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules | `ui`: 30 px minimum, six choices, safe area, on every screen ✔ |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 | `ui`: Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad ✔ |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19 | `unit`: greps ✔ |\\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\\n1020→| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\\n\\n✔ here means the check was run on the integration prototype of section 20.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 22. Planning-stage report\\n\\nTASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\\n\\n1030→**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\\n\\n**Testing Feedback**\\n1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\\n2. *How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?* 13: ground contact capping lean; a parked stick scoring 100%; Plate Spinners unlosable; Plate Spinners unreachable on keys; Tug of Peace unwinnable; Tippy Raft never ending; Lean Match impossible; the fall loop after a ragdoll; silent controller play; both mouse buttons never registering together; Rapier's page-killing panic; tests \\\"passing\\\" on a lost WebGPU device; a boot snippet that wouldn't build.\\n3. *What was the most helpful test you ran?* Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes. It found the lost device, the parked mouse, the mouse-button chord, silent pad play and the fall loop, none of which any separate check had shown.\\n4. *What was the least helpful test you ran?* The Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\\n\\n**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\\n1. *What did you like most about the engine?* `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n1040→2. *Did you get stuck or guess wrong?* Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps. `hash()` given a vec2 streaked the image, toon glass turned smoky, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\\n3. *What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?* The balance core: a torque-limited controller with gravity feed-forward on a ball joint.\\n4. *What was the hardest part about using the engine?* Knowing what was really happening. A lost WebGPU device gives no error and a loop that seems to run. A Rapier crash surfaces half a second after its cause, only once something moves.\\n5. *What did you change in the engine, and why?* None.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":644,\"limit\":400,\"raw_output\":\"## 13. Attract mode\\n\\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \\\"DEMO · Press any button\\\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\\n\\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Dev mode\\n\\nFrom the Pause menu. It follows the same 10-foot rules: pages of big controls by group, six to a page, usable by pad, keyboard and mouse. Dev Mode is off in a session.\\n\\n**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\\n\\n**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\\n- *Balance:* maxLean, margin, zeta, ankleMax, fall angle, reflex, flail boost, flail cooldown, sway scale, tremor scale, recover time.\\n- *World:* gravity, island weight (° at rim), island rest tilt, island spring, walk speed.\\n- *Events:* fruit mass, fruit interval, landing radius, stone mass, stone interval, ring size at end, gust force, plate decay, plate gain, rhythm window, rep tempo, board gain, air torque.\\n- *Rewards:* marbles per minute, medal scale.\\n- *Ink:* hull, line width, boil, grain.\\n\\n**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Touch-only devices\\n\\n**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\\n\\nShow the title with \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. There are no touch controls anywhere else. Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual, and a tap returns to this screen. If a pad connects or a key is pressed, drop this state and carry on normally. In a session the card's Ready and More time accept taps, per the hub's rules.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. Architecture and build\\n\\n**Where things go.** Keep all your dev files in the subfolder TASK.md names (your dev folder). Develop separate modules there, and bundle them into the single HTML file in the root, named as TASK.md says, when you're done. TASK.md allows external CDN dependencies; this plan inlines everything instead, so the file works offline and inside the hub.\\n\\n**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\\n\\n```\\npackage.json\\nvendor/session.js\\ntools/build.mjs  tools/test.mjs  tools/shots.mjs  tools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}\\nsrc/main.js\\nsrc/core/      loop.js  rng.js  save.js  script.js  tuning.js\\nsrc/input/     input.js  glyphs.js  bot.js\\nsrc/render/    renderer.js  ink.js  palette.js  sky.js  sea.js  cameraRig.js  particles.js  comicText.js\\nsrc/art/       materials.js  doll.js  trudy.js  heron.js  walrus.js  gull.js  island.js  props.js\\nsrc/anim/      clips.js  rig.js\\nsrc/physics/   world.js  balancer.js  ragdoll.js  island.js\\nsrc/audio/     engine.js  instruments.js  tracks.js  sequencer.js  voice.js  sfx.js\\nsrc/ui/        ui.css  focus.js  hud.js  disc.js  speech.js  screens/*.js\\nsrc/game/      director.js  programme.js  checkin.js  jar.js  hub.js  profiles.js  results.js\\n               attract.js  devmode.js  groupclass.js  session.js  ghost.js  ending.js\\nsrc/events/    base.js  poses.js  cairn.js  stillWater.js  logReps.js  plank.js  tug.js  stepClass.js\\n               plates.js  islandLoop.js  fruitHat.js  roomService.js  springboard.js  raft.js  grandHold.js\\nsrc/data/      config.js  events.js  programme.js  poses.js  charts.js  dialogue.js  tuning.js\\n               palettes.js  unlocks.js  names.js  about.js\\ntest/*.mjs\\n```\\n\\n**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\\n\\n**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\\n\\n**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\\n\\n**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\\n\\n**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\\n\\n```js\\n// tools/build.mjs (core; OUT is the file name and location TASK.md gives you)\\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js', 'utf8').replace(/\\\\r\\\\n/g, '\\\\n');\\nconst END = \\\"})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\\\";\\nconst hash = createHash('sha256').update(session.slice(session.indexOf('/*! aihub-session '), session.indexOf(END) + END.length)).digest('hex');\\nif (hash !== '529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a') throw new Error('not the released hub client');\\nconst r = await build({ entryPoints: ['src/main.js'], bundle: true, format: 'iife', target: 'es2022', minify: true,\\n  write: false, legalComments: 'none', loader: { '.css': 'text' } });\\nconst js = r.outputFiles[0].text.replace(/<\\\\/script/gi, '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\nconst font = (w) => readFileSync(`node_modules/@fontsource/fredoka/files/fredoka-latin-${w}-normal.woff2`).toString('base64');\\n// Assemble with an array join, not String.replace: the bundle contains $ patterns that replace would corrupt.\\nwriteFileSync(OUT, ['<!doctype html><html lang=\\\"en\\\"><head><meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\"><script>', session, '</script>',\\n  METAS, `<title>${TITLE}</title><style>@font-face{…data:font/woff2;base64,${font(500)}…}…</style></head><body>`,\\n  '<script>', js, '</script></body></html>'].join(''));\\n```\\nThe spike's bundle of Three and Rapier came to 3.2 MB ✔. Each Fredoka weight adds about 16 KB. Expect about 4-6 MB at the end. `format: 'iife'` means no dynamic imports. It opens from `file://` ✔.\\n\\n**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Testing\\n\\nFollow TASK.md's rules: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun it only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\\n- Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder. Don't read the folders beside yours.\\n- Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done. \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\\n- Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`: the browsers are already here. `npm i playwright@1.63.0` finds them ✔. `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` is already set; keep it if a script spawns a browser with a clean environment. If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`; this plan's tools are all Node.\\n- Prefer `file://`: every test except `hub` opens the built file directly. WebGPU only works on a secure page, `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A server is only for the hub's harness, bound to 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page is your game before trusting a result.\\n- Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n- There is no display. A headed browser needs `xvfb-run`, and you shouldn't need one.\\n\\n**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\\n\\n| Missing | Stand-in |\\n|---|---|\\n| Physical controller | Stub `navigator.getGamepads` (below) |\\n| Touch device | Playwright phone and tablet profiles (Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad), tapping with `page.touchscreen` |\\n| Someone to listen | `OfflineAudioContext` renders and `AnalyserNode` levels on the live mix |\\n| Second player | TASK.md suggests two browser pages. The hub's harness is that test, built for this: it plays 2-4 copies at once, in frames of one page, on a simulated network. Add four stubbed pads for Group Class |\\n| Real iPhone or iPad | WebKit with an iPhone profile; it isn't iOS Safari, so never require more than it shows |\\n| TV | Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080 |\\n\\n**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\\n\\n**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\\n```js\\nconst RENDER = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];\\n```\\n- **Do not use TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone. With them, Three's device is lost 0.2 s after startup ✔** (\\\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\\\"). `backend === 'webgpu'` stays true and the frame counter climbs at about 60 fps, but nothing is being drawn, and that is why the screenshots are black. It fooled me: I first reported that speed as real. With the two extra flags, which the hub's own `hub-check` docs also list, the device stays alive, frames are real at about 6 fps, and `page.screenshot` shows the true image ✔.\\n- **Every test that renders must assert `render.lost` is null.** \\\"Frames rising\\\" proves nothing by itself. Also read the screenshot back as pixels and check it has ink and a range of colours ✔.\\n- Logic tests don't need any of this: `?test=1&render=0` runs with no renderer at all, on plain Chromium.\\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\\n- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\\n- Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning is harmless. **Treat any \\\"Device Lost\\\" message as a failure.** There are none with the flags above.\\n\\n**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\\n- `state()`: JSON with `screen, attract, day, step, profile, marbles, tilt, wobbleAge, event: { id, tier, phase, t, score, lanes: [{ kind, leanX, leanZ, cob, fallen, form, props }] }, input: { lastDevice, glyphSet }, audio: { state, music, sfx, voice }, render: { backend, frames }, errors`.\\n- `advance(seconds)`: runs the fixed tick and the frame logic without rendering or waiting. All tests use it, except the ones checking real time and real frames.\\n- `bot(skill | null)`: `sharp`, `average`, `clumsy` play through the real input path: they write to the stubbed gamepad, not into the game's state.\\n- `goto(screen, args)`, `tuning`, `settings`.\\nWith `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run. With `?manual=1`, the game's own loop doesn't run at all, so only `advance()` moves time and tests never race the frame loop ✔. Add `render: { lost }` to `state()`.\\n\\n**Input stubs.** Replace `navigator.getGamepads` with `add_init_script`/`addInitScript`, returning `{ connected: true, mapping: 'standard', axes: [...], buttons: [{ pressed, value }, ...], vibrationActuator: { playEffect() {} } }` from `window.__pads`. Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\\n\\n**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\\n\\n| Skill | delay | gain | gain under load (stacks, log, tray, Lean Match) | noise | reaction lag | speed error (rad/s) | timing error sd |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| sharp | .16 s | 1.4 | .7 | 0 | .12 s | .3 | 28 ms |\\n| average | .26 s | 1.0 | .55 | .03 | .28 s | .8 | 55 ms |\\n| clumsy | .38 s | .8 | .45 | .07 | .5 s | 1.4 | 95 ms |\\n\\n**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\\n\\n**Tests to write.**\\n\\n*unit* (Node, no browser): Wobble Age mapping and bounds; marble payouts; tier unlock rules; programme table integrity (every id exists, days still work with a Tier B event removed); charts valid; tuning defaults inside min and max; dialogue ids all exist; save corrupt or old version is handled; rng repeatable; the session client hash; **the exit-interview file**, once it exists: parses, has exactly TASK.md's keys, 4, 6 and 4 feedback answers, no empty value except `series`, `usage` reading `added on import`, 3 to 6 lowercase tags, `genre` from TASK.md's list, `name` equal to the title constant; the built HTML has the metas in `name`, `content` order, the client directly after `<meta charset>`, and no `</script` inside a script. **Source greps:** no `Math.random` outside `src/core/rng.js`; no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`; no `alert(`, `confirm(`, `prompt(`, `window.open`, `WebSocket`, `RTCPeerConnection`; no audio file extensions or `decodeAudioData`; none of the source's names (`/wii|mii\\\\b|balance board/i`) outside the title constant and `src/data/about.js`; no compute nodes.\\n\\n*flow:* **the scripted whole-game playthrough.** From a fresh save on `?test=1&render=0`: Title → profile → tutorial → all days through the real menus with a stubbed pad and the bot in every class → ending → credits → Maintenance day → Free Play has everything. Assert days, stamps, marbles rising, tilt falling to exactly 0, music detune reaching 0. Reload mid-way and assert the save resumes. Also attract: `advance(10)` on Title starts it, it visits at least four events, a key exits it and that key isn't acted on, and one wall-clock run of 10 real seconds. Also pause and resume, restart, Dev Mode opens only from Pause.\\n\\n*events:* for each event and tier, with the sharp bot: it completes, the result is valid, no NaN in any body, bodies return to the baseline count after `dispose()`, a forced fall ragdolls and recovers. One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `RENDER` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, `render.lost` null, frames rising, no console errors. Also soak the removal paths with the doll swaying, because Rapier's panic only shows once something moves (section 4). One WebKit boot through a few events: WebGL2 backend, no errors.\\n\\n*balance:* run bots over 3 seeds per event at Tier 1 and assert: sharp reaches Exemplary (≥85) on average, average reaches Pleasant or Adequate, clumsy reaches at least Adequate (≥40), and a bot that parks the stick exactly on the goal and never corrects stays below Exemplary in Poses (measured ✔ 75-81 on Tier 1). Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human. **Also guard against the failures I hit,** each of which looks fine until you measure it: Plate Spinners Tier 1 must separate the bots (sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share, stick and keys alike); Tug of Peace Tier 1 must be winnable; every Tippy Raft round ends within 90 s; Lean Match scores above .3 for the sharp bot; Log Reps doesn't give the clumsy bot above 70; Room Service Tier 1 with a brace delivers all five; a breath-synced bot beats the same bot unsynced on The Cairn Tier 2.\\n\\n*input:* every verb from the pad stub, from keys, from the mouse, same game effect. Prompts switch the same frame (read `data-glyph`). Xbox glyph option. Menus fully navigable by pad alone, by keyboard alone, by mouse alone. Hover moves the focus. Clicks activate. Options ignored while Create is held. Four pads join Group Class, each controls its own lane. Tippy Raft has a winner.\\n\\n*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. On the live mix, read an `AnalyserNode` on each bus. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero. No audio files are ever fetched.\\n\\n*hub:* fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` into `tools/hub/` from the same raw folder as the client. Serve the built game beside them from an in-process `http.createServer` on `listen(0, '127.0.0.1')`, so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping. (If you ever do start a server process, stop only that one, by its PID, as TASK.md says.) First confirm the page is your game. Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `RENDER` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`, wait for `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, read `window.__hubCheck.pass` and print failing checks. Runs: `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the same with `&scenario=host-leave`. Also on its own: open the page, press Enter, Space, Enter, Space, assert no errors, `AIHub.active` false and something drawn. The harness calls time about 5 seconds into play, so `time-up` must work from any point, and a `result` with score 0 is valid.\\n\\n*ui:* at 1920×1080, walk every screen and assert: no text under 30 px in computed style, nothing outside the viewport or the 5% margin except backgrounds, at most six focusable items, a visible focus. Touch-only: Pixel 7, iPhone and iPad profiles show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings, `page.touchscreen` tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state. About shows every Source Information field and the three feedback lists, and matches the exit-interview file.\\n\\n**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080: title, island on Day 1 and Day 5, each event mid-play, results, jar, Age Reveal, Group Class with four dolls, Dev Mode, About. Look for lost legibility at 10 feet, sunk characters, messy ink.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Build order and cut order\\n\\nEach milestone ends with something playable. Commit to the order: Fruit Hat before menus, because if the core isn't fun nothing else matters. Keep everything working as you go.\\n\\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 0 | Skeleton | Build script, single HTML, renderer with ink, sky and sea, Rapier, the loop, hooks, `test.mjs`, session client in place |\\n| 1 | The soul | Balance core, Balance Disc, doll with clips and ragdoll, input with all three devices, **Fruit Hat complete with results.** Tune until it makes you want one more go |\\n| 2 | The shell | Title, menu, Who's Here, island with tilt physics, Trudy with voice, dialogue, music engine, save, pause, settings, glyphs |\\n| 3 | The programme | Check-In, Wobble Age, Jar, Today's Plan, days. **Tier A events:** Poses, Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn, Room Service, Plate Spinners, Springboard |\\n| 4 | The features | Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, Tippy Raft, hub session, touch-only, About. Passing the hub harness |\\n| 5 | The ending | The Grand Hold, ending, credits, Maintenance days |\\n| 6 | Tier B events, in this order | Still Water, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Island Loop |\\n| 7 | Polish | Juice, more lines, art pass, full tests, exit interview, final build |\\n\\n**Cut order if you run short. Cut whole things, never stub them, and leave no trace (TASK.md: no stubs, no placeholders).** First Island Loop, then Tug of Peace, then Plank & Perch, then Still Water, then Tier 3 of each event (leaving a two-tier ladder), then Tier 2. Do not cut: Fruit Hat, Poses, Check-In, Trudy, the tilting island, the jar, the finale, Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, the hub session, glyphs, audio. The day table builds from the registry, so cutting an event needs no other change.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Done and reporting\\n\\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\\n\\n- [ ] Single HTML where TASK.md says, opens from `file://`, no external requests, no audio files\\n- [ ] WebGPU backend by default, WebGL2 fallback works, cel-shaded with the ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Three R180, Web Audio, Gamepad API\\n- [ ] Original cast, events, art and music; title is your own random pick; nothing from section 2's lists\\n- [ ] All thirteen events, or the ones left after cuts, each with tiers, an intro, results and bots\\n- [ ] Programme through the ending, Wobble Age graph, jar, unlocks, Free Play\\n- [ ] Controller-first; keyboard and mouse native on the same screens; prompts follow last device; Xbox glyph option; no dense screens\\n- [ ] Attract at 10 s, any input exits; Dev Mode from Pause with tools, live sliders and reset; touch-only message and Settings\\n- [ ] Group Class 1-4 on one screen; hub harness passes at 1, 2, 4 players, on the bad network, with the host leaving; game on its own passes Enter and Space\\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default; all sound in code; gibberish voices with every word on screen\\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs full` passes; a scripted whole-game playthrough is part of it\\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in `src/`\\n- [ ] About/Credits, the exit-interview file and your chat reply are all filled in as below\\n\\n**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build. Nothing in this plan answers them for you, and section 22 is only an example. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n\\n**The last thing you do, in this order:**\\n1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\\n2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\\n3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\\n4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\\n5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\\n\\nDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \\\"unknown\\\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\\n\\n**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\\n\\n| Page | Shows |\\n|---|---|\\n| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\\n| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\\n| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\\n| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\\n| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\\n| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\\n| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\\n\\nUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\\n\\n**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\\n\\n```\\n## Source Information\\n- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\\n## Testing Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n## Engine Feedback\\n1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\\n## Prompt Feedback\\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\\n```\\n\\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\\n\\n| Field | What to write |\\n|---|---|\\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\\n| Source | Wii Fit |\\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\\n| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\\n| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\\n\\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\\n2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\\n2. What was confusing?\\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\\n\\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\\n\\n| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\\n|---|---|\\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\\n| `type` | Game |\\n| `genre` | Sports |\\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\\n| `players` | 1–4 |\\n| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\\n\\n**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\\n\\n**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\\n\\n**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\\n\\n**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \\\"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\\\", and these are as far as it goes:**\\n- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\\n- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\\n- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\\n- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\\n\\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in | Proven at build time by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep; section 2, what we don't copy | `unit`: source's names absent outside the title and About. Your art pass |\\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 | Your report, section 19 |\\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 | Art pass screenshots |\\n| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 | You run the generator; one constant in `src/data/config.js` |\\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row | Your report |\\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build | `unit`: inline scripts only. `flow`: opens from `file://` |\\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack | `flow`: `THREE.REVISION` is 180 ✔ |\\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer | `events`: `backend` is webgpu and `render.lost` is null ✔ |\\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9 | `events`: ink pixels in a read-back screenshot ✔. Art pass |\\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 | `flow`: `RAPIER.version()` ✔ |\\n| Web Audio | Section 11 | `audio`: live bus and offline renders ✔ |\\n| Gamepad API | Section 3 | `input`: every verb through the stubbed pad ✔ |\\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka | Your report |\\n| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes | Art pass |\\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 | `events`: the mixer's time advances and clips change ✔ |\\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11 | `unit`: greps. `audio`: every track, sting, effect and voice renders non-silent |\\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices | `audio`: blips per line, peaks balanced |\\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11 | `audio`: setting 10, bus gain .10 ✔ |\\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 | `hub` ✔ |\\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build | The build writes where TASK.md says; `unit` opens it |\\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined | `unit`: no external scripts |\\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer; `hub` test | `hub`: in-process server on port 0, page confirmed first ✔ |\\n| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer | Nothing to prove: no FPS test exists on purpose |\\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test | `flow` reaches the ending and beyond |\\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command | Its own output ✔ |\\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass | Your practice |\\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph | Your practice |\\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours | Your report |\\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags | `events`: `render.lost` null ✔ |\\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags | `events`: WebKit boot ✔ |\\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \\\"never required\\\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\\n| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 | `unit` greps; your art pass and judgement |\\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 | `flow`: every day and event, in order |\\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 | `balance` for mechanics; your judgement for tone |\\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 | `unit`: table integrity; a removed event still leaves a working programme |\\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 | `flow`: only reachable from Pause ✔ |\\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 | `flow`: a slider changes the running event, reset restores it ✔ |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 | `flow`: starts near 10 wall-clock seconds, loops, each device exits it and the press is swallowed ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session | `hub`: harness at 1, 2, 4 players, bad network, host leaving ✔ |\\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own | `input`: four pads, four lanes ✔. `hub`: Enter and Space on its own ✔ |\\n| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 | `unit`: lines exist, no repeats. Your judgement |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 | `ui` at 1080p ✔. `input`: all menus by pad alone ✔ |\\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 | `input`: same effects from keys and mouse ✔. `balance` on keys |\\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 | `input`: pointer weight, hover, click ✔; a parked mouse doesn't steal focus ✔ |\\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3 | `input`: `data-glyph` changes in the same tick ✔ |\\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules | `ui`: 30 px minimum, six choices, safe area, on every screen ✔ |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 | `ui`: Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad ✔ |\\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19 | `unit`: greps ✔ |\\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\\n| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\\n\\n✔ here means the check was run on the integration prototype of section 20.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 22. Planning-stage report\\n\\nTASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\\n\\n**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\\n\\n**Testing Feedback**\\n1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\\n2. *How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?* 13: ground contact capping lean; a parked stick scoring 100%; Plate Spinners unlosable; Plate Spinners unreachable on keys; Tug of Peace unwinnable; Tippy Raft never ending; Lean Match impossible; the fall loop after a ragdoll; silent controller play; both mouse buttons never registering together; Rapier's page-killing panic; tests \\\"passing\\\" on a lost WebGPU device; a boot snippet that wouldn't build.\\n3. *What was the most helpful test you ran?* Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes. It found the lost device, the parked mouse, the mouse-button chord, silent pad play and the fall loop, none of which any separate check had shown.\\n4. *What was the least helpful test you ran?* The Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\\n\\n**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\\n1. *What did you like most about the engine?* `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n2. *Did you get stuck or guess wrong?* Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps. `hash()` given a vec2 streaked the image, toon glass turned smoky, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\\n3. *What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?* The balance core: a torque-limited controller with gravity feed-forward on a ball joint.\\n4. *What was the hardest part about using the engine?* Knowing what was really happening. A lost WebGPU device gives no error and a loop that seems to run. A Rapier crash surfaces half a second after its cause, only once something moves.\\n5. *What did you change in the engine, and why?* None.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1112}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"827f4543-de38-4624-a425-89c84c0431e8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I understand the task.\n\nThe goal plan should state outcomes rather than architecture. PLAN.md already specifies the implementation details, so the plan must define what success looks like.\n\nThe plan must define two outcomes: a working game matching PLAN.md, and deliverables named in TASK.md. Verifiers should not reject correct work for differing module layouts.\n\nTASK.md deliverable names must be preserved verbatim. PLAN.md file layout should not be frozen into acceptance criteria.\n\nThe HTML filename must match the pattern `[GameTitle]-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`. The implementer selects the title with RNG. A subfolder of the same name is required. `exit-interview.json` and `PROGRESS.md` are deliverables. An About/Credits section must include source information.\n\nThe HTML filename must contain the literal word \"Wii Fit\" with its original space. Verifiers should check for the artifact in source rather than requiring interactive play.\n\nThe acceptance criteria should be small, ideally three to five grouped items. The first criterion covers the core balance physics: Rapier ball-joint doll mechanics, Balance Disc behavior, lean and flail and fall and ragdoll and recover states, and one-verb shift weight.\n\nThe second criterion covers the world ritual. The crooked island tilts with progress. Check-in wobble age and marbles jar and days one through five ending at Grand Hold are required. Free play and maintenance are also required.\n\nThe third criterion covers events. All thirteen events must be present or their cuts must follow section eighteen. Fruit Hat Poses Check-In Trudy tilting island jar finale Attract Dev Mode Group Class hub session glyphs and audio are mandatory. Tier B events may be cut if short. Island Loop Tug of Peace Plank and Perch Still Water and then tiers are optional.\n\nThe acceptance criteria must require the full game. Cuts are only a last resort if time runs out during implementation. The harness will judge against the complete plan, so gating on all thirteen events would wrongly reject correct work that follows the plan's own cut order.\n\nThe plan writer's instructions say to record any genuinely unachievable item under Non-goals. This supports requiring the full game rather than allowing cuts as acceptance criteria.\n\nThe first acceptance criterion is now drafted. It requires a single self-contained HTML file in the work-folder root named `{riff}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`. The file must open from `file://` with no external network requests or audio files. A matching dev subfolder must hold the source.\n\nThe title is a playful riff on \"Wii Fit\" chosen by RNG. `exit-interview.json` must sit beside the HTML and parse with TASK.md's exact shape: usage \"added on import\", four feedback answers, six feedback answers, four feedback answers, and an aiHub block. The in-game About/Credits screen must display the same Source Information and three feedback lists as the JSON.\n\nA fourth criterion covers originality. The game must not reuse the source's product names, characters, or forbidden activities as its own content. The title joke and About source line are exempt.\n\nA fifth criterion defines the playable demo. The shipped page is a third-person physics-balance party-sports demo using Three.js R180 with WebGPU and WebGL2 fallback. It applies a cel-shaded ink filter. Rapier 0.19.3 handles physics. All audio is synthesized in code via Web Audio. It uses Animalese and Simlish gibberish. Music defaults to ten percent. The Gamepad API is supported. Weight-shift is the shared verb.\n\nA sixth criterion defines the vertical slice. The player must complete the full programme: profile, check-in with Wobble Age, and classes that exercise the balance core. The uncuttable set includes Fruit Hat and Poses plus Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn, Room Service, Plate Spinners, Springboard, and The Grand Hold. Tier B events may be cut per the plan's order. Marbles pour into the jar. The island tilts and falls as programme steps complete and reaches level at the ending. Maintenance and free play follow. Failure triggers a ragdoll that recovers without ending the day. No skill gate blocks the story.\n\nThe plan must avoid a single whole-system gate. The acceptance criteria should be decomposed into atomic, independently checkable items. The first criterion now covers the package and reporting hand-in: the single HTML file, dev subfolder, exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, and About screen matching the JSON. It also requires the title riff, self-contained file:// loading with no external requests or audio files, and the presence of the specified stack versions.\n\nA seventh criterion defines the balance core and Fruit Hat. The ball-joint physics model, disc behavior, lean, flail, fall at roughly forty-five degrees, ragdoll recovery, and sway must be present. Fruit Hat is playable: the player catches fruit by leaning, banks with a hop, and an unbanked load topples. Direct unit tests of the balance core and Fruit Hat scoring against the three bot skills must produce the plan's qualitative outcomes. Sharp banking must not fall; never-banking must fall; a parked stick must score below Exemplary on Poses.\n\nThe island rest-tilt must depend on completed programme steps rather than player skill, and it must reach zero at the Grand Hold ending. Every shipped event needs an intro, a play phase, results, tiers, and bots. If higher tiers are cut whole, only the two-tier or one-tier ladder may remain.\n\nThe \"do not cut\" list is the absolute minimum. Tier A events are the intended programme. The computer can run all thirteen events and all three tiers, so every event and every tier must be present.\n\nThe plan's cut order should be listed under Risks. A verifier must know that whole-event cuts in that order, with no stubs, still satisfy the contract if the uncuttable list remains intact. The thirteen events are explicitly requested by PLAN.md, so cuts are a contingency rather than an unrequested feature.\n\nThe criterion will use an OR condition: either every event is playable, or a whole event was removed only under the section eighteen cut order with no stub left and the day table still runs. This gives the implementer the plan's own escape hatch without letting them ship a skeleton. The builder may run out of effort, not the computer.\n\nThe acceptance criterion is being simplified. The shipped game must include every system PLAN.md marks as do-not-cut, plus every section six event that was not removed as a whole item in the section eighteen cut order. This replaces the previous OR condition with a single clear rule.\n\nThe \"all thirteen unless cut\" wording is too messy for a small-model verifier. The criterion should simply require the full set, with the section eighteen cut order listed under Risks as a contingency. A verifier must accept a whole-event removal only when PROGRESS.md records it and no stubs remain.\n\nThe plan is now settled. The objective's done condition is \"everything in PLAN.md that can be done,\" and the computer can run all thirteen events, so the acceptance criterion will require the full game. The section eighteen cut order is advisory guidance for when effort runs short, not a pass condition.\n\nControls are controller-first on the same screens as keyboard and mouse. Prompts follow the last device with PS glyphs and an Xbox option. Ten-foot UI rules apply. Attract mode appears after ten seconds of idle time on the title and menu screens. Any input exits attract mode and is swallowed. Dev mode opens from pause with live sliders and a reset button. Touch-only devices show a controller or keyboard required message and settings. Attract mode still runs.\n\nGroup Class supports one to four local pads on a single screen for couch events. The AI Hub ghost session must pass the hub harness at one, two, and four players, including bad-network and host-leave scenarios; standalone Enter or Space input is harmless.\n\nAudio defaults to ten percent music, all synthesized with gibberish voices, and offline renders must be non-silent without clipping.\n\nThe plan is being refolded into five concise acceptance criteria.\n\nThe hand-in package must include the HTML file, a dev subfolder, self-contained file:// loading with no external requests or audio files, and exact stack versions: Three.js R180 with WebGPU and WebGL2 fallback, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, and Gamepad API support. It must also contain exit-interview.json with its exact shape, an About screen matching that JSON, PROGRESS.md, a title riff distinct from the source name, and originality bans.\n\nThe balance and programme criterion requires the shared physics balance core, every section six event with its defining play rules, intros, results, three tiers, and bots, plus check-in, Wobble Age, marbles, island tilt that tracks completed steps to level at the Grand Hold ending, ragdoll falls that recover without ending the day, and a scripted playthrough that reaches the finale and maintenance.\n\nThe programme criterion is being split into three atomic, independently checkable parts. One covers the balance model tested from a fresh world. Another covers each event's scoring and rules tested in isolation. The third covers programme progression tested via day-start hooks.\n\nThe five-criterion plan is being revised. Programme, events, and balance are too broad to be one checkable outcome, so they will be split into separate atomic criteria. The first criterion now covers only the shipped package and reporting: exact files, stack versions, About screen, exit interview, title, no stubs or TODOs, originality constraints, and self-contained loading.\n\nBalance is now a standalone criterion. It demands direct unit tests of the physics core: lean response, fall and recover without looping, sway defeating a parked stick, qualitative gust and flail behavior, prop load destabilization, and no ground-collider lock.\n\nEvents and programme are also split into their own criterion. Each event must be independently reachable and produce its defining result. The day flow, wobble age formula, marbles, island tilt formula, and ending must all be independently correct.\n\nThe events-and-programme criterion is being rewritten as two separate atomic checks. Each shipped event must run from intro through play on the shared balance core to results, with its defining rule held. The programme must start from a fresh profile and yield a Wobble Age in 18–99 that falls across days at constant skill, pour marbles, and lower island rest tilt by completed steps until The Grand Hold leaves the island level. These are independent tests, not one unbroken playthrough.\n\nAudio is folded into the UX criterion. Art and animation are folded into the package or balance criterion as cel-shaded ink, jointed peg dolls, AnimationMixer clips, procedural lean, and ragdoll.\n\nNon-goals are defined. FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Real controllers, real TVs, real ears, real second humans, and real iOS devices are not required; stand-ins suffice.\n\nThe verification plan now includes real in-repo tests. It requires a launch check of the HTML via Playwright with the plan's RENDER flags, asserting that screenshots are not blank when readback works. A blank WebGPU readback is treated as the app's fault if the plan says screenshots should be real.\n\nThe verification plan now specifies a concrete entry-point launch sequence. Playwright probes the served or file:// page, asserts zero page errors, checks render surface dimensions and substantial fill, confirms driven input produces visible change, and captures a screenshot. Multiple runs must be consistent. Balance logic will be exercised through unit tests in tools/test.mjs.\n\nThe verification plan will reference PLAN.md section 17 for the test command rather than prescribing every file name as a criterion. It must avoid dictating module or file layout, since that would constrain the solution. The `window.__TEST__` hooks specified in PLAN.md are part of the game itself and enable real shipped tests.\n\nThe verification plan will reference the test hooks as a means to exercise the game, not as a frozen implementation detail. It will specify that a single command runs the in-repo tests and prints only failures plus a one-line count, with groups covering balance, events, flow, input, audio, hub, and UI.\n\nThe verification plan now specifies exact Playwright Chromium flags for the file:// launch: `--enable-unsafe-webgpu`, `--enable-features=Vulkan`, and SwiftShader adapter switches. The screenshot readback must contain substantially filled ink-like dark pixels with a range of colors, not a blank buffer. A driven input must change game state within one second. The check runs twice to confirm consistency.\n\nIf the launcher cannot start, the failure is captured to {SCRATCH} and the run falls back to static checks and unit tests. A blank readback that succeeds is a failure to fix, not a fallback.\n\nThe hub harness is an external oracle, but it is local because its files are fetched from GitHub rather than running on remote CI. It remains part of the game's own test gating.\n\nThe objective names Wii Fit as the source, yet PLAN.md already forbids copying it and requires an original build. The named artifact is the game itself, not the source. The AI Hub protocol is a named specification.\n\nThe hub README will be fetched to confirm the protocol hash and metadata have not drifted from PLAN.md section 12. This prevents the plan from freezing a stale specification.\n\nThe hub hash will not be frozen into acceptance criteria. PLAN.md already directs using the newer client from scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs when one exists, so the criterion should only require that the page integrates the current released AI Hub session client in ghost mode and passes the hub's own harness.\n\nThe implementer must brainstorm fifty titles and select one with an RNG. The criterion is the resulting recorded title, not a pre-selected choice.\n\nSTAGE.md requires PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe chat reply containing source information is a hand-in item. The verifier may not see it.\n\nThe exit-interview JSON and the About screen are the durable evidence for completion. The chat reply is a hand-in item but not a file in the folder. The folder contains only PLAN, STAGE, and TASK.\n\nThe plan will use five tightly grouped criteria rather than one per mechanic. Every criterion will be tagged as either gating or evidence. The implementation should separate pure balance, scoring, and programme logic from rendering so tests can run with render=0. Exact filenames will not be prescribed as acceptance criteria, but the structure suggested by PLAN.md may guide layout.\n\nThe implementation approach is guidance only, not an acceptance criterion. The task checklist will list three to eight concrete milestones drawn from PLAN.md section 18, ending with testing.\n\nTwo risks are noted. Headless WebGPU can silently lose its device and report 60fps; verification must assert render.lost is null and treat a successful blank readback as a failure. Software WebGPU runs at roughly six fps and consumes too much memory for the full rendered suite, so logic tests should run without the renderer.\n\nThe build must fail closed if the hub client hash does not match the release the harness checks. Scope is enormous; PLAN.md section 18’s cut order is the only authorized reduction, and any cut that violates the do-not-cut list fails. The game uses a single IIFE HTML file, which is correct for file://.\n\nThe plan will not instruct the implementer to use subagents, since TASK.md forbids them. It will also note that Wii Fit was not re-researched, because PLAN.md already defines the deliverable mechanics and forbids copying the source.\n\nThe plan will not instruct the implementer to use subagents, since TASK.md forbids them. It will also note that Wii Fit was not re-researched, because PLAN.md already defines the deliverable mechanics and forbids copying the source.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"PLAN.md is the spec. I’ll confirm the AI Hub session contract so the criteria match the protocol the game has to pass."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bddd4a17-ee83-422f-9c52-90e82aa58157-10","name":"web_fetch","input":{"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12174,"output_tokens":5861,"cache_read_input_tokens":47872,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"19857315-72fc-4d2a-a49b-aa9d9aca2250"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-bddd4a17-ee83-422f-9c52-90e82aa58157-10","content":"{\"type\":\"WebFetch\",\"Content\":{\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\",\"content\":\"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n\\n### The start\\n\\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\\n`start`.\\n\\n### Reporting\\n\\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\\n  by the host only:\\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\\n    placed last.\\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\\n\\n### Time-up and the end\\n\\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\\n  `finish()` by then is void.\\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\\n  game, then removes it.\\n\\n| `end` reason | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\\n\\n### Leaving\\n\\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\\n  that player leaving.\\n\\n### Scoring\\n\\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\\n  progress.\\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\\n  result instead of medals.\\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\\n\\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\\nresults:\\n\\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\\n\\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Sending data between copies\\n\\n### Parallel\\n\\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\\n\\n### Ghost\\n\\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\\n\\n### Shared world\\n\\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\\n  every copy.\\n\\n### `send()` and `message`\\n\\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\\n  client drops the rest.\\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\nIn a session:\\n\\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\\n  names. Names come from the hub.\\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\\n  is held.\\n\\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\\none, the game is not affected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Test it\\n\\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\\nto the game:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\\npython3 -m http.server 8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\\n\\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\\n`\\\"true\\\"` at the end. It checks:\\n\\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\\n  player of its own;\\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\\n  told the round ended;\\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\\n\\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\\n\\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\\nruns there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\n| Tag | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\\n\\n### API\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\\\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\\\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\\\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\\n\\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\\nread them.\\n\\n| Event | Arguments |\\n|---|---|\\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\\n| `message` | `data, from` |\\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\\n\\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\\n\",\"content_type\":\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\",\"status_code\":200,\"bytes\":17587}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"276a98c2-0092-46e3-9136-7791101fefb4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The hub protocol confirms PLAN.md's integration.\n\nThe SHA-256 hash requirement from PLAN.md is confirmed. The plan will be short and concrete for small models. It targets a code-change goal: build the original Wii Fit-inspired balance game and hand in TASK.md's files. Acceptance criteria group five outcomes, including the hand-in package and the balance core with events and programme.\n\nThe first acceptance criterion covers the hand-in package, stack, and reporting.\n\nThe hand-in package must contain a single self-contained HTML file in the root folder. Its filename uses a playful GameTitle that is not the source name. A dev subfolder with the same name holds the bundled source. Beside the HTML, an exit-interview.json must exist with TASK.md's exact shape, filled values, and specific feedback array lengths.\n\nThe exit-interview.json must contain an `aiHub` name, a genre from TASK.md's catalogue, three to six lowercase tags, and an optional empty series. A `PROGRESS.md` file must also sit in the work-folder root.\n\nThe HTML must open from `file://` with no external network requests or audio files. It must use Three.js R180 with a WebGPU backend and a WebGL2 fallback, a stylized cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, and the Gamepad API.\n\nThe in-game About/Credits screen must display the same Source Information fields and all three feedback lists as the JSON, with Usage set to `added on import`.\n\nCriterion 2 specifies the shared balance core. One weight-shift verb must be driven by a standard-mapped gamepad stick, keyboard, or mouse pointer. The pointer is screen-relative and last-device-wins. The core uses a ball joint at the foot. It supports lean, flail, and ragdoll fall recovery without immediate re-fall.\n\nThe Balance Disc shows the centre of balance. Slow sway does not score Exemplary when the stick is held still on a target. A heavy carried load reduces doll stability. Falling loses only the current hold or unbanked stake, never the day. This is checkable by driving the shipped balance update from a fresh world and from Fruit Hat started on its own: banking caught fruit keeps you up, never banking spills.\n\nCriterion 3 covers events, ritual, and world.\n\nEvery event in PLAN.md section 6 must be playable from its own start. Each event shows an intro, a countdown, the play phase, and a results screen with a medal. They run on the shared balance core and use three tiers.\n\nAny removed event or tier must have been cut in section 18 only. No stubs may remain and the day list must still run.\n\nThe do-not-cut set is preserved. It includes Fruit Hat, Poses, Check-In, Trudy, the tilting island, the marble jar, The Grand Hold, Attract, Dev Mode, Group Class, the hub session, glyphs, and synthesized audio.\n\nCriterion 3 is being tightened. A fresh profile must be able to move through the full programme: check-in tests, a Wobble Age that drops across days when skill is unchanged, classes, physical marble pours, and island rest tilt that depends on completed steps rather than skill. The island rest is level after The Grand Hold and cannot be failed. Free Play and later days continue normally.\n\nThe wording \"or a whole event was removed\" was rejected as too weak and ambiguous for a verifier. The criterion will require every playable element to be independently checkable from its own start state.\n\nThe full section 6 event set and all three tiers will be required in the acceptance criterion. The cut order will be listed as a risk, not a pass condition.\n\nThe cut order is a contingency, not a non-goal. A verifier must fail any missing do-not-cut feature or Tier B event unless PROGRESS.md records the cut and confirms no stubs remain.\n\nThe OBJECTIVE done condition is \"everything that can be done on this computer\". Section 20 confirms the full game was not built yet, but the computer can run the stack. The plan itself requires a feature-complete demo and lists cuts as a contingency. Therefore the full game is the acceptance bar.\n\nCriterion 3 will list thirteen independently startable events and systems as separate outcomes. Each must be playable from its own start state. Specific defining rules are being drafted for Poses, The Cairn, and Still Water.\n\nDefining rules are now drafted for seven more events. Log Reps uses both feet with a tempo ribbon and bonus reps. Plank & Perch has gulls pushed by flail shakes. Tug of Peace heaves on calls in best-of-three and over-lean sits the player down. Step Class footprints and lean notes must be readable without music volume; lean notes judge crossing rather than on-beat. Plate Spinners use hoop speed bands, alternating A/D keys, and plates that fall and return. Island Loop requires two laps with foot cadence, steering, and a tilted road. Fruit Hat catches items, banks with a hop, applies multipliers versus spills, and includes urchins, boots, and pineapple.\n\nDefining rules are now drafted for the remaining events. Room Service uses a tray with alternate feet, telegraphed gusts, and splash respawn. Springboard requires pump and hop launch with grace versus splash scoring. Tippy Raft is a shared tilt game where players shove to stay last standing across rounds. Grand Hold has four phases plus a last hold that cannot fail; it levels the island and shows an ending line. Check-in tests require stand still, lean match, step time, quick lean, and one-foot. Island tilt is driven by gravity rather than moving the collider. Physical marbles pour with medals and unlocks. Days 1 through 5 run before maintenance.\n\nThe verifier will read PLAN.md §6 directly. Criterion 3 will require every event there to run from its own start, implement its play and scoring rules, avoid NaN or Rapier panics, and dispose physics cleanly.\n\nCriterion 4 specifies the shell UX. Titles and menus must be readable from ten feet. The interface is controller-first, with identical screens for keyboard and mouse. Prompts follow the last input device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox letters as a toggle, and keycaps or mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nAttract mode starts after ten seconds of idle input on the title or main menu. It loops bot vignettes of real events and ignores any input that returns to the title.\n\nDev mode is reachable only from pause. It exposes live sliders for balance, world, event, reward, and ink variables plus a reset-to-defaults button. It also includes the standard jump, god, and time tools from section 14.\n\nTouch-only devices show a \"Controller or keyboard required\" message and a working Settings control. They have no touch gameplay controls but still run attract mode.\n\nMusic defaults to ten percent and is synthesized in code alongside sound effects and gibberish voices. Every spoken line appears on screen.\n\nGameplay UI text must be at least thirty pixels at one thousand eight hundred by one thousand eight hundred resolution. Each screen shows no more than six choices with a visible focus.\n\nCriterion five requires Group Class to seat one to four local players on couch events. Tippy Raft must also produce a winner when played locally.\n\nCriterion 6 covers the AI Hub session. The page follows Protocol v1 ghost mode. It loads an unmodified session client first in the head after charset. Hub metas include session one, players one through four, ghost mode, minutes, goal, howto, type Game, and source Wii Fit. No `hub:webgpu` is required. Connection and readiness work without a key. Each player has their own card. Ghost poses do not affect local physics. Progress starts from time zero. A result appears. Time-up stops input and sound.\n\nThe hub harness passes at one, two, and four players. It tolerates latency jitter loss and host leave.\n\nRepeated Enter and Space on the title must not error and must leave the game drawn. Create+Options is ignored by the game.\n\nA single in-repo test command will drive the shipped game. It prints only failures and a one-line pass or fail count.\n\nThe test command must capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log`. It will assert balance outcomes such as parked stick, fall and recover, and Fruit Hat bank versus never-bank. It will verify that each event completes from its own start state. It will check programme age tilt and marbles. It will confirm input verbs and glyphs. It will require audio to be non-silent offline with music gain at ten percent. It will validate UI rules attract mode dev sliders and the touch-only message. It will test save round-trip. A pass means zero failures.\n\nA second verification gate launches the real HTML from `file://` twice with Playwright Chromium. It uses flags to keep WebGPU alive. It asserts no page errors. It checks that canvas backing size matches the intended view. It requires a pixel readback to be substantially painted with dark ink-like pixels and a spread of other colors. It also requires that a stubbed pad or key moves the title, menu, or balance state between two captures.\n\nThe two Playwright runs must return the same result class. Screenshots and logs are saved to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `launch-2.png`, and `launch.log`. If the browser fails to start, the launcher error goes to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and the unit checks become the bar. A blank readback is a failure, not a fallback.\n\nThe hub harness serves the built HTML from 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port. Only that process runs and stops by PID. The page is confirmed to be this game.\n\nThe hub harness runs at one, two, and four players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5 percent loss, and host leave. It asserts `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true and the body carries `data-complete=true`. The file is opened outside a session and Enter and Space are pressed twice; no errors occur and something is drawn. Results go to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\n\nStatic checks on the built HTML and exit-interview.json will be folded into the unit test command. They verify JSON shape, About strings, filename patterns, no external scripts or audio references, no TODO or FIXME placeholders, session client placement immediately after the charset meta, metas in name-then-content order, and the usage string. Step 4 will grep the unit test log to confirm these assertions ran and passed.\n\nThe test suite must also assert Criterion 1: the files and About content. Criterion 5 is only partially covered by the suite, so screenshots are required as evidence. These include 1920×1080 images of the title, island day one and level, each event, results, jar, age reveal, group class, dev mode, and about. They go to `{SCRATCH}/shots/` and are not a gating check if scripts pass. A WebKit load must show a WebGL2 fallback without errors. FPS targets, optimization, and performance budgets are excluded.\n\nReal hardware, real TVs, real listeners, real second humans, and real iOS Safari are stand-ins only; they are never required. Recreating the original Wii Fit activities, names, BMI, balance board, Mii avatars, or its visual look is out of scope. Chat, emotes, custom names, and any network beyond the hub client are excluded. Text entry for profile names is forbidden. A skill gate that blocks the story is not allowed. Shared-world hub mode is not supported; ghost mode is the only valid mode. Subagents and review workflows are not permitted.\n\nThe work is confined to the current folder: PLAN.md, TASK.md, STAGE.md, a new dev subfolder, the root HTML, exit-interview.json, and PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe build depends on three@0.180.0, @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3, esbuild, playwright@1.63.0, @fontsource/fredoka, and hub session.js 1.4.0 with harness files from the ai-hub repo.\n\nSibling agent folders must not be read. Project files must not be placed in /tmp. pkill must not be used.\n\nBalance torque, scoring, wobble age, marble payout, tilt integration, chart judgment, and RNG will be pure modules callable without a renderer. A fixed 1/120 loop will run the simulation. Test mode will advance sim time without waiting and can skip the renderer.\n\nEvents are data-driven lanes. Solo, bots, group class, ghosts, and attract all share one path.\n\nThe build is a single IIFE HTML for file:// compatibility. No ES module entry is required.\n\nA single test command reruns only the changed group. Issue notes are kept for the exit interview.\n\nThe first task is to pick a title with real RNG after fifty riffs, record it, create the dev subfolder and PROGRESS.md, then scaffold the single-file build with Three r180, Rapier 0.19.3, ink, the hub client, and test hooks. A boot script will draw the island from file://.\n\nThe remaining tasks are to implement the balance core, disc, input for three devices, doll, ragdoll, and Fruit Hat with direct lock tests. Then add the island, Trudy, check-in, age, jar, days, and the rest of the events, each tested from its own start. Next build menus, attract mode, dev mode, settings, audio, glyphs, touch-only, Group Class, and the hub ghost session. Finally add the ending, about/credits, exit-interview.json, the full test command, and two launch captures under the scratch directory.\n\nA risk is noted: headless WebGPU may lose the device while frames still climb despite the flags in TASK.md.\n\nTests must use the extra Vulkan and ANGLE SwiftShader flags. A lost WebGPU device or a blank readback is a failure.\n\nSoftware WebGPU runs at roughly six frames per second and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Logic tests must not need a renderer.\n\nThe hub client is verified by SHA-256. If the repository's released hash changes, the build fails on mismatch. The session.js file must not be edited.\n\nPLAN.md §18's cut order is the only allowed reduction for a short build. It must be a whole-feature cut with no stubs and an intact do-not-cut list. Any such cut must be recorded in PROGRESS.md. A missing Tier B event is a failure.\n\nThe acceptance bar is the full set of thirteen events. PLAN.md §18's cut order is implementer guidance only. It does not relax the criteria. A stub, partial event, or cut of a do-not-cut system fails.\n\nThe built HTML filename must follow TASK.md exactly: `{GameTitle}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`. The chosen title is inserted before the literal source string \"Wii Fit\", which contains a space.\n\nThe dev subfolder will use the same basename as the root HTML. The implementer will produce the source info for the chat reply at the end.\n\nThe exit-interview.json and About screen are the handed-in copies of the source report. The chat reply must paste the same text, but verifiers audit the files directly. Criterion 1 includes PROGRESS.md as required by STAGE.md. The title is chosen with a real random number generator, and that fact is recorded in PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe title must be a playful riff, not a trademarked activity name. The implementer must record that a real random number generator selected it from candidates.\n\nCriteria will not require specific function names. PLAN.md mandates `window.__TEST__` for testability, but the verification plan will use that hook to drive the game rather than bake it into acceptance criteria.\n\nTests will drive the shipped simulation through the page's own test hook or an exported module, not a copied formula. The harness requires a double-run launch. The plan must include an implementation approach and a task checklist for code-change review.\n\nCriterion 3 will list one non-negotiable observable per event so small models do not have to infer section 6 rules from PLAN.md. The plan also requires 200 Trudy lines, 60 per instructor, and 60 per guest.\n\nThe dialogue bank criterion now requires sufficient volume to avoid immediate repeats, a polite slightly-off tone, and no body-weight or food commentary. Animation requirements are grouped into the same criterion: jointed wooden peg dolls will use the engine's clip mixer plus procedural lean, with falls swapping to a Rapier ragdoll that snaps back.\n\nArt requirements are consolidated into criterion 1. The build must use cel-shaded ink and a code-built cast and island. It must not copy the source's white gym or Mii look. The launch screenshot is verified by pixel count. The plan will use five tight criteria.\n\nTests must run the simulation and compare against thresholds from the plan, such as Exemplary ≥85, rather than hardcoding expected scores. The hub is a named gating check because it is the protocol's own harness.\n\nThe test command will include a scripted playthrough that reaches the ending. It advances simulation time rather than wall-clock time, so the full game completes in about a minute. This is gated as part of the test suite, but per-event tests starting at each event remain the primary proof and must stay local.\n\nTouch and 1080p UI checks will be gated inside the test command using Playwright. The plan file path is set under the Grok session work directory, and the implementer must ensure that directory exists before writing.\n\nThe write tool creates missing parent directories automatically, so no manual folder setup is needed. The plan will briefly note that the original Wii Fit mechanics were not re-researched, since PLAN.md forbids copying activities and treats it as assumed scope. The final response must be exactly \"Done\". Criteria are being tightened for length and clarity.\n\nThe exit-interview.json must parse as a single object in TASK.md's exact shape. It needs sourceInformation fields filled, usage set to \"added on import\", testingFeedback of length four, engineFeedback of length six, and promptFeedback of length four, all in question order. The aiHub section requires the title screen name, type Game, genre from TASK.md's list, source Wii Fit, players 1–4, three to six lowercase tags, and an optional empty series. The About and Credits screens must display those same strings.\n\nThe build will use Three.js R180 with WebGPU by default and WebGL2 fallback, Rapier 0.19.3, in-code Web Audio, and the Gamepad API. It opens from `file://` with no external requests or audio files. No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features remain. Source product names, the forbidden activity list, and the source's look are absent except for the title joke and the About source line.\n\nCriterion 2 requires a single weight-shift verb that drives one Rapier foot ball-joint core for every standing event. The verb must read from the last-used device: a standard-mapped stick, keys that hold a pose instead of springing back, or mouse offset. The Balance Disc displays that lean.\n\nBalance core behavior is now defined by observable simulation states. A full lean settles near the tuned maximum without falling. Falls past the fall angle trigger a ragdoll that recovers without snapping back instantly. An uncorrected stick on a stillness hold fails to reach Exemplary. Extra carried mass makes tipping easier. Flail is a timed save, not a free win. Fruit Hat rewards banking caught fruit with a hop and spills unbanked fruit on a fall. These checks run against the shipped step, not a re-coded formula.\n\nCriterion 3 requires every event in PLAN.md §6 to run independently. Each must present an intro, execute its rules, award a three-tiered medal, and include a bot lane.\n\nCheck-in produces a Wobble Age from 18 to 99 that falls across days when skill does not. Marbles pour into the jar from results. Island rest tilt follows completed steps, not skill, and is level after The Grand Hold, which cannot be failed. Days, Free Play, and later days keep going. A sharp bot can finish each Tier 1 event without NaN or a physics panic, and disposing the event does not leak bodies. No skill gate blocks the story.\n\nCriterion 4 covers the 10-foot shell. Keyboard and mouse share standard pad screens with prompts that switch to the last device, using PlayStation glyphs, an Xbox-letter option, keycaps, and mouse buttons. After ten seconds of idle input on the title or main menu, a looping bot demo of real events starts; any input exits it without also acting on the game. Dev mode opens only from pause, exposing §14 tools and live sliders for balance, world, events, rewards, and ink that change the running game and reset to defaults.\n\nA coarse-pointer device shows a controller prompt and Settings, skips touch gameplay, and keeps the attract demo running. Music defaults to ten percent; all audio is synthesized and every spoken line appears on screen. At 1080p, gameplay text is at least thirty pixels, each screen holds at most six choices, and focus is visible. Criterion 5 covers multiplayer: Group Class seats one to four local players on a single screen and runs couch events, including a Tippy Raft that can end with a winner.\n\nCriterion 6 covers the AI Hub. It runs as Protocol v1 ghost mode: an unmodified session client loads first, the hub metas omit the webgpu-required tag, and the game card connects and plays on `AIHub.time()` without a key press. Ghost dolls never touch local physics, progress tracks from the start of the clock, and time-up or end-of-session stops input and sound. The harness reports pass at one, two, and four players with latency, jitter, loss, and host leave.\n\nThe title screen must not error on Enter or Space and must leave a drawn frame. Options input is ignored while Create is held. Criterion 3 groups all programme events as one outcome. The verification plan stays concrete. Non-goals include FPS targets, real controllers, TVs, ears, second-person views, iOS Safari, and copying Wii Fit activities, boards, age or bank product names, Miis, or the gym look.\n\nShared-world sessions, chat, emotes, and any socket beyond the hub client are excluded. Player-typed profile names and a fail state that halts days are also out of scope. The work folder is the only target. Dependencies are Three.js 0.180.0, rapier3d-compat 0.19.3, esbuild, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka, and the AI Hub session client and harness from github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.\n\nScoring, age, payouts, tilt, and the balance torque step will be pure functions callable without a canvas. A single fixed 1/120 step will serve both the page and tests. Events will accept a list of lanes so solo, bots, couch, ghosts, and attract share one code path. The build ships as an IIFE bundle for file://. Test mode advances simulation time without waiting and can skip the renderer. One test command prints only failures and a count. The first task is to choose the title with real RNG, record it in PROGRESS.md, and create the dev subfolder.\n\nThe implementation checklist is now concrete. It starts with a single-file WebGPU/WebGL2 ink scene booting Rapier, the hub client, and a test advance hook to confirm file:// rendering. Next comes the balance core, three-device input, disc, doll, ragdoll, and Fruit Hat, tested from a fresh world. Then the island ritual and every other §6 event are added, each verified from its own start. Shell UX follows: menus, attract, dev sliders, audio, glyphs, touch-only mode, Group Class, and the hub ghost session. The final steps are the ending, About screen, exit-interview.json, full test log, and two launch captures.\n\nRendering tests must add `--use-vulkan=swiftshader` and `--use-angle=swiftshader` to prevent device loss while the frame counter climbs. A lost device or a blank readback fails the test. Logic tests must not need a renderer; at most two browsers should run. PLAN.md §18 allows cutting whole events if the build runs short, but the objective requires everything this computer can do, so the full demo must run.\n\nThe acceptance bar is the full §6 event set; a stub is never a cut. The hub rejects an edited session.js and checks its hash; if the published client hash differs from the one in PLAN.md, the build fails on mismatch. Audio quality has no human verifier, so signal tests and the on-screen keypress fallback are the bar. Verification steps will be numbered carefully. A present but non-empty launch file is insufficient.\n\nThe primary boot observable is the title screen: a substantially painted canvas with the GameTitle string visible and the crooked island in view. A driven input must change state, such as moving off the title or starting the profile. The test output path is `{SCRATCH}`. Hub verification is treated as a separate gating step.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0ec866de-fad9-4676-85d8-646a429f5897-11","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9%2Fwork/01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66/goal/plan-01a1aa57-1c92-7a82-ba74-69a4fec8b5d0.md","content":"# Plan: Build the original crooked-island balance game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md file\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML file named `{GameTitle}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`, a dev subfolder with that same basename, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. GameTitle is a playful riff on \"Wii Fit\" (not that name and not a source activity name), chosen with a real random-number generator and recorded in `PROGRESS.md`, and it is the title drawn on the title screen. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape: every `sourceInformation` value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, feedback arrays of length 4, 6, and 4 in question order, and `aiHub` whose `name` equals that title, `type` Game, `genre` from TASK.md's catalogue list, `source` Wii Fit, `players` `1–4`, 3–6 lowercase tags (`series` may be empty). The About/Credits screen shows those same strings. The page is Three.js R180 (WebGPU by default, WebGL2 fallback that still draws), a cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, and the Gamepad API. It opens from `file://` with no external requests and no audio files. No TODO, placeholder, or stubbed feature remains. Source product names, the forbidden activities, and the source's gym/avatar look from PLAN.md §2 appear only in the title joke and the About source line.\n\n2. One screen-relative weight-shift verb, taken from whichever device was used last (a standard-mapped stick, keys that leave a pose where they were released, or the pointer's offset from centre), drives one Rapier foot ball-joint core shared by every standing event. The Balance Disc shows that lean. From a fresh world, a full lean settles near the tuned max lean without falling; crossing the fall angle becomes a ragdoll and recovery does not instantly fall again; a stick parked on a stillness target and never corrected does not score Exemplary; extra carried mass makes the doll easier to tip; flail is a timed save with a cooldown, not a free win. Fruit Hat, started on its own, banks caught fruit on a hop and spills unbanked fruit on a fall. Checks run the shipped step, not a re-coded copy of the formula.\n\n3. Each event in PLAN.md §6 (Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Island Loop, Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Tippy Raft, The Grand Hold), started on its own, plays that section's rules through intro (what you can and cannot do), play, and a medal result, on three tiers, with a bot lane. Check-in yields a Wobble Age from 18 to 99 that falls across days when skill is unchanged; marbles pour into the jar from results; island rest tilt follows completed programme steps, not skill, and is level after The Grand Hold, which cannot be failed. Days, Free Play, and later days continue, and no skill gate blocks the story. A sharp bot finishes each Tier 1 event with no NaN and no physics panic, and disposing the event does not leak bodies.\n\n4. Keyboard and mouse play the same screens as a pad. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox-letter option, and keycaps or mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or main menu, a looping bot demo of real events starts; any input leaves it and is not also acted on. Dev mode opens only from pause and includes the §14 tools plus live sliders for balance, world, events, rewards, and ink that change a running event and reset to defaults. A coarse pointer with no fine pointer shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a working Settings control, has no touch gameplay, and still runs attract mode. Music defaults to 10%. Music, effects, and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, and every spoken line is also on screen. At 1920×1080, gameplay text is at least 30px, a screen has at most six choices, and focus is visible.\n\n5. Group Class on one screen seats 1–4 local players on the couch events, and Tippy Raft can end with a winner. An AI Hub session is Protocol v1 ghost mode: the unmodified session client is the first script in `head` after the charset meta; the §12 metas are present (`hub:session` 1, players `1-4`, mode `ghost`, minutes, goal, howto, type Game, source Wii Fit) and `hub:webgpu` required is not set; the game connects and calls ready with no key press, shows its own card, schedules the round on `AIHub.time()`, draws ghost dolls that never affect local physics, reports progress from the start of the clock, sends one result, and on time-up or end stops input and sound. The hub harness passes at 1 player, at 2 players, at 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss, and with the host leaving. Outside a session, Enter and Space on the title leave a drawn frame and no error. Options is ignored while Create is held.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: Add in-repo tests that drive the shipped simulation (the same fixed step the page runs), not a copy of the formulas and not a mock of the unit under test. One command prints only failures and a one-line count, and its full run covers criteria 2–4 from their own start states: balance (parked stick, fall/recover, load, Fruit Hat bank versus never-bank), each §6 event, check-in/age/marbles/tilt, a sim-time playthrough from a fresh profile through the ending and a later day, input verbs and glyphs, attract at 10 seconds with the press swallowed, dev sliders and reset, touch-only copy and Settings, offline audio renders that are finite, non-silent, and not clipped with music gain 0.10, the 1080p UI limits, the exit-interview shape, and the built HTML (no external requests, no audio files, no TODO/placeholder/stub, client immediately after charset). Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log`. Pass only if the count shows zero failures.\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` twice, in Playwright Chromium, with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Each run must show no page errors, a live WebGPU device (a lost device fails), a canvas whose drawing size matches the requested view, and a pixel readback that is substantially painted (a large painted fraction or a bbox covering most of the surface, with dark ink-like pixels and a spread of other colours — not merely non-zero). The title string on screen equals GameTitle. A stubbed standard pad or a key then changes screen or balance state in a second capture. Both runs must succeed the same way. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. If the browser cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and criterion 1's file checks plus the non-render tests in step 1 are the bar. A readback that succeeds and is blank or nearly blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\n3. gating: Serve the built HTML and the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port, confirm the response is this game, then run the harness to `body[data-complete=\"true\"]` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` for `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and `scenario=host-leave`. Stop only that server, by its PID. Also open the file outside a session and press Enter, Space, Enter, Space: no errors and a drawn frame. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\n4. evidence: At 1920×1080, with the same rendering flags, capture title, island on day 1 and level, each event mid-play, results, jar, age reveal, four-player Group Class, dev mode, and About into `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. A WebKit load that reports the WebGL2 fallback and no page errors goes to `{SCRATCH}/webkit.log`. Absence of these shots does not fail the goal once steps 1–3 pass.\n\n## Non-goals\n- FPS targets, optimization, or a performance budget.\n- A real DualSense, TV, listener, second human, or iOS Safari. Stand-ins in TASK.md are as far as those checks go.\n- Recreating Wii Fit activities, its balance board, its age/bank product names, Mii avatars, or its white gym look.\n- Shared-world hub mode, chat, emotes, custom names, or any network besides the hub client.\n- Typed profile names, or a fail state that stops the days.\n\n## Assumed scope\nOnly this work folder. New sources live in the named dev subfolder; the bundled HTML, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md` land in the work-folder root. Runtime deps are three@0.180.0, @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3, esbuild, playwright@1.63.0, Fredoka, and the AI Hub session client plus harness (protocol v1, client 1.4.0) from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Wii Fit's own activity list was not re-derived: PLAN.md already maps the source's pillars onto original events and forbids copying those activities.\n\n## Implementation approach\nKeep age, payouts, tilt, scoring, and the balance torque step pure so tests call them without a canvas. One fixed 1/120 step is shared by the page and the tests. Events take a list of lanes so solo, bots, couch play, ghosts, and attract are the same code. Bundle an IIFE into one HTML file so `file://` works with no module import. A test mode advances sim time without waiting and can skip the renderer. One test command prints only failures and a count; rerun only the part a change touches.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Pick GameTitle with a real RNG after brainstorming riffs, record it in PROGRESS.md, and create the matching dev subfolder.\n- [ ] Boot a single-file build: Three r180 ink scene, Rapier 0.19.3, unmodified hub client, WebGL2 fallback, and a test advance hook. Confirm `file://` draws.\n- [ ] Add the balance core, three-device input, Balance Disc, jointed doll, ragdoll recovery, and Fruit Hat. Lock them with tests from a fresh world.\n- [ ] Add the island ritual (check-in, Wobble Age, jar, tilt) and every other §6 event, each tested from its own start.\n- [ ] Add menus, attract, dev sliders, synthesized audio, glyphs, touch-only, Group Class, and the hub ghost session.\n- [ ] Ship the ending, About/Credits, exit-interview.json, `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log` with zero failures, the two launch captures, and the hub log.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone lose the device while the frame counter still climbs. Rendering checks must also pass `--use-vulkan=swiftshader` and `--use-angle=swiftshader`. A lost device, or a readback that succeeds and is blank, is a failure.\n- Software WebGPU is only a few frames per second here, and extra browsers are killed when memory runs out. Logic tests must not need a renderer; keep at most two browsers open.\n- PLAN.md §18 lets a short build cut whole events in a fixed order. That does not relax this plan: this computer can run the full demo, so every §6 event is required. A stub, a placeholder, or a cut of a do-not-cut system fails.\n- The hub rejects an edited `session.js` and checks its hash. If the published client hash differs from the hash written in PLAN.md, use the hash shipped with that client release and fail the build on a mismatch.\n- A real pad unlocking audio, and whether the music sounds good, cannot be confirmed here. Signal tests, plus the on-screen prompt if audio stays suspended after pad input, are the bar.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10640,"output_tokens":10574,"cache_read_input_tokens":60032,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"88cf8589-c4ab-4de6-a621-f5bf005400d7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-0ec866de-fad9-4676-85d8-646a429f5897-11","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the original crooked-island balance game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md file\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML file named `{GameTitle}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`, a dev subfolder with that same basename, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. GameTitle is a playful riff on \\\"Wii Fit\\\" (not that name and not a source activity name), chosen with a real random-number generator and recorded in `PROGRESS.md`, and it is the title drawn on the title screen. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape: every `sourceInformation` value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, feedback arrays of length 4, 6, and 4 in question order, and `aiHub` whose `name` equals that title, `type` Game, `genre` from TASK.md's catalogue list, `source` Wii Fit, `players` `1–4`, 3–6 lowercase tags (`series` may be empty). The About/Credits screen shows those same strings. The page is Three.js R180 (WebGPU by default, WebGL2 fallback that still draws), a cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, and the Gamepad API. It opens from `file://` with no external requests and no audio files. No TODO, placeholder, or stubbed feature remains. Source product names, the forbidden activities, and the source's gym/avatar look from PLAN.md §2 appear only in the title joke and the About source line.\\n\\n2. One screen-relative weight-shift verb, taken from whichever device was used last (a standard-mapped stick, keys that leave a pose where they were released, or the pointer's offset from centre), drives one Rapier foot ball-joint core shared by every standing event. The Balance Disc shows that lean. From a fresh world, a full lean settles near the tuned max lean without falling; crossing the fall angle becomes a ragdoll and recovery does not instantly fall again; a stick parked on a stillness target and never corrected does not score Exemplary; extra carried mass makes the doll easier to tip; flail is a timed save with a cooldown, not a free win. Fruit Hat, started on its own, banks caught fruit on a hop and spills unbanked fruit on a fall. Checks run the shipped step, not a re-coded copy of the formula.\\n\\n3. Each event in PLAN.md §6 (Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Island Loop, Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Tippy Raft, The Grand Hold), started on its own, plays that section's rules through intro (what you can and cannot do), play, and a medal result, on three tiers, with a bot lane. Check-in yields a Wobble Age from 18 to 99 that falls across days when skill is unchanged; marbles pour into the jar from results; island rest tilt follows completed programme steps, not skill, and is level after The Grand Hold, which cannot be failed. Days, Free Play, and later days continue, and no skill gate blocks the story. A sharp bot finishes each Tier 1 event with no NaN and no physics panic, and disposing the event does not leak bodies.\\n\\n4. Keyboard and mouse play the same screens as a pad. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox-letter option, and keycaps or mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or main menu, a looping bot demo of real events starts; any input leaves it and is not also acted on. Dev mode opens only from pause and includes the §14 tools plus live sliders for balance, world, events, rewards, and ink that change a running event and reset to defaults. A coarse pointer with no fine pointer shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a working Settings control, has no touch gameplay, and still runs attract mode. Music defaults to 10%. Music, effects, and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, and every spoken line is also on screen. At 1920×1080, gameplay text is at least 30px, a screen has at most six choices, and focus is visible.\\n\\n5. Group Class on one screen seats 1–4 local players on the couch events, and Tippy Raft can end with a winner. An AI Hub session is Protocol v1 ghost mode: the unmodified session client is the first script in `head` after the charset meta; the §12 metas are present (`hub:session` 1, players `1-4`, mode `ghost`, minutes, goal, howto, type Game, source Wii Fit) and `hub:webgpu` required is not set; the game connects and calls ready with no key press, shows its own card, schedules the round on `AIHub.time()`, draws ghost dolls that never affect local physics, reports progress from the start of the clock, sends one result, and on time-up or end stops input and sound. The hub harness passes at 1 player, at 2 players, at 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss, and with the host leaving. Outside a session, Enter and Space on the title leave a drawn frame and no error. Options is ignored while Create is held.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add in-repo tests that drive the shipped simulation (the same fixed step the page runs), not a copy of the formulas and not a mock of the unit under test. One command prints only failures and a one-line count, and its full run covers criteria 2–4 from their own start states: balance (parked stick, fall/recover, load, Fruit Hat bank versus never-bank), each §6 event, check-in/age/marbles/tilt, a sim-time playthrough from a fresh profile through the ending and a later day, input verbs and glyphs, attract at 10 seconds with the press swallowed, dev sliders and reset, touch-only copy and Settings, offline audio renders that are finite, non-silent, and not clipped with music gain 0.10, the 1080p UI limits, the exit-interview shape, and the built HTML (no external requests, no audio files, no TODO/placeholder/stub, client immediately after charset). Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log`. Pass only if the count shows zero failures.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` twice, in Playwright Chromium, with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Each run must show no page errors, a live WebGPU device (a lost device fails), a canvas whose drawing size matches the requested view, and a pixel readback that is substantially painted (a large painted fraction or a bbox covering most of the surface, with dark ink-like pixels and a spread of other colours — not merely non-zero). The title string on screen equals GameTitle. A stubbed standard pad or a key then changes screen or balance state in a second capture. Both runs must succeed the same way. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. If the browser cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and criterion 1's file checks plus the non-render tests in step 1 are the bar. A readback that succeeds and is blank or nearly blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n3. gating: Serve the built HTML and the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port, confirm the response is this game, then run the harness to `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` for `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and `scenario=host-leave`. Stop only that server, by its PID. Also open the file outside a session and press Enter, Space, Enter, Space: no errors and a drawn frame. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. evidence: At 1920×1080, with the same rendering flags, capture title, island on day 1 and level, each event mid-play, results, jar, age reveal, four-player Group Class, dev mode, and About into `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. A WebKit load that reports the WebGL2 fallback and no page errors goes to `{SCRATCH}/webkit.log`. Absence of these shots does not fail the goal once steps 1–3 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or a performance budget.\\n- A real DualSense, TV, listener, second human, or iOS Safari. Stand-ins in TASK.md are as far as those checks go.\\n- Recreating Wii Fit activities, its balance board, its age/bank product names, Mii avatars, or its white gym look.\\n- Shared-world hub mode, chat, emotes, custom names, or any network besides the hub client.\\n- Typed profile names, or a fail state that stops the days.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nOnly this work folder. New sources live in the named dev subfolder; the bundled HTML, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md` land in the work-folder root. Runtime deps are three@0.180.0, @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3, esbuild, playwright@1.63.0, Fredoka, and the AI Hub session client plus harness (protocol v1, client 1.4.0) from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Wii Fit's own activity list was not re-derived: PLAN.md already maps the source's pillars onto original events and forbids copying those activities.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep age, payouts, tilt, scoring, and the balance torque step pure so tests call them without a canvas. One fixed 1/120 step is shared by the page and the tests. Events take a list of lanes so solo, bots, couch play, ghosts, and attract are the same code. Bundle an IIFE into one HTML file so `file://` works with no module import. A test mode advances sim time without waiting and can skip the renderer. One test command prints only failures and a count; rerun only the part a change touches.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick GameTitle with a real RNG after brainstorming riffs, record it in PROGRESS.md, and create the matching dev subfolder.\\n- [ ] Boot a single-file build: Three r180 ink scene, Rapier 0.19.3, unmodified hub client, WebGL2 fallback, and a test advance hook. Confirm `file://` draws.\\n- [ ] Add the balance core, three-device input, Balance Disc, jointed doll, ragdoll recovery, and Fruit Hat. Lock them with tests from a fresh world.\\n- [ ] Add the island ritual (check-in, Wobble Age, jar, tilt) and every other §6 event, each tested from its own start.\\n- [ ] Add menus, attract, dev sliders, synthesized audio, glyphs, touch-only, Group Class, and the hub ghost session.\\n- [ ] Ship the ending, About/Credits, exit-interview.json, `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log` with zero failures, the two launch captures, and the hub log.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone lose the device while the frame counter still climbs. Rendering checks must also pass `--use-vulkan=swiftshader` and `--use-angle=swiftshader`. A lost device, or a readback that succeeds and is blank, is a failure.\\n- Software WebGPU is only a few frames per second here, and extra browsers are killed when memory runs out. Logic tests must not need a renderer; keep at most two browsers open.\\n- PLAN.md §18 lets a short build cut whole events in a fixed order. That does not relax this plan: this computer can run the full demo, so every §6 event is required. A stub, a placeholder, or a cut of a do-not-cut system fails.\\n- The hub rejects an edited `session.js` and checks its hash. If the published client hash differs from the hash written in PLAN.md, use the hash shipped with that client release and fail the build on a mismatch.\\n- A real pad unlocking audio, and whether the music sounds good, cannot be confirmed here. Signal tests, plus the on-screen prompt if audio stays suspended after pad input, are the bar.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9%2Fwork/01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66/goal/plan-01a1aa57-1c92-7a82-ba74-69a4fec8b5d0.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__202