sland Loop, Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Tippy Raft, The Grand Hold), started on its own, plays that section's rules through intro (what you can and cannot do), play, and a medal result, on three tiers, with a bot lane. Check-in yields a Wobble Age from 18 to 99 that falls across days when skill is unchanged; marbles pour into the jar from results; island rest tilt follows completed programme steps, not skill, and is level after The Grand Hold, which cannot be failed. Days, Free Play, and later days continue, and no skill gate blocks the story. A sharp bot finishes each Tier 1 event with no NaN and no physics panic, and disposing the event does not leak bodies.\\n\\n4. Keyboard and mouse play the same screens as a pad. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox-letter option, and keycaps or mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or main menu, a looping bot demo of real events starts; any input leaves it and is not also acted on. Dev mode opens only from pause and includes the §14 tools plus live sliders for balance, world, events, rewards, and ink that change a running event and reset to defaults. A coarse pointer with no fine pointer shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a working Settings control, has no touch gameplay, and still runs attract mode. Music defaults to 10%. Music, effects, and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, and every spoken line is also on screen. At 1920×1080, gameplay text is at least 30px, a screen has at most six choices, and focus is visible.\\n\\n5. Group Class on one screen seats 1–4 local players on the couch events, and Tippy Raft can end with a winner. An AI Hub session is Protocol v1 ghost mode: the unmodified session client is the first script in `head` after the charset meta; the §12 metas are present (`hub:session` 1, players `1-4`, mode `ghost`, minutes, goal, howto, type Game, source Wii Fit) and `hub:webgpu` required is not set; the game connects and calls ready with no key press, shows its own card, schedules the round on `AIHub.time()`, draws ghost dolls that never affect local physics, reports progress from the start of the clock, sends one result, and on time-up or end stops input and sound. The hub harness passes at 1 player, at 2 players, at 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss, and with the host leaving. Outside a session, Enter and Space on the title leave a drawn frame and no error. Options is ignored while Create is held.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add in-repo tests that drive the shipped simulation (the same fixed step the page runs), not a copy of the formulas and not a mock of the unit under test. One command prints only failures and a one-line count, and its full run covers criteria 2–4 from their own start states: balance (parked stick, fall/recover, load, Fruit Hat bank versus never-bank), each §6 event, check-in/age/marbles/tilt, a sim-time playthrough from a fresh profile through the ending and a later day, input verbs and glyphs, attract at 10 seconds with the press swallowed, dev sliders and reset, touch-only copy and Settings, offline audio renders that are finite, non-silent, and not clipped with music gain 0.10, the 1080p UI limits, the exit-interview shape, and the built HTML (no external requests, no audio files, no TODO/placeholder/stub, client immediately after charset). Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log`. Pass only if the count shows zero failures.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` twice, in Playwright Chromium, with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Each run must show no page errors, a live WebGPU device (a lost device fails), a canvas whose drawing size matches the requested view, and a pixel readback that is substantially painted (a large painted fraction or a bbox covering most of the surface, with dark ink-like pixels and a spread of other colours — not merely non-zero). The title string on screen equals GameTitle. A stubbed standard pad or a key then changes screen or balance state in a second capture. Both runs must succeed the same way. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. If the browser cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and criterion 1's file checks plus the non-render tests in step 1 are the bar. A readback that succeeds and is blank or nearly blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n20→3. gating: Serve the built HTML and the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port, confirm the response is this game, then run the harness to `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` for `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and `scenario=host-leave`. Stop only that server, by its PID. Also open the file outside a session and press Enter, Space, Enter, Space: no errors and a drawn frame. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. evidence: At 1920×1080, with the same rendering flags, capture title, island on day 1 and level, each event mid-play, results, jar, age reveal, four-player Group Class, dev mode, and About into `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. A WebKit load that reports the WebGL2 fallback and no page errors goes to `{SCRATCH}/webkit.log`. Absence of these shots does not fail the goal once steps 1–3 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or a performance budget.\\n- A real DualSense, TV, listener, second human, or iOS Safari. Stand-ins in TASK.md are as far as those checks go.\\n- Recreating Wii Fit activities, its balance board, its age/bank product names, Mii avatars, or its white gym look.\\n- Shared-world hub mode, chat, emotes, custom names, or any network besides the hub client.\\n- Typed profile names, or a fail state that stops the days.\\n\\n30→## Assumed scope\\nOnly this work folder. New sources live in the named dev subfolder; the bundled HTML, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md` land in the work-folder root. Runtime deps are three@0.180.0, @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3, esbuild, playwright@1.63.0, Fredoka, and the AI Hub session client plus harness (protocol v1, client 1.4.0) from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Wii Fit's own activity list was not re-derived: PLAN.md already maps the source's pillars onto original events and forbids copying those activities.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep age, payouts, tilt, scoring, and the balance torque step pure so tests call them without a canvas. One fixed 1/120 step is shared by the page and the tests. Events take a list of lanes so solo, bots, couch play, ghosts, and attract are the same code. Bundle an IIFE into one HTML file so `file://` works with no module import. A test mode advances sim time without waiting and can skip the renderer. One test command prints only failures and a count; rerun only the part a change touches.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick GameTitle with a real RNG after brainstorming riffs, record it in PROGRESS.md, and create the matching dev subfolder.\\n- [ ] Boot a single-file build: Three r180 ink scene, Rapier 0.19.3, unmodified hub client, WebGL2 fallback, and a test advance hook. Confirm `file://` draws.\\n- [ ] Add the balance core, three-device input, Balance Disc, jointed doll, ragdoll recovery, and Fruit Hat. Lock them with tests from a fresh world.\\n40→- [ ] Add the island ritual (check-in, Wobble Age, jar, tilt) and every other §6 event, each tested from its own start.\\n- [ ] Add menus, attract, dev sliders, synthesized audio, glyphs, touch-only, Group Class, and the hub ghost session.\\n- [ ] Ship the ending, About/Credits, exit-interview.json, `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log` with zero failures, the two launch captures, and the hub log.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone lose the device while the frame counter still climbs. Rendering checks must also pass `--use-vulkan=swiftshader` and `--use-angle=swiftshader`. A lost device, or a readback that succeeds and is blank, is a failure.\\n- Software WebGPU is only a few frames per second here, and extra browsers are killed when memory runs out. Logic tests must not need a renderer; keep at most two browsers open.\\n- PLAN.md §18 lets a short build cut whole events in a fixed order. That does not relax this plan: this computer can run the full demo, so every §6 event is required. A stub, a placeholder, or a cut of a do-not-cut system fails.\\n- The hub rejects an edited `session.js` and checks its hash. If the published client hash differs from the hash written in PLAN.md, use the hash shipped with that client release and fail the build on a mismatch.\\n- A real pad unlocking audio, and whether the music sounds good, cannot be confirmed here. Signal tests, plus the on-screen prompt if audio stays suspended after pad input, are the bar.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9%2Fwork/01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build the original crooked-island balance game in PLAN.md and hand in every TASK.md file\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML file named `{GameTitle}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317.html`, a dev subfolder with that same basename, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML. GameTitle is a playful riff on \\\"Wii Fit\\\" (not that name and not a source activity name), chosen with a real random-number generator and recorded in `PROGRESS.md`, and it is the title drawn on the title screen. The JSON parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape: every `sourceInformation` value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, feedback arrays of length 4, 6, and 4 in question order, and `aiHub` whose `name` equals that title, `type` Game, `genre` from TASK.md's catalogue list, `source` Wii Fit, `players` `1–4`, 3–6 lowercase tags (`series` may be empty). The About/Credits screen shows those same strings. The page is Three.js R180 (WebGPU by default, WebGL2 fallback that still draws), a cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, and the Gamepad API. It opens from `file://` with no external requests and no audio files. No TODO, placeholder, or stubbed feature remains. Source product names, the forbidden activities, and the source's gym/avatar look from PLAN.md §2 appear only in the title joke and the About source line.\\n\\n2. One screen-relative weight-shift verb, taken from whichever device was used last (a standard-mapped stick, keys that leave a pose where they were released, or the pointer's offset from centre), drives one Rapier foot ball-joint core shared by every standing event. The Balance Disc shows that lean. From a fresh world, a full lean settles near the tuned max lean without falling; crossing the fall angle becomes a ragdoll and recovery does not instantly fall again; a stick parked on a stillness target and never corrected does not score Exemplary; extra carried mass makes the doll easier to tip; flail is a timed save with a cooldown, not a free win. Fruit Hat, started on its own, banks caught fruit on a hop and spills unbanked fruit on a fall. Checks run the shipped step, not a re-coded copy of the formula.\\n\\n3. Each event in PLAN.md §6 (Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Island Loop, Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Tippy Raft, The Grand Hold), started on its own, plays that section's rules through intro (what you can and cannot do), play, and a medal result, on three tiers, with a bot lane. Check-in yields a Wobble Age from 18 to 99 that falls across days when skill is unchanged; marbles pour into the jar from results; island rest tilt follows completed programme steps, not skill, and is level after The Grand Hold, which cannot be failed. Days, Free Play, and later days continue, and no skill gate blocks the story. A sharp bot finishes each Tier 1 event with no NaN and no physics panic, and disposing the event does not leak bodies.\\n\\n4. Keyboard and mouse play the same screens as a pad. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, an Xbox-letter option, and keycaps or mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or main menu, a looping bot demo of real events starts; any input leaves it and is not also acted on. Dev mode opens only from pause and includes the §14 tools plus live sliders for balance, world, events, rewards, and ink that change a running event and reset to defaults. A coarse pointer with no fine pointer shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a working Settings control, has no touch gameplay, and still runs attract mode. Music defaults to 10%. Music, effects, and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, and every spoken line is also on screen. At 1920×1080, gameplay text is at least 30px, a screen has at most six choices, and focus is visible.\\n\\n5. Group Class on one screen seats 1–4 local players on the couch events, and Tippy Raft can end with a winner. An AI Hub session is Protocol v1 ghost mode: the unmodified session client is the first script in `head` after the charset meta; the §12 metas are present (`hub:session` 1, players `1-4`, mode `ghost`, minutes, goal, howto, type Game, source Wii Fit) and `hub:webgpu` required is not set; the game connects and calls ready with no key press, shows its own card, schedules the round on `AIHub.time()`, draws ghost dolls that never affect local physics, reports progress from the start of the clock, sends one result, and on time-up or end stops input and sound. The hub harness passes at 1 player, at 2 players, at 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss, and with the host leaving. Outside a session, Enter and Space on the title leave a drawn frame and no error. Options is ignored while Create is held.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add in-repo tests that drive the shipped simulation (the same fixed step the page runs), not a copy of the formulas and not a mock of the unit under test. One command prints only failures and a one-line count, and its full run covers criteria 2–4 from their own start states: balance (parked stick, fall/recover, load, Fruit Hat bank versus never-bank), each §6 event, check-in/age/marbles/tilt, a sim-time playthrough from a fresh profile through the ending and a later day, input verbs and glyphs, attract at 10 seconds with the press swallowed, dev sliders and reset, touch-only copy and Settings, offline audio renders that are finite, non-silent, and not clipped with music gain 0.10, the 1080p UI limits, the exit-interview shape, and the built HTML (no external requests, no audio files, no TODO/placeholder/stub, client immediately after charset). Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log`. Pass only if the count shows zero failures.\\n2. gating: Launch the real root HTML from `file://` twice, in Playwright Chromium, with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Each run must show no page errors, a live WebGPU device (a lost device fails), a canvas whose drawing size matches the requested view, and a pixel readback that is substantially painted (a large painted fraction or a bbox covering most of the surface, with dark ink-like pixels and a spread of other colours — not merely non-zero). The title string on screen equals GameTitle. A stubbed standard pad or a key then changes screen or balance state in a second capture. Both runs must succeed the same way. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. If the browser cannot start, save that launcher error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-env.txt` and criterion 1's file checks plus the non-render tests in step 1 are the bar. A readback that succeeds and is blank or nearly blank is a game failure, not this fallback.\\n3. gating: Serve the built HTML and the hub harness on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port, confirm the response is this game, then run the harness to `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` for `players=1`, `players=2`, `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and `scenario=host-leave`. Stop only that server, by its PID. Also open the file outside a session and press Enter, Space, Enter, Space: no errors and a drawn frame. Capture `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. evidence: At 1920×1080, with the same rendering flags, capture title, island on day 1 and level, each event mid-play, results, jar, age reveal, four-player Group Class, dev mode, and About into `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. A WebKit load that reports the WebGL2 fallback and no page errors goes to `{SCRATCH}/webkit.log`. Absence of these shots does not fail the goal once steps 1–3 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or a performance budget.\\n- A real DualSense, TV, listener, second human, or iOS Safari. Stand-ins in TASK.md are as far as those checks go.\\n- Recreating Wii Fit activities, its balance board, its age/bank product names, Mii avatars, or its white gym look.\\n- Shared-world hub mode, chat, emotes, custom names, or any network besides the hub client.\\n- Typed profile names, or a fail state that stops the days.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nOnly this work folder. New sources live in the named dev subfolder; the bundled HTML, `exit-interview.json`, and `PROGRESS.md` land in the work-folder root. Runtime deps are three@0.180.0, @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3, esbuild, playwright@1.63.0, Fredoka, and the AI Hub session client plus harness (protocol v1, client 1.4.0) from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Wii Fit's own activity list was not re-derived: PLAN.md already maps the source's pillars onto original events and forbids copying those activities.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep age, payouts, tilt, scoring, and the balance torque step pure so tests call them without a canvas. One fixed 1/120 step is shared by the page and the tests. Events take a list of lanes so solo, bots, couch play, ghosts, and attract are the same code. Bundle an IIFE into one HTML file so `file://` works with no module import. A test mode advances sim time without waiting and can skip the renderer. One test command prints only failures and a count; rerun only the part a change touches.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Pick GameTitle with a real RNG after brainstorming riffs, record it in PROGRESS.md, and create the matching dev subfolder.\\n- [ ] Boot a single-file build: Three r180 ink scene, Rapier 0.19.3, unmodified hub client, WebGL2 fallback, and a test advance hook. Confirm `file://` draws.\\n- [ ] Add the balance core, three-device input, Balance Disc, jointed doll, ragdoll recovery, and Fruit Hat. Lock them with tests from a fresh world.\\n- [ ] Add the island ritual (check-in, Wobble Age, jar, tilt) and every other §6 event, each tested from its own start.\\n- [ ] Add menus, attract, dev sliders, synthesized audio, glyphs, touch-only, Group Class, and the hub ghost session.\\n- [ ] Ship the ending, About/Credits, exit-interview.json, `{SCRATCH}/test-full.log` with zero failures, the two launch captures, and the hub log.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone lose the device while the frame counter still climbs. Rendering checks must also pass `--use-vulkan=swiftshader` and `--use-angle=swiftshader`. A lost device, or a readback that succeeds and is blank, is a failure.\\n- Software WebGPU is only a few frames per second here, and extra browsers are killed when memory runs out. Logic tests must not need a renderer; keep at most two browsers open.\\n- PLAN.md §18 lets a short build cut whole events in a fixed order. That does not relax this plan: this computer can run the full demo, so every §6 event is required. A stub, a placeholder, or a cut of a do-not-cut system fails.\\n- The hub rejects an edited `session.js` and checks its hash. If the published client hash differs from the hash written in PLAN.md, use the hash shipped with that client release and fail the build on a mismatch.\\n- A real pad unlocking audio, and whether the music sounds good, cannot be confirmed here. Signal tests, plus the on-screen prompt if audio stays suspended after pad input, are the bar.\\n\",\"total_lines\":49}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2b362782-3503-4d20-ac50-c4d31869cce0-1","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2b362782-3503-4d20-ac50-c4d31869cce0-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ListDir\",\"Content\":{\"content\":\"- /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/\\n  - PLAN.md\\n  - STAGE.md\\n  - TASK.md\",\"absolute_root_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"0faa28b7-ae6a-45ef-b06c-eb4a8f5afb00"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_9","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plan is a full original balance game. Next I’ll read PLAN.md and TASK.md and turn their acceptance checks into a working list."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5d2de3c2-ef6f-43d3-9140-20470de5b7cb-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/TASK.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5d2de3c2-ef6f-43d3-9140-20470de5b7cb-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3190,"output_tokens":171,"cache_read_input_tokens":21376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"f02b9533-96f9-4ab1-a64a-38bc23426846"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-5d2de3c2-ef6f-43d3-9140-20470de5b7cb-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Use Three.js R180, WEBGPU, use a stylized cel-shaded / ink filter, rapier 0.19.3 for physics, Web Audio, Gamepad API; The rest of the tech stack is yours to choose.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 23:13:17\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html fi