102,114,111,109,40,123,32,108,101,110,103,116,104,58,32,110,32,125,44,32,40,95,44,32,115,108,111,116,41,32,61,62,32,40,123,32,107,101,121,58,32,96,112,36,123,115,108,111,116,125,96,44,32,115,108,111,116,44,32,110,97,109,101,58,32,78,65,77,69,83,91,115,108,111,116,93,32,125,41,41,59,10,50,48,56,58,32,32,102,111,114,32,40,99,111,110,115,116,32,112,32,111,102,32,91,46,46,46,112,108,97,121,101,114,115,44,32,46,46,46,40,115,101,114,118,101,114,77,111,100,101,32,63,32,91,123,32,107,101,121,58,32,39,115,101,114,118,101,114,39,44,32,115,108,111,116,58,32,110,117,108,108,44,32,110,97,109,101,58,32,39,83,101,114,118,101,114,39,44,32,115,101,114,118,101,114,58,32,116,114,117,101,32,125,93,32,58,32,91,93,41,93,41,32,123,10,52,49,49,58,32,32,99,111,110,115,116,32,114,101,112,111,114,116,32,61,32,123,32,100,111,110,101,58,32,102,97,108,115,101,44,32,112,97,115,115,58,32,102,97,108,115,101,44,32,103,97,109,101,58,32,36,40,39,103,97,109,101,39,41,46,118,97,108,117,101,44,32,112,108,97,121,101,114,115,58,32,78,117,109,98,101,114,40,36,40,39,112,108,97,121,101,114,115,39,41,46,118,97,108,117,101,41,44,32,115,99,101,110,97,114,105,111,44,32,99,104,101,99,107,115,44,32,108,111,103,58,32,108,111,103,76,105,110,101,115,32,125,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":14,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/tools/hub/harness.html\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":11,\"content\":\"  html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); font: 14px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif; }\"},{\"line_number\":15,\"content\":\"  header label { color: var(--dim); display: inline-flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center; }\"},{\"line_number\":16,\"content\":\"  input, select, button { font: inherit; color: var(--ink); background: #241f31; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; padding: 4px 8px; }\"},{\"line_number\":20,\"content\":\"  button:hover { border-color: var(--dim); }\"},{\"line_number\":26,\"content\":\"  .cell__state { margin-left: auto; color: var(--dim); }\"},{\"line_number\":31,\"content\":\"  aside h2 { font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--dim); margin: 0 0 6px; }\"},{\"line_number\":34,\"content\":\"  .ok::before { content: '✓ '; color: var(--ok); }\"},{\"line_number\":35,\"content\":\"  .bad::before { content: '✗ '; color: var(--bad); }\"},{\"line_number\":36,\"content\":\"  .wait::before { content: '… '; color: var(--warn); }\"},{\"line_number\":39,\"content\":\"  .hint { color: var(--dim); }\"},{\"line_number\":103,\"content\":\"      players: meta('hub:session-players'),\"},{\"line_number\":173,\"content\":\"  const players = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, slot) => ({ key: `p${slot}`, slot, name: NAMES[slot] }));\"},{\"line_number\":208,\"content\":\"  for (const p of [...players, ...(serverMode ? [{ key: 'server', slot: null, name: 'Server', server: true }] : [])]) {\"},{\"line_number\":411,\"content\":\"  const report = { done: false, pass: false, game: $('game').value, players: Number($('players').value), scenario, checks, log: logLines };\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"c0190043-ecc5-4bbb-82b2-49642f2d6af1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Players have an id, name, and color.\n\nCard ready is an array of player ids. The simulation will be built as a cohesive architecture with tests importing from `src/sim.js` or individual modules without starting the renderer. Balance steps, events, programme, and scoring will live in renderer-independent modules. Art and render are kept separate.\n\nEvents will optionally build visuals through a `visuals` callback or a `headless` flag. Physics modules will import only rapier to avoid slow Three.js initialization in tests. Visual meshes can be built separately from physics bodies. I need to verify whether Three.js object creation works in Node without WebGPU.\n\nThree.js imports cleanly in Node, so mesh creation is viable despite the import cost. A compact event system will use a shared `StandingEvent` helper that instantiates worlds, ground, and dolls per lane, steps the balancer, handles falls and ragdolls, and disposes physics bodies. Individual events will extend this base class.\n\nI will keep each event in its own file as the plan specifies. The full simulation is estimated at roughly twelve thousand lines across physics, events, data, UI, audio, rendering, the game director, and tests. I can write this systematically.\n\nI will write the balance core and a node test first, then build events on top. I am starting with config.js and tuning.js data.\n\nThe runtime tuning module will expose live getter and setter functions backed by a shared values object. Sliders will display degrees, which the balance step converts to radians on demand. I have started drafting balancer.js with a degree-to-radian constant.\n\nThe balancer module now defines the physics constants: reduced gravity, moment of inertia, and damping. It also implements `readLean` to extract lean angles from a body's quaternion rotation. The main `balanceStep` function is being drafted next.\n\nThe balance step now implements the core physics loop. It blends desired lean targets into current ones using a reflex rate, computes torque from gravity and spring-damping terms against the body's actual lean angles, and begins applying ankle limits based on difficulty.\n\nThe balance step now caps torque at the ankle limit and applies sway and disturbance scaling. Difficulty multipliers for ankleMax and disturbances will be applied in the event layer when computing nx, nz, and passing ankle values.\n\nThe balance step will multiply the base ankleMax by a difficulty multiplier. Difficulty is a static setting, not a slider, so the dev slider controls only the base value and avoids conflicts with the automatic difficulty scaling.\n\nA `swayTorque` helper is being drafted to generate disturbance torques from combined sine waves. It blends a sway term with a tremor term across two frequencies for each axis.\n\nThe `swayTorque` helper is now complete. A physics collision-group system is being defined with bit masks for ground, ragdoll, and per-lane dolls and props. Each group uses a dedicated filter function so dolls collide only with their own props while ignoring other lanes.\n\nThe `createDoll` helper is being drafted. It creates a kinematic foot and a dynamic body with four solver iterations, then attaches a capsule collider using the lane-specific collision group.\n\nThe `createDoll` helper now attaches a spherical impulse joint between the kinematic foot and the dynamic body. Contacts are disabled on that joint. The capsule geometry is verified to match the planned dimensions.\n\nThe ground is a flat cuboid at y=0. The doll colliders are excluded from ground contacts by their collision group filters. A fall trigger is being drafted: after the recover grace period expires, tilt above the fall angle initiates a fall.\n\nA simplified ten-body ragdoll with joints will be used. On recovery, the ragdoll bodies will be removed and the core body restored, with pose and lean cache reset. Body handles will be collected, removed, and nullified immediately to avoid dangling references. The correct Rapier 0.19 API for removing joints before bodies is being verified.\n\nJoints must be removed before bodies in Rapier 0.19. On fall, the core body will be disabled rather than destroyed, and re-enabled on recovery with its transform reset. Ragdoll parts only collide with ground, so they will not interfere with the disabled doll.\n\nFruit will be kinematic during flight and dynamic after landing. The 1.5-second parabola starts 7 meters out at y=1.75 and arcs back to the landing spot at the same height. After release it becomes dynamic and falls into the basket from 0.25 meters above the rim.\n\nLanding spots are randomly chosen within a radius around the doll's rest position. The shadow follows that spot, and the player leans to move the basket there.\n\nThe bot tracks any active fruit with less than 0.95 seconds left before landing. It targets the closest such fruit and computes a wish vector from the landing offset divided by reach, clamped to a unit disc. Reach is roughly 0.463 based on maximum lean and basket height.\n\nWhen the hat count reaches the bank threshold, the bot presses hop. This removes the fruit bodies and colliders, adds their value to the banked score, and starts a one-second banking timer.\n\nFruit caught in the basket will be removed from the world and replaced by a mass collider attached to the doll body at the local position. This makes the doll top-heavy through real rigid-body mass, matching the props-push-back contact behavior.\n\nThe catch volume is a 0.32-meter horizontal radius and a 0.30-meter vertical range around the basket center. When a fruit lands inside this zone, its body is removed from the world and replaced by a mass collider on the doll body at the local offset plus a small stack height.\n\nThe caught fruit collider will use the doll's collision groups so it does not hit the ground. Incoming flying fruit can still contact it, allowing them to stack.\n\nResting fruit will rely on natural contact forces to push the doll, avoiding special attachment code. The risk is test flakiness from bouncing or jitter.\n\nThe test for extra carried mass will add a collider directly to the doll body rather than relying on fruit settling in the basket. This avoids flakiness from bouncing or jitter.\n\nFruit types are now defined with specific masses and values. Apples weigh 0.015 and score 1 point. Urchins weigh 0.02, score -1, and apply a knock impulse. Boots weigh 0.03 and score 1. Pineapples weigh 0.028 and score 5. The tier-1 baseline mass is 0.015.\n\nBank multiplier is `bank value * (1 + 0.1 * (n - 1))`.\n\nOn fall or hop, attached fruit colliders are removed from the doll body without destroying it. Falling fruit are discarded; banked fruit are added to the score.\n\nSpecial fruit spawn less frequently than normal ones. The bot will never bank, so the hat accumulates until the doll tips and spills. A quick torque estimate shows roughly fifteen 0.015 kg fruits stacked at a height of 1.5 meters would destabilize the doll even without lean; over sixty seconds the bot can accumulate about thirty-seven pieces, guaranteeing a fall. Banking at seven keeps the load safely below that limit.\n\nThe bot will avoid whip-like stick movements. It will only lean when a fruit is within its last 0.95 seconds of flight and will not chase early fruit. If multiple fruits are in the air, it targets the one landing soonest. Missed fruit that hit the ground below 0.2 meters will be removed.\n\nA crate will be placed at the feet purely for visual effect.\n\nFor pose scoring, each tick inside the ring awards `0.30 / ringRadius`. Form is the share of the maximum possible score, capped at 100.\n\nThe ring radius shrinks during the hold, so the per-tick score `0.30 / ringRadius` changes each frame. Form is the share of the maximum possible earnings if the pose were always inside the ring.\n\nForm equals 100 times the sum of time-in-ring divided by radius, over the sum of inverse radii. A parked stick on the target will drift out of the shrinking ring, so form should stay below 85. The plan measured 75–81, and the formula should work once sway is verified. The pose flow begins with three tier-1 poses.\n\nThe pose sequence glides the ring from center to a spot over three seconds, then holds for eight seconds while the ring shrinks from 0.30 to 0.16 and sway increases from 0.08 to 0.22. The bot follows the ring with delayed correction using a wish vector that blends goal position, lean error, and noise; gain is set to 1.4 for sharpness. Check-in tests will reuse the same balancer in a special mode.\n\nThe Stand Still check-in lasts ten seconds with sway 0.1 and tremor 0.06. Score is one minus mean distance from the target divided by 0.25.\n\nLean Match runs for eighteen seconds across three six-second targets. The first 1.5 seconds are ignored. Score is one minus mean error divided by 0.4. Error is the absolute difference between actual lean and the target lean fraction.\n\nStep Time alternates feet at ninety-six beats per minute. Score is one minus mean timing error divided by 120 milliseconds.\n\nQuick Lean prompts six quadrant leans. Score is one minus mean reaction time divided by 0.9.\n\nOne-Foot Hold uses R2 and L2 to halve the margin. It scores like Stand Still.\n\nWobble age is computed from mean form and day index with handicaps of 24, 16, 8, 3, and 0. Age ranges from 18 to 99.\n\nMarble payout logic is being drafted.\n\nMarble payout is now defined: base points scale with minutes, bonus depends on the form medal, and a two-point first-clear bonus applies. Check-in tests award one marble each.\n\nTilt is computed as nine degrees per uncompleted step, so it reaches zero after all steps finish.\n\nThe programme total equals the count of classes across days one through five.\n\nMedal thresholds are now defined: suspicious at 97, exemplary at 85, pleasant at 65, adequate at 40, and attended below that.\n\nTier unlock requires tier-one form to reach 65 and tier-two form to reach 85. The system returns the highest tier still unlocked.\n\nThe input system will accept pad, keys, and mouse. The last device to register wins for weight.\n\nThe input state now includes wish direction, foot pressure, action buttons, look and zoom, device source, pointer offset, keys held, and latched presses. Screen-relative axes are defined with up away from the camera and right as screen right; a yaw rotation will be applied before use.\n\nThe screen-relative wish axes are mapped to world space. With the camera behind the doll looking toward -z, \"up away from the camera\" corresponds to world -z. Gamepad stick up is axis1 negative, so wishZ equals axis1.\n\nThe yaw rotation formula is now defined. Screen right maps to world +x and screen up maps to world -z when the camera sits at +z looking toward -z. The bot's wish vector will be computed as `stickX * cos(yaw) - stickUp * sin(yaw)` for horizontal and `stickX * sin(yaw) + stickUp * cos(yaw)` for vertical, where stickUp is the negative of the gamepad axis1. A simple test case will verify this mapping.\n\nBalance events will map screen x to leanX and screen y to leanZ, with up on the disc representing away from the camera. The ring target is the wish vector. Keyboard controls move this ring at 1.6 units per second; holding A shifts it left, and releasing drops it; C recentres it.\n\nKey presses are latched between polls so a brief press still registers. Mouse buttons map to bits 1, 2, and 4. Foot pressure ramps from zero to one over 0.12 seconds. Plate spinners alternate A and D for half-turns.\n\nPointer weight is normalized by 33% of screen height and clamped to the unit disc. The active device is determined by last input: pad ignores pointer, keys win while held, otherwise pointer once it moves; resting mouse does not fight a resting stick. Hover focus triggers only on pointer-move events with changed coordinates. Prompts will display keycaps or mouse buttons based on whether keyboard or mouse was last used.\n\nA glyph set setting will toggle between PlayStation and Xbox controllers. The gamepad poll maps standard axes and buttons, with L2 and R2 treated as analog triggers. Create plus Options is handled by ignoring Options while Create is held. Haptics are guarded, and pad input resumes audio. The bot skill configuration begins with a sharp preset using delay 0.16, gain 1.4, and reaction time 0.12.\n\nThe bot skill presets now include average and clumsy variants with their own delay, gain, noise, and reaction parameters. The `correct` function blends the goal position with the current dot position using the active skill's gain, adds a sine-wave noise term, and clamps the result to the unit disc. The bot writes its computed wish vector directly into the pad stub axes.\n\nThe bot writes its computed wish vector into the pad stub axes. For node tests, the real input path will use these stubs directly.\n\nA parked bot mode will set the stick to a fixed target and leave it unchanged. The test can supply the pose target directly and bypass the correcting bot entirely; sway alone will move the doll, so the score stays below 85.\n\nThe game director now owns screen state, profile, day, event instance, UI model, audio, simulation time, and scheduler. A `createGame` factory function is being drafted.\n\nThe `createGame` factory returns the director with screen, profile, day, event, tuning, settings, input, audio, and render modules, plus `advance`, `state`, `press`, and `bot` methods. The page entry point in main.js creates the game and runs the animation loop unless manual mode is active. The `advance` method batches fixed simulation steps and then performs a single frame update so UI timers advance correctly.\n\nThe director will call `frame(dt)` after every fixed step to keep simulation time consistent. Tests advance one step per frame for determinism, while the page accumulator runs multiple steps per frame. Both paths share the same `fixed()` and `frame()` functions.\n\nAn idle attract sequence triggers after ten seconds of no input on the title or menu screen. Input during that window sets a swallow flag to interrupt the attract.\n\nTest hooks will be exposed on `window.__TEST__` when the query string contains `?test=1`. A new `renderer.js` module will initialize a WebGPU backend, watch for device loss, post ink, and resize to the canvas drawing buffer. The renderer is skipped entirely when `test=1&render=0` is active. The title scene will use a simple painted island, sea, sky, and lighthouse with wooden-block title letters plus DOM text. The outline pass produces dark `#1A1420` pixels.\n\nThe UI CSS sets the viewport background to #1A1420 with no overflow. The #ui container is fixed at 5% inset and disables pointer events on itself while restoring them for buttons and choices. Focus styling uses a dark outline and a golden box-shadow. The test requires all content to stay inside the viewport or within the 5% margin, so focus rings must not extend outside that boundary.\n\nFocus styling will use a border instead of an outline so it cannot extend outside the 5% margin. Every visible element must remain within that boundary, including the HUD disc. A touch-only gate will display a full-screen message inside the margin with a Settings button and hide other controls when only touch input is detected.\n\nA `createAudio` factory will manage a Web Audio context with music, SFX, and voice gain levels. Offline test rendering will use an `OfflineAudioContext` to generate audio cues without a browser.\n\nThe browser audio test will call `__TEST__.renderCue('track', 'title')` to generate a buffer via the same instrument engine. Node lacks OfflineAudioContext, so audio tests must run in Playwright. Music gain is 0.10 and must stay unclipped with finite peaks and RMS above floor. Voice synthesis uses blips from text at roughly -6 dB per voice.\n\nDetune will be `tiltDeg * 1.6 * sin(t * 0.7)`, tested directly and through the engine. A dialogue generator in dialogue.js will build unique sentences from template fragments, cycling through them without repeats to satisfy the 200/60/60/60 line targets.\n\nThe dialogue will use real written arrays rather than generated lines. About 200 short Trudy lines will be stored in dialogue.js, keeping the file compact at roughly 12 KB. A name generator is also being drafted from adjective and noun lists.\n\nA `spinName` helper will combine a random adjective and noun from the lists. Save loading reads localStorage with a version check and falls back to memory if storage is unavailable. The About screen is built from `exit-interview.json` at build time into `src/data/about.js`; development uses a draft file.\n\nA Node test suite in `test/sim.test.mjs` will exercise the shipped modules. It covers balance settle, fall recover, save load, flail, fruit hat bank versus never bank, tier-one sharp bot completion, check-in age marbles tilt, and a full playthrough.\n\nA `tools/test.mjs` script will run Node tests and Playwright tests together. It covers input verbs, the ten-second attract swallow, dev sliders, exit interview shape, HTML build checks, and source greps. The full group includes hub, UI, and audio tests that require browsers. Launch verification is a separate step but should also be included.\n\nLaunch screenshots are a separate verification step that must be captured and saved to scratch. The test command will print only failures and a one-line count, suppressing all success messages. To keep the playthrough from running too long, physics will be optimized with eight solver iterations and fewer bodies.\n\nThe full playthrough will stay under three to four minutes. A sharp bot event is complete when it reaches a valid result with no NaN or throw, not necessarily a high score. The longest events alone require roughly eighty-four thousand steps, and the full playthrough repeats them.\n\nThe per-event sharp-bot test and the full playthrough will both run, since verification requires both. Each event will be tested from a fresh start as tier one, then repeated during the playthrough. I will benchmark an early doll step to confirm the combined workload stays fast. The bots will be implemented so they finish without physics panic and produce reasonable scores.\n\nBot falls are acceptable for most events, but the fruit hat sharp bot must not fall and the grand hold event cannot fail. The session configuration will compress each event into roughly fifty seconds of play. Because the harness only runs for about six seconds, the session code must send progress updates immediately rather than waiting for the event to finish. Progress will be reported every second starting from the moment the timeline reaches zero, skipping the negative countdown phase.\n\nGhost progress will be sent at ten hertz once the timeline reaches zero, even if the event world is not yet built. The implementation order is: PROGRESS.md, data files, core modules, physics, input and bots, events, game systems, then a Node test for balance and fruit hat.\n\nThe remaining implementation steps are: the rest of the events, rendering, art, UI, and audio; main entry and build; full tests; and launch plus hub. A director flow will drive the scripted playthrough by transitioning through a screens state machine on real game inputs. A `scriptedPlay` helper in the test file will feed those inputs directly into the live game instance.\n\nThe playthrough test will enable the sharp bot via `game.bot('sharp')` and feed inputs directly into the live game instance. A `press` helper will inject actions like confirm to advance through menus and events. This avoids building a separate story script or autopilot system.\n\nMenu defaults will be deterministic so the playthrough can advance automatically. The \"Who's here\" screen will default to the first mat, accept the default name and paint, then drop onto the island. The tutorial will wait for the bot to lean left, right, and forward before continuing.\n\nThe day flow will be a generator script that runs inside the live game. The bot performs tutorial leans and check-in tasks automatically. Check-in auto-starts, then the bot completes tests, reveals age, confirms the plan, plays the event, confirms results, opens the jar, and advances to the next event or bed. The test can simply advance time when the script times out.\n\nThe day script will auto-advance dialogue lines after their natural duration in all modes. A `?test=1` flag will shorten test dialogue waits to 0.05 seconds, but events must still run their full length. This keeps tests from skipping the thing under test while speeding up the playthrough.\n\nThe test will drive menus through `director.confirm()` so it follows the exact same path as real UI buttons. Every physics step will check body positions for NaN and record any errors, with tests asserting that none occur.\n\nA dispose leak test will verify that the physics world fully reclaims its bodies. It will create an event, record the body count, call dispose, and confirm the count returns to baseline when the world is rebuilt. It will also assert that a second dispose is safe and that `event.world` becomes null after cleanup.\n\nA physics debug state will be exposed for tests to inspect. I am now writing the core files and balance test, then running them.\n\nA compact generator of unique authored dialogue lines will be written directly into the source files. PROGRESS.md will be created and the plan checklist updated once the title folder exists. The project will use ES modules in package.json. Source code must not contain stubs, TODOs, FIXMEs, or placeholders. Terms like \"wii\", \"mii\", and \"balance board\" are forbidden outside about.js and the title screen. Browser APIs such as alert, confirm, prompt, window.open, WebSocket, and RTCPeerConnection are banned. Audio formats including .mp3, .wav, and .ogg are also excluded.\n\nAll randomness will come from a custom `rng.js` module using mulberry32, with no `Math.random` calls anywhere. TSL compute nodes are forbidden. I am checking the Rapier API for solver iterations, contact toggling, debug rendering, and version info, and may write a quick spike if the first test fails. The capsule collider constructor in rapier3d-compat 0.19 is `ColliderDesc.capsule(halfHeight, radius)`.\n\nThe spherical joint will anchor at the origin and 0.75 units below. Contacts on the impulse joint will be disabled, and torques will be reset and applied with wake enabled. Kinematic bodies will use `setNextKinematicTranslation` for foot movement. The physics world will run a fixed 1/120 second step with eight solver iterations.\n\nDefault gravity is set to negative 9.81 on the Y axis. Angular velocity and rotation are assumed to return objects with x, y, z, and w components. The additional solver iterations setting will be verified against the Rapier 0.19 API and removed if unsupported. I am now writing the core files directly with the Write tool.\n\nEvent implementations will be split into separate files. The visual doll will use a jointed mesh with the lean rotation applied at the root so the feet stay fixed on the foot body and the whole doll does not tip as a rigid toy.\n\nBalance events will use a rigid visual doll whose whole body copies the physics transform, while ragdoll events switch to per-part bodies. The flail animation will run for 0.35 seconds with procedural shoulder rotation and ankle flailBoost. I am batching the source file writes now.\n\nThe scratch directory is `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/tmp/grok-goal-cfc5772a1498/implementer`, and test logs will be written there. An `issues.md` tally will live in the notes folder. HTML inputs must not use placeholder attributes, and comments must not contain the word \"stub\". A quick node script will verify one remaining Rapier detail before the first test runs.\n\nThe HTML build inlines the font, so Playwright tests of the built file will have it available. Node tests import source directly, while HTML tests verify the bundle. Both test paths are needed. A test mode query parser will be added to main.js.\n\nA monolithic `src/game/sim.js` will export the simulation API for both main and tests to avoid circular imports. The director will optionally accept a renderer instance, and the renderer will be imported statically since its creation is lazy and defers DOM access until called.\n\nThe renderer module will import Three.js and WebGPU statically, but only instantiate the WebGPURenderer when rendering is enabled. This prevents Node tests from crashing on construction. Sky and sea will be simple meshes. The island geometry will include a disc base, rock, lighthouse, pavilions, trees, and the jar.\n\nTitle letters will be boxes arranged in the DOM. Comic text will be canvas sprites. The plan checklist will be updated as files are written. Dialogue strings will be fully explicit and generated from sentence lists inside the same file. Multiple files can be written in parallel.\n\nMedal names will display as \"Exemplary\", \"Pleasant\", \"Adequate\", \"Attended\", and \"Suspicious\". Each event in the data will carry a \"can\" line for success and a \"cant\" line for failure. Step class charts are also being planned.\n\nPlate spinners will derive hoop angular velocity from the weight vector's angular velocity. The balance core will remain in the doll but falling will be disabled. Plates will be simulated as energy numbers with visual meshes, and shards will spawn on crash as independent rapier bodies removed after six seconds. A chart builder will encode the step patterns.\n\nPlate spinners will use a smoothed inertia value that eases toward the hoop angle. Energy gains when inertia falls inside a band and decays otherwise. Each A or D press adds π radians, so tapping at 1.35, 2.3, and 3.2 presses per second lands in the L, M, and S bands respectively. The log-rep offset will grow by 0.05 meters per second for every degree of sideways lean past six degrees.\n\nThe log-rep offset formula is finalized: 0.05 meters per second times the sideways lean past six degrees, applied for the full step duration. The ribbon event will cycle target depth through a five-second pattern—down two seconds, pause half a second, up two seconds, rest half a second—and score by time spent in each band multiplied by a zone bonus. Room service will use foot steps, telegraphed gusts, and cylindrical glasses; the brace input will push against the wind at twenty percent of its strength, with the green zone indicating the correct lean.\n\nThe springboard will use a deterministic analytic spring followed by a ragdoll entry. The tippy raft will feature walkers on a tilting disc with swells and shrink, ending within ninety seconds; solo and group modes are both possible. The island loop will have a walker pace on a circle radius nineteen with alternating feet and shells, restricted to solo play. The grand hold will run four phases plus a final hold that cannot fail.\n\nThe grand hold will restart on fall and remove stones, step, then remove the plate. Tug will be a rope number best-of-three winnable on tier one. Still water will use a calm meter with sneezes over sixty seconds and creatures at thresholds. Plank will have gull pushes in a zone where flail shakes them off. All events will use real rules with simple primitive visuals. The physics step order is input read, wish update, disturbance compute, balance torque, then world.step().\n\nThe fall check will run after `world.step()` so it uses the newly integrated lean. When the tilt exceeds the threshold, the body is disabled immediately afterward. This skips `balanceStep` on the next step and lets the ragdoll take over.\n\nTo prevent an instant fall bug after re-enabling the body, the rotation will be reset to identity and lane lean values zeroed before the next step. Angular velocity will also be zeroed so the damper does not misbehave. A recovery grace period of 0.4 seconds will block immediate falls during the snap. The test will push past forty-five degrees into the ragdoll, wait for recovery time, then assert the body stays upright for one second while the wish is zero.\n\nThe test will apply a large torque to trigger the shipped fall detection rather than teleporting the body into a fallen state. A sustained eight Newton-metre horizontal torque for one second should exceed the forty-five degree threshold even when braced. After recovery, the test will hold zero wish and zero torque for two seconds and assert that the body remains upright and the fall count stays at one. The flail input will be implemented as a timed save with a thirty-five millisecond animation window and a two-second cooldown.\n\nThe flail test will use three cases: flailing at the start of a 3.5-second gust, flailing when lean exceeds 30 degrees, and a double press to confirm cooldown blocks extension. If physics numbers do not match the plan, the cause will be debugged.\n\nThe default Standard difficulty will set ankle and disturb parameters to 1.0. A `createStandingWorld()` export will let tests reuse the same `createDoll` and `balanceStep` logic as events, including the fruit hat test. The game module will also export a `runBalance` helper for events, but tests must not reimplement it.\n\nA `rapierReady()` helper will initialize the Rapier WASM module once per process. The physics exports are split into `world.js` for `createWorld` and `createDoll`, and `balancer.js` for `balanceStep` and `readLean`. Tests will drive the shipped balance loop directly.\n\nThe UI will be written in a single file `src/ui/screens.js` with CSS. Art assets will live in `src/art/cast.js` and `src/art/island.js`. Audio will be handled by `src/audio/engine.js`. The core data files are `config.js`, `tuning.js`, `poses.js`, `charts.js`, `dialogue.js`, `programme.js`, `names.js`, `unlocks.js`, `palettes.js`, `about.js`, `events.js`, plus `rng.js` and `save.js`.\n\nThe full file tree is now mapped. It covers physics modules, input and bot logic, event handlers, game director and session, rendering, art, audio, UI, main entry, and build or test tools. Some small files will be merged to keep the structure lean.\n\nAnimation clips will live in `anim/clips.js` to support the AnimationMixer requirement. The Fredoka license will appear in the about credits. Physics modules and tests will be written first so the core is solid before building events. Rapier 0.19 collider methods are being verified: collision groups use a 32-bit integer, and mass can be set via `setMass` or `setDensity`.\n\nThe foot will be a kinematic body with a spherical joint anchoring the dynamic body above it. Gravity will pull the body downward while the joint constrains its position. The controller applies torque to keep the body upright. A first test will verify that zero wish lets the body settle and stay upright, while full wish drives it to the maximum lean angle.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1fe3222b-e5e0-404e-baa5-36c2db1f1225-26","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"setAdditionalSolverIterations|resetTorques|setContactsEnabled|numSolverIterations","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/node_modules/@dimforge/rapier3d-compat","glob":"*.d.ts","head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1fe3222b-e5e0-404e-baa5-36c2db1f1225-27","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"capsule\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/node_modules/@dimforge/rapier3d-compat/rapier.d.ts","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2592,"output_tokens":15062,"cache_read_input_tokens":107136,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"31deb227-fed7-4e7e-b38a-0a172e683d7c"}
