rk/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/node_modules/@dimforge/rapier3d-compat/geometry/collider.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":274,\"content\":\"    setMass(mass: number): void;\"},{\"line_number\":587,\"content\":\"    static capsule(halfHeight: number, radius: number): ColliderDesc;\"},{\"line_number\":781,\"content\":\"    setMass(mass: number): ColliderDesc;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"e6c8333b-8a48-4fa7-9f92-07ffcb15e826"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The API matches the plan.\n\nI'll write a comprehensive physics module first and test it immediately with a node script. Then I'll build the director as a clean state machine and design an HTML UI that passes the 30px and 6-choices tests.\n\nEvery screen must be reachable via goto() so the test can walk through each one. The play screen needs HUD text and disc labels at least 30px, with prompts along the bottom. Trudy's peek can stay visual-only.\n\nThe play screen HUD must contain at most six focusable items. Pause is separate. Page switchers on journal pages will use `data-pager` so they don't count toward the limit.\n\nSettings pages will keep their sliders under six plus pagers. Focusable elements will use a thick `.is-focus` border for visible focus.\n\nThe test checks for a 3px or thicker outline or border via `getComputedStyle`. I'll enforce this with CSS rules setting `#ui` text to 36px, headings to 84px, and titles to 96px. About.js will split long answers across pages as the plan requires. Exit interview content will be drafted near the end once issue counts are known.\n\nA chicken-and-egg problem exists: the full test suite checks exit-interview.json and the about screen, but the about screen is generated from that same file. I will write a draft exit interview first, build, run tests, update the counts, rebuild, and retest.\n\nI will seed issues.md empty and append bug counts as tests catch them. The attract mode test requires both a ten-second simulation advance and one wall-clock run of ten real seconds; the simulation advance is the primary check.\n\nThe attract-mode test will verify that pressing confirm while attract is active returns to the title screen without starting the game. The dev-slider test will confirm that tuning a parameter on a running event immediately changes its behavior, then resets it to the original value.\n\nBalance tuning is read live during each step. The touch-copy test will run in Playwright with an iPhone profile, plus a unit-level check that the detection function and string exist in both source and DOM template. Offline audio will be tested inside Playwright to keep the full suite fast, and the launch test requires two separate browser launches.\n\nI will run the launch test after the build succeeds, using one browser at a time to avoid memory exhaustion. The canvas drawing size will match the viewport multiplied by devicePixelRatio, and I will record that ratio explicitly.\n\nI will expose the renderer's view state so tests can verify CSS and drawing dimensions. Because WebGPU canvas readback is often blank or blocked, I will rely on page screenshots to satisfy the pixel-readback requirement.\n\nThe launch script will capture a screenshot via Playwright and decode the PNG buffer to analyze pixels. Python Pillow is available through uv for image processing.\n\nThe launch script will open Chromium at 1280×720, wait briefly for software WebGPU to settle, then read the renderer state and capture a screenshot. It will verify the title text, canvas presence, and absence of errors.\n\nThe launch script will verify the title string is present in the DOM even without test mode. It will also check for a live WebGPU device, since a lost device fails the verification.\n\nA global `window.__RENDER__` object will expose backend, lost-device status, and frame count at all times. The launch script will read this to verify the WebGPU device is live. The title string will be rendered in a DOM element with id `#title-name`.\n\nI will track the active screen via a `dataset.screen` attribute on the body element so tests can detect transitions without test mode. The hub test presses Enter and Space on the title screen, which must not throw errors and must leave something drawn.\n\nI will implement a `normCode` helper that converts single-letter key strings like `a` into `KeyA` so the game handles both normalized and raw event codes. I also need to verify that holding Create while pressing options buttons 8 and 9 does not open the pause menu.\n\nI will accept both `Space` and ` ` as valid key codes. The session card Enter listener is safe because the hub's answer function ignores inputs once the round starts, so it cannot collide with the game's hop action.\n\nI will keep Enter from triggering game actions on the session card before the round starts. The card's Ready and More buttons will accept taps, and the More button can also be triggered by the M key or Triangle. Ghost dolls will be purely visual: they update from messages with a 150ms interpolation, remove when the user leaves, display name tags via textContent, show seat colors on shirts, and play a flop clip if fallen. They will not affect physics because they are just THREE groups without Rapier bodies.\n\nThe schedule will pick three events from a fixed set of six. Each event runs for 62 seconds: six seconds of intro, fifty seconds of play, and six seconds of score. The director will jump to the slot that the current clock time indicates. Progress updates will fire once per second during the frame loop.\n\nThe director will use `AIHub.time()` to stay synchronized with the hub clock. Ghost messages will be batched and sent at 10 Hz. When time-up arrives, the result is sent immediately if it has not already been sent; the loop may continue until the end signal stops input and sound.\n\nA `locked` flag will stop input on time-up while keeping the renderer alive, and audio will halt on end. Score sums completed slots at ten points each plus partial progress; during the first six-second intro it stays zero. The progress label shows the upcoming event name with its score. Session deals must use `AIHub.rng('deal')` instead of `Math.random`.\n\nI realized `AIHub.rng(label)` returns a fresh generator on every call, so the `rng` helper must cache each generator per label to keep the sequence advancing correctly.\n\nA `resetRng(seed)` helper will clear all cached generators and reset the base seed so tests can reproduce exact sequences.\n\nA test will verify that resetting the RNG produces identical number sequences. The cosmetic stream will remain separate from gameplay logic. Island tilt physics will use spring and damping constants tuned to 2.2 and 1.8, with a gravity helper derived from the tilt angles.\n\nRest tilt is 9 degrees on day one toward a fixed direction. It decays as steps are completed. Skill form does not affect the decay formula. Tests will verify the pure function and the director after completing events with different forms.\n\nA CheckIn class will wrap the balance core for stand-still, lean-match, one-foot, and timing logic. Scores will return 0–1. Wobble age derives from the mean. A sharp bot will run check-in and assert age stays between 18 and 99. A pure function test will confirm that wobbleAge falls across days when the mean is unchanged. The director stores daily ages, so a constant-skill playthrough can verify those stored values decrease. Variability in physics might make that assertion flaky.\n\nThe check-in bot will be deterministic so scores stay stable. Handicap drops by 8–24 points per day, which is larger than the skill term’s variation, guaranteeing that age always falls when skill is unchanged.\n\nThe sharp bot will use zero noise and a fixed RNG seed so check-in scores are perfectly reproducible. If the playthrough still fails to show strictly falling ages due to physics noise, the test will fall back to asserting the pure function contract and that every recorded age stays within 18–99.\n\nI will not fake check-in ages; the simulation must be genuinely deterministic. Playthrough length should stay within 30–60 seconds. I will add a `godMode` tuning flag that disables balance falls during attract mode and development.\n\nThe shipped fall check respects the `godMode` flag. Tests for falls will not set it.\n\nAttract vignettes will run loop events with bot lanes, each about 18 seconds long. A title overlay reading \"DEMO · Press any button\" appears after a pass, stays for five seconds, then repeats.\n\nThe Fruit Hat vignette will use god mode off so the greedy bot can actually topple. This resolves a contradiction in the plan between god mode on and a falling bot.\n\nGroup Class will run a lobby with four mats, three pads joining each lane, and Tippy Raft racing to a winner. The test will simulate four pads, start the race, and assert the winning pad is 0–3. The full simulation should take about ninety seconds of CPU time, which is acceptable.\n\nI am starting the implementation now and will update PROGRESS.md and the plan checklist. I am also working out the file paths: tests live in a dev subfolder, the HTML stays in the game root, and tools/test.mjs runs from there while checking the parent directory.\n\nThe test HTML filename will embed the \"Wii Fit\" string. The source name will live in `about.js` inside `src`, and the build script will read it from a constant to generate the final output path.\n\nThe config.js TITLE is \"Level Best\" without the Wii Fit string. Dev mode will expose a large set of tuning controls across pages of six, including event and day jumps, god mode, time scale, step, camera, physics debug, stats, input monitor, force generators, dialogue playback, age and marbles setters, unlock all, palette toggles, bot play, save reset, copy tuning, and default reset. These will be real controls that call director methods. The first settings page covers sound with music, sfx, and voice sliders, defaulting music to ten.\n\nThe remaining settings pages cover controls, display, and game. Controls include glyph style, rumble, auto-breathe, and rhythm offset. Display includes UI scale, ink strength, reduce shake, and fullscreen. Game includes difficulty and reset save.\n\nThe UI scale slider will be clamped so text never shrinks below 30 pixels. The CSS uses `max(30px, calc(36px * var(--ui-scale)))`.\n\nPausing the simulation stops the event timer from advancing. A test will verify that pausing and then advancing leaves the event time unchanged.\n\nHaptics will be implemented as a guarded call. The events system will use a single registry file that imports and exports all event classes, with each event file exporting its own class. A shared `StandingEvent` base class in `base.js` will handle common setup like lanes, tier, timer, and intro-to-play phase transitions.\n\nThe `StandingEvent` base class will manage physics world and dolls, stepping them in `fixed(dt)` while the director handles the intro UI separately. Each event test will create a game in manual mode to drive the full flow: intro card, play phase, and medal result.\n\nThe Fruit Hat test will advance the simulation by seventy steps to reach the results screen. A maximum time limit will be added so the test fails if the event does not end. If the sharp bot finishes early by whistling, the test will still proceed to results.\n\nThe countdown lasts three seconds of simulation time. The director will build the physics world at the start of the play phase rather than during the intro, keeping the intro fast. Disposing an event will free its world. The results medal is computed from the lane result. A marbles pour screen follows results in the programme, but free play skips it to continue.\n\nI will create an empty `notes/issues.md` and update PROGRESS.md and the plan checklist. The dialogue file will be written as short original lines to save tokens. I will generate files in batches, starting with physics, core, fruit hat, poses, and tests, then continue with the remaining modules.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d0793cce-274d-47d6-9fb3-9b0850fc33f8-30","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317\"\nnode --input-type=module << 'EOF'\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nfor (const n of ['AnimationMixer','AnimationClip','QuaternionKeyframeTrack','Group','Mesh','SphereGeometry','CapsuleGeometry','CylinderGeometry','ConeGeometry','TorusGeometry','LatheGeometry','TubeGeometry','CatmullRomCurve3','DataTexture','NearestFilter','PCFShadowMap','DirectionalLight','HemisphereLight','Color','CanvasTexture','Sprite','InstancedMesh','LineSegments','BufferGeometry','Float32BufferAttribute']) {\n  console.log(n, typeof THREE[n]);\n}\nimport { RoundedBoxGeometry } from 'three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js';\nconsole.log('RoundedBox', typeof RoundedBoxGeometry);\nEOF","description":"Check Three.js exports needed for the game"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1030,"output_tokens":6678,"cache_read_input_tokens":125696,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"f9e3f39e-cb65-4b3e-b11a-1b6d3ed9a378"}
