,112,84,105,108,116,44,32,103,114,97,118,105,116,121,70,114,111,109,84,105,108,116,32,125,32,102,114,111,109,32,39,46,46,47,112,104,121,115,105,99,115,47,105,115,108,97,110,100,46,106,115,39,59,10,56,54,58,32,32,32,32,103,114,97,118,105,116,121,70,114,111,109,84,105,108,116,40,116,104,105,115,46,116,105,108,116,46,116,120,44,32,116,104,105,115,46,116,105,108,116,46,116,122,44,32,103,101,116,84,117,110,101,40,39,103,114,97,118,105,116,121,39,41,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":3,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/physics/island.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":19,\"content\":\"export function gravityFromTilt(tx, tz, g = 9.81) {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/events/islandLoop.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":2,\"content\":\"import { createTilt, stepTilt, gravityFromTilt } from '../physics/island.js';\"},{\"line_number\":86,\"content\":\"    gravityFromTilt(this.tilt.tx, this.tilt.tz, getTune('gravity'));\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"767d5553-51a9-44ef-b831-de64744d8907"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_474","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Island Loop ignores gravity return.\n\nThe NaN likely stems from assigning a plain object to `world.gravity`. Rapier may require a special vector type or direct mutation. The gravity formula itself looks correct.\n\nThe docs confirm that assigning a plain object to `world.gravity` should work. The NaN may instead come from `getTune('gravity')` returning an unexpected value, or from the setter corrupting memory in this version. The deviation already left the call as a no-op. Uphill stride uses a kinematic sine approximation rather than real gravity. The next step is to determine whether this is a core defect.\n\nThe island loop is a counter, not a physical run. The doll's foot moves kinematically at a tiny scale, so the walker capsule barely shifts in world space. The balance core still steps, and the jog is foot alternation driving a distance counter. PLAN requires a real walker on the tilted disc.\n\nAcceptance criterion 3 demands that each PLAN.md §6 event plays its full ruleset through intro, play, and a medal result. The Island Loop rules specify jogging two laps, alternating feet, speed, stick steering, shells, real island tilting, and scoring by time plus shells. The current implementation only includes foot alternation.\n\nThe remaining mechanics are mostly present: stride speed, cadence band, steering, shells, tilt state updates, and scoring. The deviation explicitly attributes the NaN to the gravity assignment, so the missing physics is a confirmed bug rather than an incomplete design choice. Whether this alone refutes the event is still being weighed.\n\nThe goal plan explicitly forbids stubs or placeholders for §6 events. PLAN.md §18 allows short builds to cut whole events, but this computer can run the full demo, so every section is mandatory. The Island Loop is a playable ruleset that scores, yet gravity remains unwired and documented as a deviation. This raises the question of whether an incomplete physics system qualifies as a stub.\n\nThe implementer accepted the gravity deviation, but the skeptic must judge whether the criterion holds regardless. The jog is abstract: the balance doll's foot only jitters by 0.02 times its position, so it never physically walks the disc. If the renderer uses a tilt quaternion, the visual island may rotate and the stride may change with slope sine, yet the gameplay effect remains simulated rather than real.\n\nThe goal plan's acceptance criteria are a derived checklist that may clarify but never narrow or override OBJECTIVE. PLAN.md is the contract via OBJECTIVE. If it requires a real walker capsule with gravity tilt, faking that physics is a miss of OBJECTIVE.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80bb6622-c2f8-4ec7-a7f5-5cbc53204b3f-29","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"stepTilt|gravity|tilt","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/events","glob":"*.js"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80bb6622-c2f8-4ec7-a7f5-5cbc53204b3f-30","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"gravity","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/physics"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80bb6622-c2f8-4ec7-a7f5-5cbc53204b3f-31","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/physics/world.js","limit":120}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80bb6622-c2f8-4ec7-a7f5-5cbc53204b3f-32","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/notes/issues.md","limit":150}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"inp