{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_501","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Springboard auto-launches after an 8-second pump. It shows zero during air time until landing.\n\nThree 6-second dives take roughly 30 seconds of physics simulation. The 55.9 window test is the cheapest proof of the multi-start bug: jump the clock to 55.85 and advance one step so the event starts.\n\nAdvancing 0.09 seconds from 55.900 keeps the local clock at roughly 55.95 for all steps. The event starts once and scores, but before the fix it repeats for every step in that window. After the fix there is only one part. Continuing to 61.9 still produces a single part.\n\nThe storm test alone does not cover early-finish restarts. I will also run a full springboard event to completion. A wrapper can advance the clock in 0.5-second chunks while freezing the local time, letting the physics engine tick forward internally.\n\nThe springboard event finishes after roughly 30 seconds of physics, leaving only results afterward. That is about 3600 Rapier steps at 120 Hz, so the test should take a few seconds. Advancing the clock while freezing local time keeps the event running normally.\n\nThe test must pass through the scoring branch at 55.9 seconds. If the clock jumps directly from 55.5 to 56.0 without a step where local time is between 55.9 and 56, the event may never score because the results screen would run before the score is recorded.\n\nThe no-score risk is a test artifact of coarse clock jumps, not the game bug. Real time advances smoothly. The test should advance the clock by at least FIXED_DT each call so a step occurs inside the 55.9–56 window. I will update time() to advance by that fixed amount per call.\n\nThe wrapper will advance the clock by exactly one fixed step each time `time()` is called. A single `advance(50)` call therefore runs 6000 steps and moves the clock from 6 to 56 seconds in one go. The springboard event finishes after roughly 30 seconds of internal time, so it latches around local time 36.\n\nSpringboard is the chosen event because it finishes early and hits the 55.9 boundary cleanly. After t=62, advancing a few more steps will enter slot 1 and confirm that slot 0 contributed exactly one part.\n\nStarting the clock at 0 and advancing to 61.9 seconds yields only about 7,400 physics steps while an event is active. Springboard consumes roughly 30 seconds of that window, leaving the rest as cheap results-only frames after the latch. This avoids the slot-1 reset and keeps the test fast.\n\nThe implementation plan is to fix sessionFixed to latch one part per slot, expose session parts and total on state(), add the test in flow.mjs, run it, rebuild the HTML, and rerun the targeted test. The full suite will follow after the rebuild. The full suite is ninety seconds and serves as the gating log; test-full.log must be updated since behavior changed.\n\nThe rebuilt HTML must be relaunched and the full suite rerun so the adversarial verifier sees the updated file. The new bug should also be recorded in issues.md, which will require rebuilding the about text and running the full suite again.\n\nThe verifier caught this bug after the interview, so it belongs in issues.md as issue 23. It is critical because the hub score can exceed a legal class and the slot restarts worlds every frame, which risks physics panics or inflated results. Updating the interview will also change about.js, but the about screen already handles long lines with paging.\n\nThe about screen will list twenty-three issues, fourteen critical. The first sentence adds a clause about a hub slot that scored a finished class and then started it again. The second sentence updates the critical count to fourteen and includes the hub slot bug.\n\nThe scoring sequence will set the latched flag before disposing the event so a throw cannot double-score. The session latch also resets when the slot changes.\n\nThe sessionFixed structure now starts an event only when none is running and the slot is not yet latched. Once started, the event runs until it finishes or local time reaches 55.9 seconds, at which point it scores exactly once. The latch flag is set before disposal to prevent double scoring.\n\nThe test will assert that slot 0 contains exactly one part, the total is at most 1000, and the total equals that single part. It also checks that the screen is results at local time 61.9 seconds. The score cap of 1000 implies the form value is clamped between 0 and 100.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-855a4de2-c14f-4d92-bd02-db06dca90e49-118","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function setSessionMode|AIHub|setSessionMode","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-855a4de2-c14f-4d92-bd02-db06dca90e49-119","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work/Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/test/flow.mjs","offset":140,"limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2128,"output_tokens":3218,"cache_read_input_tokens":156032,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"a34c42ed-4907-49d8-8f57-5c9f32be03a3"}
