 ],\\n        \\\"total\\\": 47,\\n        \\\"et\\\": null,\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"springboard\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"sec\\\": 57.167,\\n        \\\"steps\\\": 20,\\n        \\\"screen\\\": \\\"results\\\",\\n        \\\"parts\\\": [\\n          47\\n        ],\\n        \\\"total\\\": 47,\\n        \\\"et\\\": null,\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"springboard\\\"\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"node /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/tmp/grok-goal-cfc5772a1498/skeptic-0/slot-fps.mjs\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Replay hub slots at 6fps and 60fps\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9%2Fwork/01a1ab04-cb70-7800-80e8-92245a08ec20/terminal/call-21549bcc-d603-412a-963f-bc9c51bb7284-86.log\",\"total_bytes\":3472}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b66","uuid":"ae97690d-97ec-43ed-a42a-fc042daaf0bb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_524","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"At 6fps, the plates/fruit/step output is notable.\n\nAt second 56, event.t reads 50 while parts is empty and screen is results. The clock jump to 56 should bypass the play branch entirely. The \"near\" log only appears after all 20 steps finish.\n\nThe frame at 56 is the first to hit the >=56 threshold. The event timestamp of 50 is unexpected because the play branch should not have advanced it.\n\nThe 55.833-second frame ran 20 steps while local time stayed below 55.9 and 56. The event timestamp advanced from 48.167 at the start of the 54-second frame to roughly 50.0 by the end of that frame. This explains why event.t reads 50 at second 56.\n\nThe event steps during the 55.833-second frame while local time remains below 55.9. It crosses t=50 and sets done=true mid-frame. The scoring condition checks for event.done or local >= 55.9, so it should trigger and score during that same frame.\n\nThe event should have latched during the same step that set done=true. The empty parts at second 56 suggests the latch failed or the done flag was not checked in time.\n\nRechecking the frame-by-frame timestamp accumulation confirms the event reaches 50.0 by second 56. The exact timing of the t=50 crossing is being reconsidered.\n\nThe 55.833-second frame ends with event.t at 49.833, so done is still false when the clock reaches 56. That should freeze the event entirely. Yet the log shows et: 50 at second 56. This means some steps during the 56-second frame must have seen local time below 56. The suspicion is an IEEE floating-point error where 336/6 is not exactly 56.\n\nIf the clock reads 55999.999 ms, local time becomes 55.999 and the play branch is entered. The scoring condition should trigger immediately. The empty parts list remains unexplained.\n\nThe 50-second timestamp at second 56 requires roughly 20 extra steps, so local time must have stayed below 56 for the entire frame. A floating-point error of about 0.001 ms would still leave local near 55.999 and trigger the scoring check immediately. The empty parts list therefore remains unexplained.\n\nThe log contradicts itself. The event timestamp is 50, so the event still exists. Yet the screen is results and parts is empty. That combination should only occur after the event is disposed. This inconsistency remains unresolved.\n\nThe slim filter likely dropped the 55.833-second sample, so the event timestamp of 50 may have persisted from that frame rather than being reached during the 56-second frame. The focus is now on the exact step order at local=55.833333 to determine why the done flag was not scored.\n\nThe exact step order at local=55.833 is being examined. The scoring condition checks `event.done` immediately after `stepEvent`, so the done flag should trigger scoring during that same frame. The empty parts list remains unexplained.\n\nThe scoring branch should trigger if `event.done` is true. The event's own logic sets `done` when `t >= 50`, which the timestamp confirms. The contradiction remains: either `stepEvent` never updates the flag, or the check is somehow bypassed.\n\nThe root cause is now clear. At 6fps, hub timestamps advance only 166ms per frame. The event timestamp can read 49.999 and display as 50.000, so the done flag stays false. When local time later jumps to 56 or higher, the play branch skips scoring entirely because done is still false. The event persists with an et of 50, the screen becomes results, and parts remain empty. This confirms the bug: the 166ms frame interval is too small for the event to reach its duration before the clock crosses the threshold.\n\nThe 6fps frame interval is too narrow to land inside the [55.9, 56) latch window, so the event never scores and parts remain empty. At 60fps, a frame lands on 55.9 and scores correctly; springboard ends early at 47. The prior fix only prevented double-scoring, but the close-of-slot logic still drops the score whenever the clock misses that narrow window.\n\nThe 6fps cadence zeroes three of the seven session events: Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, and Step Class.\n\nThe fix is to score the event immediately when local time reaches 56 or the slot changes, then dispose it. This removes dependency on the narrow 0.1-second window.\n\nThe existing test only steps the hub by 1/120 seconds, which always lands inside the 55.9 window and cannot catch this bug. The test should instead advance AIHub.time in roughly 1/6-second frames for a 50-second session event, asserting one part with total greater than zero and less than or equal to 1000.\n\nThis is a regression against the round-scheduling and per-slot scoring criteria. The prior test was insufficient because the 1/120-second step always landed inside the narrow window. The real requestAnimationFrame clock has an arbitrary phase offset, so roughly sixty percent of sessions hit the latch while forty percent miss it entirely.\n\nA single garbage-collection pause longer than 100 ms also misses the narrow window. The bug is structural: the play interval must commit the score on exit, regardless of probability. A full round of three zero-score events would submit a total of zero. Confidence is high.\n\nThe bug is located at director.js:841. The `else screen = 'results'` branch inside the `local < 56` condition never latches the event, so it persists with an empty parts list.\n\nThe bundled HTML retains the same logic, so the bug is present in the shipped page. Step Class has a duration of roughly 50.055 seconds, meaning its timestamp can read 50 before the done flag is set. This confirms the same latch failure affects every session event.\n\nThe 20 fixed steps at second 56 confirm the event is never stepped once local time reaches 56, leaving the timestamp frozen at 50. The prior fix is genuine: springboard scored once at 47. I will now write the verdict files.\n\nThe prior gap is fixed for early events. The remaining defect is the window miss for full-length events. This qualifies as a new objection because it is a demonstrable defect in shipped behavior, not a stylistic preference.\n\nThe prior simulation assumed a frozen clock during the 20-step frame. In reality, each physics step advances performance.now() by roughly 8 ms, so the 166 ms frame still moves the clock forward. The narrow latch window would therefore be hit during slow frames, making the prior explanation for the empty parts list incorrect. A fast JavaScript burst with a slow rAF timestamp is now the more likely cause of the missed window.\n\nThe simulation is correct. A typical JavaScript burst processes 20 steps in about 5 ms, so the clock stays nearly constant during the burst and the gap between frames is what matters. Rapier physics steps are fast, so 20 steps likely take only 10–40 ms rather than 166 ms.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eaf4107b-2de0-4ed1-bb37-b0cb19894ecb-87","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/tmp/grok-goal-cfc5772a1498/goal-verdict-cfc5772a1498-2-0.json","content":"{\n  \"refuted\": true,\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"bug\",\n      \"location\": \"Level Best-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231317/src/game/director.js:841\",\n      \"detail\": \"A slot still scores only while local is inside [55.9, 56) or the event already set done. The local>=56 branch only switches to results. At 6fps (hub time jumps ~1/6s and stays put across that frame's fixed steps) Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, and Step Class reach results with parts=[] and total 0. Score the live event once on any exit from the play window, including local>=56 and a slot change. Extend the sim-time test so AIHub.time() advances in ~1/6s frames, constant during the frame's steps, and slot 0 of a 50s class still has one part with 0<total<=1000.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": \"6fps frame replay of sessionFixed: plates/fruitHat/stepClass end slot 0 at total 0 (et~50, screen results, parts []); the same plates run at 60fps latches one part (35). Springboard still latches once (47) because it ends early. test-full.log 103 passed only steps the clock by 1/120, which always hits [55.9, 56).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"blocking\": \"none\",\n  \"details_md\": \"The double-score latch holds for a class that ends early. It still drops the score for a class that lasts the whole slot.\\n\\n`session.latched` in `sessionFixed` stops a second `startEvent` after a part is pushed. Springboard, which finishes well before 50s, keeps one part through the end of slot 0. That matches the new flow test and a 6fps replay (one part, 47).\\n\\nThe push itself is still gated by `event.done || local >= 55.9`, and that check sits only in the `local < 56` branch (`director.js` around 830 and 841). When `local >= 56` the code sets the results screen and does not read `event.result`. The window is 0.1s wide. This machine's software WebGPU path is about 6fps, so `AIHub.time()` jumps by ~1/6s between frames and stays put while that frame's fixed steps run. From a session start at 0, those stamps go 55.833s then 56.000s and never land in [55.9, 56).\\n\\nReplay of that loop, deal forced to one class: Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, and Step Class all finish slot 0 with `parts: []`, `total: 0`, screen `results`, event time still ~50. The same Plate Spinners run at 60fps latches a single part of 35, because a frame does fall on 55.9. A 50s class must not score 0 just because no frame sampled a 100ms band.\\n\\nCommit the part on every way out of the play window (`local >= 56`, or the slot index changing) when `event` is still live and `latched` is false. The existing 1/120 clock test cannot see this; drive `AIHub.time()` in ~1/6s frames, held constant across that frame's steps, and require one part with `0 < total <= 1000`.\"\n}\n"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eaf4107b-2de0-4ed1-bb37-b0cb19894ecb-88","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231317__x29b4d9/tmp/grok-goal-cfc5772a1498/goal-classifier-cfc5772a1498-2-skeptic-0.md","content":"The double-score latch holds for a class that ends early. It still drops the score for a class that lasts the whole slot.\n\n`session.latched` in `sessionFixed` stops a second `startEvent` after a part is pushed. Springboard, which finishes well before 50s, keeps one part through the end of slot 0. That matches the new flow test and a 6fps replay (one part, 47).\n\nThe push itself is still gated by `event.done || local >= 55.9`, and that check sits only in the `local < 56` branch (`director.js` around 830 and 841). When `local >= 56` the code sets the results screen and does not read `event.result`. The window is 0.1s wide. This machine's software WebGPU path is about 6fps, so `AIHub.time()` jumps by ~1/6s between frames and stays put while that frame's fixed steps run. From a session start at 0, those stamps go 55.833s then 56.000s and never land in [55.9, 56).\n\nReplay of that loop, deal forced to one class: Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, and Step Class all finish slot 0 with `parts: []`, `total: 0`, screen `results`, event time still ~50. The same Plate Spinners run at 60fps latches a single part of 35, because a frame does fall on 55.9. A 50s class must not score 0 just because no frame sampled a 100ms band.\n\nCommit the part on every way out of the play window (`local >= 56`, or the slot index changing) when `event` is still live and `latched` is false. The existing 1/120 clock test cannot see this; drive `AIHub.time()` in ~1/6s frames, held constant across that frame's steps, and require one part with `0 < total <= 1000`.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1346,"output_tokens":5603,"cache_read_input_tokens":172416,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa57-1af6-7b91-b0b8-da8c4d991b